      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0007" n="art0007">
        <head>Warrington.</head>
        <p>Warrington.</p>
        <p>SPEECHES BY MR. AND MRS. A. H. CROSFIELD.</p>
        <p>HOBE RULE AND WELSH DISI</p>
        <p>For the first time Warriogtrn was on Wednesday the venue of the members of the Lanca,hire and Cheshire Union of Women's Libersl Associations on the occasion of their annual Council meeting. Admir- able arrangements for the reception of the delegates bad been made by the local association, with Mrs E. C. Osborne as secretary, and the prrceedings will doubtless prove a great stimulus to the progressive cause in this constituency. The meetirgs lode place in the Parr Hall, two sest•ions being held in the morn- ing and afternoon respectively. Brand Mrs. A. H.Cros- field were present and had a very cordial recepti n. Delegates came from all parts of Lancashire and , Cheshire, representatives beit.g sent by the six divisions of Manchester, Preston, Lancaster, liner- pod, Witten, idnes, Rochdale, Bury. Prestwich, Oldham, Hollingwerth, Failsworth, Chorton-cum- Hardy, Blackburn, Warrington. Didsbury, Mtn:wham, Ashton-under-Lyne. Bond. n, Ac. Mrs. Stewart-Brown (of Liverpool) presided. and amongst thcoe present on the platform were Lady Haworth, Mrs Barton (hon. secrtrary), Mrs. Healey (hon. treasurer), Mrs. A. H. Crosfield, Mrs. C. E Parker, Mrs. Ziba Armitage, Miss Bessie Dutton, Mrs. E. C. Osborne, Mrs. Hayfield, Mrs. James Bliss, Miss Cicely Corbett, Miss Patricia Hoey, Mica Crosfield, Mrs. Ellis, Miss Bertha Mason, Bus. Chorl ton, Min Parke t (Manchester).</p>
      </div>
