      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>PIT BROW WORKERS. PROTEST AGAINST DISPLACEMENT OF WOMEN.</head>
        <p>PIT BROW WORKERS. PROTEST AGAINST DISPLACEMENT OF WOMEN.</p>
        <p>The PRESIDENT Inoved-ilie—f.Alowing resolution : That this meeting much replores the introduction of a clause in the Coal Mines Bill which would prohibit the future employment of women at the pit brow, as it will debar from an honest and not unhealthy employ- ment large numbers of women, without their being able to make any effects il political protest, and calla upon the Government and Liberal M.P.'s to strtnuouely oppose this clause tehen the Bill comes before tint louse of Commcns for its flnal reading." S'lts said the miners at their recent Cusgress unfortunately pissed a resolution supporting that clatne,lwhich was nut intro- duced by the Gavernu• it ani sh"cl3, ace trding to Mr. klasterman, would probably Le oppostd by the Govern- ment. It was</p>
        <p>AN ARBITRARY INTERFERENCE WITH TIFF RIGHT OF WOM RN</p>
        <p>to work, and it was extremely bard that a congress, representing a democratic section of the people, should be so unwise and short-sighted—in fact no blind to the true interests of women as to attempt to debar them from any honest livelihood. She disclaimed any intention on the part of the supporters of the retention of female labour to encourage a band-to-hand tussle between the sexes as to who should have work and wages. She si 'ply echoed the words of Mr Masterman that in amen s Parliament, e'ected exclusively by men, it was wrrng and illiberal that legislation should be passed which would throw women out of honest, not unhealthy and not immoral employment. (Applause.) Mrs.NUTTALL (Prestwich) seconded and said the men weretrying to oust about 5,1)00 women from their y maim In many cases she believed women were working at the pit brow under doctor's orders. One of the oljects of displacing the women was that decrepit men and boys should do their work. That wonld be an injustice to women who bad worked at the pit brow for many years acre, in many instancy's, were the supporters of (Apylause.) Miss CRosFIELD suggested that the local associations should bring pressure on members of Parliament with a view to sr curing the rejection of the clauve. (Applause). . . .</p>
        <p>Tne resolution was unanimously approved. The delegates iben adjourned for lunch whiob was provided by the Warrington Associalion.</p>
        <p>A PRESENTATION.</p>
        <p>Before the afternoon session Logan, Mrs. eftoSPltt.D, on behalf of the local association, presented Mrs. Stewart-Brown with a handsome bouquet. She men- tioned that the Blackburn Association came eat in membership, with a total of 1700, bat as the ‘Varringtoo Associstion had 1300 members cf a much smaller p. pulatir n, she thought it could almost cliim equality. (Applause).</p>
