        <head>INSURANCE BILL</head>
        <p>THE INSURANCE BILL</p>
        <p>Wet Ciextv Conn Err (Slencbes:mr, introduced the folloeing resofutica :—" (I Pais L'.111161 we!c,r..lee the</p>
        <p>Government Insurance Bill as an attempt to prevent poverty, ill-health, and misery, but is of opinion that the clauses dealing with women are inadequate, and urges that a married woman, not being an employed contributor, shall be entitled to become a voluntary contributor at the full rate with full benefits, or at the half-rate of threepence, to which the State should odd stink benefits as the actuarial calculations would allow, and that she should continue on this basis on becoming a widow, provided always that if she has been as employed contributor before marriage she shall resume that status if she re-enter employment upon widow- ! hood ; (2) that unmarried mothers who are wage-earning I ,men and insured shall be entitled to draw 7s. gd. per week for maternity as for any other illness which incapacitates her from working, and that the ffis. benefit ahall come out of the men's fund, as in the case of married women ; and (3) that provision will be made for the election of at least one- third women members on all the local health committees to be appointed under the bill." She said she did not went to minimise the good that would be done by the Bill or to suggest that they would rather not have the Bill at all than accept it with its present defects. All Liberal women felt that it was a tremendocs advance in the cause of social progress. (Hear, hear.) England was now taking her place among the nations with a real Insurance Bill which had no equal elsewhere. We were in the van of civilization. (Applause ) That, however, did not exempt women from regarding their osn interests and asking the Government to make what changes they considered necessary. They would have to ask with a great deal of emphasis or they would not be heard. She admitted that the question of insuring women was a difficult one, their industrial position being much complicated by marriage and motherhood. The first feature of the measure that was not satisfactory from their point of view, was the provision whereby the benefits received by women were smaller in proportion than those received by men, when their earnings were less than 15s, a week or 2s. 6d. a day. The fact that the employer's contribution in the case of those women would also be smaller than for men, would tend to the diminution of male employment in certain directions. The speaker criticised the Bill's provisions with regard to maternity braille, suggesting that unmarried mothers should be entitled to a weekly payment for maternity as for any other illness, and that it would be better (nail cases for the benefit, to be paid directly to the mother, as very often her greatest need FM food , and nonrishment instead of medicine and medical attendance. It was also unfortunate that the Bill made , no provision for widows left with young children, and that there was no basis on which women could be , certain that approved societies would admit them as • members. It was also unfair that women workers who i married and stayed at home should forfeit the sums I which tley had paid into the insurance fund. They re zed that the Insurance Bill brought help where it • was badly needed. It was much better than the ordinary insurance society, bocati,:e not only most the , employer contribute but the State also paid to much • per week. The married woman, however, was cut off • from its advantages. The Bill penalized the wife in the home and encouraged her deliberately to neglect her home and go out and earn her living in paid employ- ment. It was of the utmost importance that they should not perpetnate by law the idea that</p>
        <p>WORK IN TIM HOME .WAS NOT WORTH ANY MONEY PA . _</p>
        <p>How csuld the scheme te called 'a national one, when the best body of women in the country was excluded ? Sic contended that the maternity benefits in all cases should be drawn from the men's side of the scheme. With regard to the rrpresentation of women on the Health Committees, it was important as those coin- mi tses would deal with housing and other health ques- tions on which women in the home were experts. (Applause). Lady HAwOftni, in sccenling, said all Liberals were agreed that the Insurance Bill was an endeavour to do what had long been needed in this country. It must not be supposed that is raising certain sbjections they were opposed to the Bill. They were not the only people who were Dalting complaints. The doctors were up in arms and men all over the country were defending I their own rights. Women were therefore well within their rights in saying their little word. With regard to maternity benefits, she was not in agreement with the suggestion that the whole of the money should be paid directly to the woman, because under therm conditions a woman was ill-fitted to receive money and deal with it, if she had not a husband who was in agree- ment with her. She could conceive of cases in which the man might get hold of the money and spend it on himself, while the suffering woman would not receive the benefit to which she was entitled. It would be better to pay a proportion of the benefit directly to the woman, because it was very often good food and attendance that she wanted. As to the proposal that married women should be allowed to become contribu- tors, from whom would the money have to come? Most womrn had not n.eans of their own, and the money would come from their husbands. Why not say that the man should pay a certain amount of insurance for his wife ?</p>
        <p>Mrs. C. E. PARKETt (Wartingten) said sbe felt strongly on the point that unmarried mothers should be cared for. We wanted a good population. We did not want our nation to grow up from weakly, under-fed and under-helped peot.le. She would like unmarried mothers in this country to be cared for, as they were in France, during confinement and until they were well enough to go out with their little ones. There was a rooted idea that a husband earned money and a wife who stayed at home did not. In her opinion the woman who kept house and brought tip a family did just as much to earn the money as her husband. (Applause.) If men could be educated to regard the matter in that light, and if all women would tit themselves to spend the money wisely and well we should be on the road to the millennium. (Applause.) Se believed it was the desire on the part of woman, whether married or single, to develop the test that was in her. that had culminated in the demand for votes for women. (Applause). The resolution was agreed to. HOME RULE SUPPORTED.</p>
        <p>MiIS PATRICIA Bony (United Irish League) moved : " That in the opinion of this Council the granting of an efficient measure of Home Rule is an act a justice to Ireland, which the it'd no longer be delayed, and which will ultimately tend not only to the prosperity of Ireland, but to stronger and more cordial relations between the two countries." She said liberty seas the birthright of every nation, tut since the Act of Union there had been no liberty in Ireland. After a hundred years of trial, England herself had to acknowledge that she was unable to govern Ireland either to her own credit or the good of the sister isle. Before the Act was parsed the taxation of the Irish amounted to Is. per head ; when Mr. Gladstone brought in his Home Role Bill it had risen to 31s per head, and to-day it was £2 per head. During that time, as a result of English administration, the population of Ireland had steadily decreased. People had been driven out of Ireland, because, in an over-taxed country, they had no money to support their industries, their agricultore, or the homes of the people. At &amp;at England's policy towards Ireland was one of oppression ; now it was one of mis- take. Having given further illustrations of the unfair- ness of the burden of officialdom, police charges and taxation which rested upon the Irish people, Miss Hoey said Ireland was</p>
        <p>11E ONLY COMM UNITY OF WHITE MEN</p>
        <p>under British rule, which lied been cleiaie'il the right of managing its own affairs. It had teen stated in the English Presa that when Mr. Redmond and Mr. O'Cocnor cent to America they gave a different version of Home Rule from what they told the English people. Before Mr. Redmond went abroad on his last dollar- collecting expedition—(langhter)—he wrote for a idely circulated American magazine a statement of whit was meant by Home Rule. According to that article, it meant legislative and executive control of all purely Irish •ffairs, sot ject to the supreme authority of the Imperial Parliament. By local affairs they meant education, local government, transit, labour, industry, local taxes, order, justice, police, &amp;c.—matters which every small community managed for itself. Since the daja of Butt there had</p>
        <p>NEVRRIREEN A .DEMAND FOR SEPARATION</p>
        <p>• on Ireland's part. (Applause). The working of the County Councils in Ireland had showed that the people were capable not only of proper government but of moderate and prober control of money. As ts the allegation that Protestants would be oppressed by the Roman Catholics under a Home Rule system, if there was the faintest chance of that, not one member of the United Irish League would raise his or her hand to help Home Rule. (Applause). Miss Iloey gave striking figures to show that in the distribution of public office. the Roman Catholic poptilatitn bad exercised ills utmost (airman toward the Protestant minority. Mr. Redmond had offered, without reservation or limitation, to accept any safeguard that Protestants wished to include in the Home Rule Bill. In the recent fight against the House of Lords the democracies of Engisnd and Ireland stood side by side to overcome the poser of those who hal oppressed the liberties of the people. The Irish now said, "As we helped you in your hour of need, come and help Its to make Ireland like every other portion of the British Empire—s contented, prosperous and devoted friend of England." (Applause). Miss CRO.,FIELD seconded. She said Lord Morley once stated that we would never read history, if it were not for the lea OES it taught us. To put it in more homely phrase, the proof of the pudding mt, in the eating, and they had splendid proof of the success of Home Rule in Canada. When Queen V,ctoria came to the Throne in 18.17 Canada was in armed rebellion against England. She had intimidation, cattle driving, and everything that had taken place at the worst crises of Irish history. There were two races and religions, which looked upon each other with hatred and suspicion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>:STABLISHMENT SUPPORTED.</head>
        <p>:STABLISHMENT SUPPORTED.</p>
        <p>STRKSGTHENEU 'CHEER CLAIM VI.3N THAT PARTY FOR</p>
        <p>FULLER RECOGNITION when opportunity arose, They ought to receive con- sideration as citizens. (Applause). They had shown in the past a political intelligence not inferior to that possessed by the average male canvasser or voter. They had shown an usezampled amount of unselfish devotion to a party which had hitherto not been able to gratify all the claims they had put forward on behalf of their sec Because of that loyalty and et•votien and CI r political intelligence they wanted the party seriously to consider their claims for further opportunities of political and naticnal service. (Hear, hear). At the last election they obtained a victory over their</p>
        <p>_ GREAT TRADITIONAL EYEAIT, the IL:use of Lords—(applause)-5 victory which in its consequences it would be most ditnzult to measure, as assisting not only the fortunes of the Liberal party —that was a secondary object—but in pressing forward legislation for the improvement of the conditions of the labouring classes, which was essential in any country yvolesaing to be democratic and self-governed. Too long had we tolerated the absurd and anarcbcinal system of a astute of Lords in our midst. Too patient seers the British electors in allowing the Lords to possess is veto which enabled them to frustrate almost every attempt made by successive Liberal governments to accomplish really Liberal legislation. the pr.ivisians of the Parliament Bill were</p>
        <p>NIORRE kW. IN TIIE EXTREME. It would rest with future governmeuts to reform the actual coostitution of the House of Lord- but for the pre% et Li herals were satisfied that its power for evil had been ert-ctually curbed. The Lords could no longer throw out Budgets like Mr. Lloyd George's—(applause)</p>
        <p>Welsh part of the British Church sutered into a state of fusion with the Homan Church. That, however, did not mean that the Welsh Church accepted anything in the nature of a Saxon or Canterbury supremacy. In 1115, however, they had the subjugation of the Welsh Church hy force sod It was front that year that the Establisameot as they knew it t).ilty d tted in Wales Whenever they tad had anything lathe nature of a national movement or awakening in Wales, the Church had always been against it. Having quoted instances, the speeier said that during the last fifty }ears Wales had witnessed</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>NATIONAL REVIVAL</head>
        <p>A NATIONAL REVIVAL</p>
        <p>which c, ..td hardly he paralleled in the last century— a revival coupled with the nen es of Tom Ellis, Lloyd George and Ellis Griffith—(applause)—snd a new and unexampl,d 83 stem of education had been established in Wales. Hod tLe Church done anything in that direc- tion? Ho had in his bend a list of educational scheme. brought before the House of Lords since 1893. Eleven schemes, which had been demanded by the Welsh people came before Parliament for consideration nod the Welsh bishops wrecked eight of them. It aces not to be wondered at tbat enyone who believed in that little country, who thought it still had a mission to fell% would like to remove the one great force which tended to denationalise the Church in Wales and make it unsympathetic, cold and sullen towards national movements. Not only was it unsympethetic but it was at the present moment unpopular. The evidence given before the Royal Commission showed that there were 5.50,000 communicants of the Nonconformist churches in Wales, which meant that a quarter of the population were full members, taking a full p.rt in the work and government of those churches. Of the 2,000, 000 population only 193,000 were communicants of the Established Church. Considering the distribu- tion of the populati n, the figures meant that of every three Baptists worshipping in England nn i Wales one worshipped in Wales and of every four Con- gregationalists one worshipped in Wales, although the population of Wales was only one sixteenth of the whole. There never was a stranger religious p'tenomenon than that in the course of 100 years Nonconformity should capture to that extent the life of is little nation. The Ni nconformists provided 1,500,00 seats in their chapels as compared with 47,8,C00 provided by the Church of England. In 1905 the Nonconforrnista collected nearly half a million pounds —largely from the poor peasants of Wales—for the maintenance of their ministry end services, whilst in the tame 3 eat the wealthiest Church in the world collected in Wales for the sane purposes £16,000. The Establishment had divorced the Church from the people of Wales, though tie people did their best to remain loyal to it for nearly a century. The policy of disendowment was inseparable frost din- establishment, the State having asserted its right to control over the Church and having extended privilege* to it for the reason that the Church was gathering into its own bands a large amount of money. Tie endow- ments, be maintained, were national property. Tithes, 1 glebe, Parliamentary grants from Queen Anne's Bounty, and all the proporty of the Church, except private belie- . factions since the Act of Uniformity, they considered</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>AN-ADA_DEMAN_DED HOME RU 1.11</head>
        <p>AN-ADA_DEMAN_DED HOME RU 1.11</p>
        <p>to settle her own affairs. Her trade was stagnant ; her small population was decreasing, no capital was going into the country. The English GovercmPnt tried a half-measure, but it was no good. Disaffection and unrest went on much as before. In face of the condi- tion of affairs, the British Government was at length brave enough to grant a full measure of Boma Rule. The result was extraordinary. Within a short period, prosperity replaced misery, and at Queen Victoria's Jubilee fifty years later Englishmen could hardly be got to relieve that Canada had ever been disaffected. (Applause ) That was the result of giving them complete local autonomy and letting them settle their own affairs. To-day Canada was</p>
        <p>rItOr•WIRO,', (20NTENTSIr A'l) HAW MONIOUS, arid the two feCts worked aide by ,ije. Sipco Home</p>
        <p>Rule was granted, Canada had never taken one step towards separation from Great Britain. She was only separated by an imaginary line from the greatest Republic in to world, but with every natural and , political reason for throwing in her lot with the United Stater, she hal never attempted to do so—in fact, only the other day ahe took a step rather in the other direc- tion, remaining faithful to the British Empire which had given her n free hand in the management of her own affairs. Having referred to the happy results of self-government in South Africa, Mlsa Crosdeld asked why the stme Literal policy ahould fail in liebind ? It eras net eurprising that the COLONIES HAD REPEATEDLY URGED THE GOVERNMENT</p>
        <p>TO GRANT HOME RELB to Ireland. (Applause ) If there was any religious bitterness in that country she was afraid,as a Protestant, one must confess that it existed on the Protestant and not on the Roman Catholic side. If any Irish leader had gone about making the incendiary speeches which Sir Edward Carson, hi'. F. E. Smith and others had been making, IN4 I PING PEOPLE TO OPEN REBELLION,</p>
        <p>it would have been said that hanging was too good for him. (Laughter.) They could not speak in too unmeasured terms of the conduct of those men in trying to egg on Ulster—to whip ep a dig which did not want to be driven. (Hear, hear.) They had asked the people to fight and shed blood, to go into opea rebellion against the King's Writ. Such conduct would base been called treasomms and the instigator's hoick would have been on tho block in the days of Henry VIII. (Linghter). Is 1894 a Financial Relations Commission was appointed and found that in that year Ireland was being over- taxed to the tune of £2,500,000. The Union bad been in existence for a hundred years, and during that time Ireland must have been over-taxed to the amount of £300,000,00 or .C400,000,000.Th0 average income per head in Ireland was only .Cl 5 as against .ClO per head in Eng- land. In short, the system of goverment in Ireland was absolutely rotten. Scotland had a larger popula- tion than Ireland, but its aalaried offtcials who were liable for income tax numbered 9:38 as against 4,500 in Ireland. In Scotland ethzials' salaries amounted to £315,000: Ireland had to pay £1,435,C00 under that bead. She was the poorest part of the British Isles, but her government was</p>
        <p>ONE -MASS OF EXTRAVAGANCE AND CONFUSION. If people would only read Irish history their hearts would bleed for what that country had suffered at the bands of English governments. If there was a forgiving nation on the face of the earth, it. was the Irish. Ireland reminded her of a beautiful, refined and spiritual woman who had been joined in an ill-assorted union with a man who was dense, material and misunderstand- ing and who had nearly broken her heart with injustice and oppression. Think of all the resources there were in that race! Ireland stood fi-st in the world for morality end for high spiritual intelligence. She was the teacher of the world in the early days ; to-day she was lying almost in ruins. No wonder that Gladstone fought for Home Ride and as true Liberals they ought to follow in his steps. (übpil'aure.) The resolution was carried unanimously..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>WOMEN IN LOCAL GOVERNMENT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN IN LOCAL GOVERNMENT.</p>
        <p>Mien BERTHA MASON proposed a resolution demanding the amendment of the Qualification of Women Act, 1907. " Thie Council," it read, "would call the attention of the Government to the fact that the Act, which eeclares that 'a woman shall not be disqualified by sex or marriage from being elected to or being a councillor or alderman of the council of any county or borough,' is a dead letter for married women outside London ; end would also point out that for lack of the electoral qualification, many good workers among married women are also ineligible. Wherefore this meeting prixss the Government to bring in a till providing a residential qualification for candidates for county and borough councils as an alternative to the existing qualification." 3liss Mason said the reason why there were SO few women serving upon borough and county councils was that only electors were eligible to serve upon them. Nearly all married women were debarred from standing as candidates because, in most cases, the husband held the voting qualification. Such a Bill as the resolution asked for would extend the work of women in local government enormously and it would give electcrs the right to choose whom they would like for their representatives. , (Applause). Airs. Etus seconded. _ _</p>
        <p>The PRESIDFST Mid. it wag melancholy to think of the specimens of men who were sometimes put up to contest muds at municipal electicns, and to contrast them with ladies who were ineligible to serve. The resoluticn was agreed to.</p>
        <p>WOMEN'S SUFFRAGE. SUPPORT FOR TIIR CONCILIATION BILL.</p>
        <p>Mrs. STEWART-BROwN propr heel a resolution: "That this Connell, while deeply regretting that Ilse Women's Enfranchisement. Bill introduced by Sir George Kemp, which passed its second reading by still a splendid maj irity, did not make further progress in the House of Commons last session, gratefully appreciates the Prime Minister's promite thi.t facilities shall be granted next year." She pointed out that the Bill had commanded the largest majority in the House of Commons that they had been able to secure for a Women's Suffrage Bill. It had served to draw together all sectiona of the women's suffrage party, all the societies being united in their demand for the measure. Although the Bill would not enfran- chise more than 1,000.0t0 women they believed it would break down the sex barrier, which they resented, and would be the means of introducing a wider franchise in the future. They had an undertaking from the Prime Minister that he would give facilities for the Bill dur- ing the next Session of Parliament. They believed in Mr. Asquith's promise, and they had ample confidence that when he raid that be would give reasonable facilities he would give such facilities as would assure the passage of the till, so that if the Lords rejected it there would he time for it to be sent back to the House of Lords and passed tinder the Veto Bill before Perlis- meet came to an end. Women suffragists were unanim- ous in wishing their sex to be enfranchised before another general election came. (Applause). Mrs.Brocrox seconeed.She card that they were willing to take what they could get in the direction of enfranchisement of women tut, like Oliver Twist, they would "ask for more:' They felt that it would not be right to press the Government unduly to pass a wider bill which the country at large was not ready for. They had tn deal gently with the uninitiated—(laughter)— and a large mass of public opinion had yet to be educated to the justice of the women's demand to *mkt In the government of the net ion.l he people who objected to women getting a yore under the bill were people who objected for a sentimental reason. If a hundred women had ten castles each they would not outvote the go percent. of working women who would be enfran- chised by the bill. Opposition to the resolution came from one delrgate who f.ankly declared that she was rot in favour of the franchise being given to women. She did not believe that the moss of women desired it, and, speaking from experience, she said certainly not the mass of working women. There was a difference of opinion in the party about the matter, end tha hill would do more harm than good to women, would set up more inequalities than it remedied, wculd he detrimental to the party, and would be bad in the true interests both of women and the nation.</p>
        <p>Mrr. FINDLAY (Manchester), speaking es in &amp;dolt suffragist but not in opposition to the resolutis n, said that there were some difficulties to be consieered. She had burn told thst some members of the Labour party would not vote for the bill unless the qualificstion were amended. Why should not the Imperial franchise be on the same basis an the municipal Mies CROSFIELD pointed out that when the MO of England first got the vote ooly 1.000,C00 were enf ranchised.lt would be a revolution to aids the Govern- ment to enfranchise everybody immediately. To the Indy who no strongly objected to the resolution, ate would say that the one considerwinn which converted her (Miss Crosfleld) to women's suffrage sae : Why were the men so keen to get the vote? It was f their own protection. Women were weoker than wee and re:ded protection even more. (Applause). Mrs. HAYFIELD said she had not found env great : desire on the part of advocates of adult suffrage to have women included in the Bill. She believed many of them thought there ws• a sporting (hence of the clause in favour of women being thrown out. (Laughter). Miss MASON printed out that in Colonise where women had the vote there had bees an enormous advance in the method of dealing with questions of purity, temperance end the protection of child's n. lb. resolution was passed with one dissentient. A JOINT CONFERENCE.</p>
        <p>Tbe Council also passed a resolution desiring the Executive Committee of the Union to ripproach the Executive Committee of the Derbyshire, Yorkshirencid ober Northern Unions of Women Liberal Associstions for the purpose of holding a joint conference with the Lancashire and Cheshire Union in the spriog of 1912, at s convenient provincial centre,to confer on orgsoiss- tion and propagandism. THE EXECUTIVE.</p>
        <p>The mimes lA' the ruccearful candidates for the Executive Committee were announced. They were Lady Toolmin, Min Crotfleld, Mns [Homer!. Mrs Chorlton, Mrs Clifford Muspratt, Mra Ellis, and Mrs Beret f ord</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <p>MR. A. H. CROSFIELD PROPOSES A VOTE OP THANKS. -- WOMEN AND SOCIAL REFOSII. • -- -</p>
        <p>. - Mr. A. H. CROSIrIBLD, who hal a cordial reception, moved a vote of thanks to Mrs. Stewart-Brown for her able conduct of the proceedings. lie raid all Liberals in Warrington deeply appreciated the advantage of having Mrs. Stewart-Brown preeiling over that very important meeting in their n i Ist. They admired her career, and the ability and distinction with which eke had carried out her politictl work—(hear, bear)—in not the easiest quarter of England t propagate Liber ri opinions. (Laughter and applause.) In Warrington the, Liberal women were in the very forefront of poll, i sal activity. (Applause.) They had in face of consider- able obstacles which had to be dealt with in tbie pert of the ocruntry, dons splendid and energetic work, and be was certain that that floe gather• ing would be an additional incentive to them—if any were needed—to put even more enerpy and vigour intc that work. Personally, he ow truly grateful to them for the work they had done in Warrington. He did not wonder that so many leaders of political thought were assembled in that conference, because the more they saw of the development end complexity of modern life, the more they came to close qrs Hoer, with social reform, the more they realized that, however vital it was to every man, it seemed to come home with additional and closer force to the women of the country. (Hear, hear.) Whether one considered Free Trade, with the comparative abundance of commodities for their houraholds at comparatively reasonable prices; the many phases of social reform and the democratic finance which was making such work possible; or the people's Badget which embodied the principle of a better distribution of wealth—on every count modern politics came horns even more directly to the life of the womanhood of the country than to the mole population. (Applause.) Ile rejoiced eno-mously in the incressed activity in pilaf* affairs on the part of the women of E island—ad activity which he believed was destined to even grester extension in the future. (Applause.) MN. ARNITTAGE seconded and the vote was pa sss with enthusiasm.</p>
        <p>After the conference the delegates were entertaidei at tea by the local ass 'ciation. WARRING ros DELEGATE 3 AND WORKERS. The arrangements for the conference reflected the utmost credit on the memhere of the Warringtoo Women's Liberal Associaton. Toe Warring toa delegates to the conference were Mrs James D idds. Mrs Walker, Mrs Lindley, Mrs Pemhert in, hire T I Locker and Mrs O'Brien. Mrs E C Oaboroe, the capable- and herd-working hon. secretary, was the representative of the association on the Esecutive Committie of the Union. Otbcr workers who rendered valuable service were :—Returning Officer, Nliss Parker, Mannhester attendants, Mrs T Bunch and Mrs Roberts; stewards attending to the delegate', Mrs E V Walker, Miss Dodds, Miss Walker, Miss Short ; stewards attending to the visitors, Mns J Hawthorn, Misses Wil stetson and Miss Willett; committee attending to the lunch (superintended by Miss Baoth), Mrs Dotton, Mrs Delaney, Mrs Jr Locker. Mrs Ridgway, 'lire H use, Mrs R Jones, Mrs Atkin, Mns Gorse, Mrs Fox, M's Knowles Mrs Nihon", Mrs Looker, Mrs Whitlow. Mn J.Hary,. Mrs IVinakill, Miss Fairhurst, Mrs Booth.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>This week we have a return visit of Fred Ginnetee reproduction of "Dick Turpin's ride to York " and Wart ingt m people have gone in ciowds a this popular music hall. The production is on a grand scale—lavish scenery and plenty of characters - but the feature is the fire discipline of the four horses, notably "Black Bess." Everybody knows about this fascinating story of outlaw life, but one's idea of the past is made more vivid by this realistic presentation. The production is also greatly aided by the cinemato- graph which depicts scenes of robbery on the highway. Cavallotti Bros. submit a fine mmical programme, dis- playing efficiency in the playing of se, eral instuments As vocalists Stewart and Morgan are decidedly. entertaining, their falsetto style appealing in great measure to the taste of the auctierce. Bert Welds and Dorothy Wynne engage in comedy jugg Mg and give a creditable exhibition of sand dancing. Ilyan and Hall are superb eccentric comedians and burlesque wrestlers. They evince considerable dexterity of move- ment and introduce very laughable allusions. Nina May is a charming soprano vocalint and May de Howes gains popularity with a c rtain section as a commethenne. Her dancing is uumWakably and fantastic.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>WORN OUT AT 40.</head>
        <p>WORN OUT AT 40.</p>
        <p>Men who are Nervous Wrecks made Fit and Well by Dr. Williams Pink Pills.</p>
        <p>There a,e lundreds of weak, .worn-out. nervous</p>
        <p>men who would give all their worldly got- de to</p>
        <p>!egaia a</p>
        <p>fair c'afia-r-e of</p>
        <p>he—alth and steady nerves. They are nct o'd men, but men who alictdd he making their mark in businese, handicapped because their nerves are</p>
        <p>exhausted. To such sufferers the words of ..Mr. Ftederick</p>
        <p>S. Mitchell, an engineer, cf 20, Gathorne Road, Ashton Gate, Btistol, will</p>
        <p>be instructive. Chatting with a journalist rccently, Mr. Mitchell said : —"Work</p>
        <p>began to get.. Mficalt for Delea!</p>
        <p>me semi time after I</p>
