      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>LATE MR. HUBERT CONGREVE.</head>
        <p>THE LATE MR. HUBERT CONGREVE.</p>
        <p>DRAMATIC SUICIDE FROM CANTILEVER BRIDGE.</p>
        <p>"A Deplorable and Distressing Case."</p>
        <p>RESULT OF WORRY AND NERVOUSNESS.</p>
        <p>The pathetic and sensational circumstances surround- ing the d. ath of Mr. Hubert Consreve, Summerville, Crappenhall, chief engineer of the Man, heater Ship Canal Company, wham body was found in the canal near the Cantilever Bridge on Friday of last week, were inquired into by Mr. H. C. Yates, comity coroner, and a jury at the late residence of the deceased on Monday morning. Mr. F. R. Roberts was the foreman of the jury. The Ship Canal Company was represented by Mr. Newman, solicitor, Manchester. The first witness was _ . . . .</p>
        <p>r or, Dr. Carruthers, of Halton, who stated that he had been deceased's medical attendant since Mr. Congreve name to live in Cheshire some years ago. The Coroner : What have you attended him for chiefly ? Witness: I attended him for an attack of laryngitis and also for acute dyspepsia. Did that dyspepsia continue I—No. He had not complained of it for two or three years. I think you saw him recently I,—My last attendance commenced on Sunday week. He sent for me, and when I came over I found him in bed. I asked him what wan the matter with him, lie said he did not know ; he felt completely " done." Bodily, do you mean ?—He said he felt completely done, and did not feel able to do any mock at all. He</p>
        <p>;LEM ED VERY MUCH DEPRESS'ED. IN SPIRITS.</p>
        <p>Ile was nervous, was he wondered whether he was going to have an attack of influenza. I examined him and found there was nothing bodily wrong with him. Ile eaa iu perfect health, bodily. I said to him, " You are a little over-worked. You ought to take a rest." Ile asked me what I would recommend him to do then, so I avid" You had I otter get up, have your Imo+ end take a walk, and you will feellatter." I also said I would give him a tonic and something to take at night, as he complained of inanmniv. Did you see him again?—! saw him on Monday ' morning. Wes ha any better I—As soon as I left 'aim on Sunday, I woe told, he got up, took a walk, and amused himself in the evening with his friend Mr. Murray who is staying here. Ile seemed all right, but he had not slept well. t asked him if he hat taken what I prescribed and he said he did not think there was any reed for it. . _ -</p>
        <p>Did you continue attending him daily ?--I saw him on Tuesday morning and he then walked into the dining-room joi.t as usual and said "lam quite well this morning. There is nothing the matter with me." I said "I think you had better have a rest. You had better apply for leave of absence. I think you are over-worked and you must not have this occur again." He then expressed a wish to see a specialist, no I took him to I.iverpool and we saw Dr. Glynn. Was that for the nerves ?—yrs. .</p>
        <p>What wan his report 2—His report was that he seemed to have had a nervous breakdown and he strongly advised him to apply for a month's leave of absence to go a say and amuse himself, lie also prescrib:ed for him. Then you did not ewe him again ?—I asked him if I should go the next day but he said " I shall be at the office. You need not come. If Jam worse, I will send for you." In your opinion, would this nervous breakdown affect the brain ?—Certainly, if continucus. When you attended him did you consider his brain was troubled?—l thought he was very depressed about himself. Then a abort time after! had talked to him, be seemed all right again. He bad lucid intervals. That is often the ens, when the brain is affected ? Yes ; they have lucid intervals. At the same time, you did not consider him a clan who ought to be under restraint ?—I did not. Ile seemed no very much better that I thought it was jest a temporary I reakdown and after a little rest he would he all right again. Was he anxious about his work ?—Yes, he was. Of course, some people can bear anxiety better than others ?—Of course, they can. I said to him We all get anxious, but you base done your best, and what more can you do ? " Ile was highly strung ?—Yes, very highly. Did you think he confided in you all his trouble 2—l think no. _</p>
        <p>He was not a reserved ma ?—He told me everything that he felt. -</p>
        <p>..•.. It was merely his business—the work of the canal— and nothing else that was upsetting him f—Entirely the work of the canal hemline I said to him " It is</p>
        <p>NOT YOUR FAULT IF TIIERF. IS NOT MUCH WATER in the csnal or if the sand is silting up. " Mrs. Congreve was then called. She said the deceased was 53 years of age and was the chief engineer of the Manchester Ship entail Company. On Thursday evening at a little before 7 o'clock they went upstairs to dress for dinner. Deceased had been in the house since 5.30. . .</p>
        <p>Did he say anything to you about any letters that he had to Bend ?—that he must writes letter or anything 2 No, I don't think so. After dressing, did you go into his dressing room to see if he was there ?—No I heard him go downstairs and called out to him whether he was ready for dinner. He said he was. _</p>
        <p>Did he answer quite naturally I—No, not quite naturally ; rather hastily. Did you hear him go out of the house ?—No. When I came down I found he was not there.l asked my maid what had become of him, and she said she thought he bad gone to the post. Did you wait awhile ?—I waited a little bit, but I had my suspicions because the door was shut. Wioness next related how she went to the post and saw nothing of the deceased. She then searched the garden and alsk went to gee Dr. Carruthers, but could find no trace of the deceased. After having made every effort to discover his whereabouts sbe went to his room upstairs and in the drawer of the dressing table she found deceased's purse, his pocket book and also an undirected note which stated that</p>
        <p>_H E COULD NOT FACE LOSING HIS</p>
        <p>The Coroner : In the conversation that you had on Thuralay night, was he talking rationally to you?— Absolutely rationally. Was he &amp;premed at all 2—No. We were talking about the holiday we were going to have. The doctor had advised a holiday and he had made up his mind to go, arid the letter he posted that night was to ask for a holiday. Wes he looking forward to the holiday ?—Yes. He made inquiries auto the place he should go to. Did you know that he possessed a revolver ?—No I did not. I knew that he had bad OW, but he gave it away. We hear from the doctor that he had been very depressed of late. Had he to you ever expressed any intention of taking his life ?-oh, n 0... .</p>
        <p>He was moat anxious about the Sbp Canal. Thi summer ha upset him a good deal 7—Yea. Wee it because</p>
        <p>THE DRY WEATHER HAD AFFECTED THE CANAL? —Yes. That was what vas chiefly on his mind. He used to talk most about that nnd the strikes. .</p>
        <p>He was over an riotts,l suppoeee— X ea,be wee oertaioly overreux ions.</p>
        <p>He had not long been chief engineer bad he 2—Since last January. Ho he had the whole weight of the Canal on his own mind /—Yea. _</p>
        <p>In reply to Mr. Murray of Edinburgh. who had been fitsyint at Summervills, witness said she was away from borne the week-end before last and the deceased sent for her to come btck at once. When she returned he said</p>
        <p>"A DRPIADFUL THING HAS HAPPENTD.</p>
        <p>I have utterly broken down. I have boat my nerve.' She asked him why he was in the garden and he mid " The doctor say. it in not influenm. It is much worse than that. I have utterly broken down." Witnere stated that some time after deceased bad been niirmed, a</p>
        <p>RIVOLVER_CASN WAS FOUND</p>
        <p>hidden behind. co-at. She had not. the alighteet idea that .these wee such thing in the house.</p>
        <p>Ada Taylor, housemaid et Summerville, raid that at 645 on Thursdsy rsening she saw the deceased writing at his desk. Afterwards he vent upstairs and came down dreamed for dinner. He asked if she had posted the letters and she replied in the affirmative. Seeing that dace wed had a letter in his hand she volunteered to post it, hot deceased thanked her and arid be would go himself. He then left the house. Ile talked quite rationally. Harold Mansell Owen, 11 years of age, 3 Fairfield- road, Latchford Without, stated that about 7.15 on Thursday evening he and two other bovs were pla3ing under the Cantilever Bridge. Ile heard • crash and thought it was the report of a firework. The sound appeared to come from the station side of the bridge. The Coroner : Did you look up at the bridge 1 Witness : Yea What did you use P—l SAW A DARK FORM FALL INTO THE MAUR _</p>
        <p>About the CicrZe Ii was too dark for you to discern whether it was ft human being or not ?—Yes. I thought somebody had thrown • log of wood. You only heard one report ?—Yee, air. Did you call out ?—Yes, I shouted " Is there anybody In the can't ? " There wss no answer.</p>
        <p>Could you see anything Ilaating in the canal ?—No, . .</p>
        <p>about</p>
        <p>Witness added that he did not see anybody walk across the bridge and when he looked up he could not distinguish anybody. Percy M. Owen, elder brother of the last witness, stated that at two o'clock on Saturday afternoon be VOl.ll crossing the Cantilever Bridge, along with a friend named John Peacock. He was looking over the west</p>
        <p>side parapet when in a recess he saw a fire-arm. He got over the parapet and found the Mauser pistol produced. He took the weapon to the Chief Constable of Warring- ton. By the side of the pistol arts a spent cartridge. The side of the recess bore marks as if a man had recently stood on it. Witneas's brother had told him about hearing a noise on Thursday night hut witness had not gone to the budge with the intention of finding anything. Jobnathan Hughes, captsio of the Ituncorn Grappling Corps, Alcook-street, Runcorn, spoke to recovering the body of the deceased under the Cantilever Bridge wbere there was a depth of 261 t. of water. In reply to the foreman of the jury, witness said there was no truth in the rumour that the body was weighted with dumb-bells. P.C. Holland said he ...died deceased's clothing and found 80. in money, an eye glass and other small articles, but no letter. Deceased was in e‘ening dress. On examining the body witness found a bullet wound in the left breast. The bullet must have passed right through the body and come out below the left shoulder. There was a hole through the clothing which was also singed, indioating that the pistol must have been held very clone to the body when it wan fired. The Coroner, In his address to the jury, described the case as a very deplorable and distressing one. One bad always sad cases to inquire into, but this waa one of the saddest that had come before him for a long time. Decoaaed had only recently been promoted to the chief engineership of the Ship Canal Company, and the past summer had apparently been a most trying one for him, owing not only to the scarcity of water hut to the strikes. Both factors combined to give him a great deal more labour and anxiety than would otherwise have been the case:Chat was the more unfortunate because he had only just taken over the command of the canal. It had evidently had a great effect upon him, so much AO that Dr. Carruthers was called in and found that deceased, although bodily he was perfectly well, was very much run down. His</p>
        <p>NERVES WERE EVIDENTLY IN A VERY ALARMING CONDITION.</p>
        <p>Deceased had decided to go on a holiday In the hope that it might bring restoration of health and strength and on the 'Thursday night he had actually discussed the arrangements with his wife. The Coroner drew attention to the curious sequence of events which led to Percy Owen finding the pistol after his younger brother had heard the report on the Thursday night and seen something fall into the river. An important feature of the case was that deceased's cloth- ing was very much singed on the left breast, drawing one to the conclusion that be must have pressed the weapon close to his, breast and fired it. Ihecause of death was perfectly clear—that deceased committed suicide. Now came the important question as to the state of his mind when be did it. , They had ample evidence from the doctor, coupled with the fact that the wants and necessities of the canal during the rummer must have played a sad and serious part with deceased's nerves. The nerves affected the brain and if in a moment of aborr ation he did that which the next moment he would have bitterly regretted or, if he had read of anybody else doing it would have resented it most strongly, they must 001113 to the con- clusion that his mind was momentarily unhinged. The jury returned a verdict of " Suicide whilst temporarily insane." The Coroner said be was sure the jury would join with him inn vote of sympathy with Mre. Congreve in her sad bereavement. It might be some consolation, however small, to her to know that there were people who sympathised with her in her sad affliction. Mr. Newman, OD behalf of the Canal Company, said they could not yet realize the full extent of their loss. From the Chairman down to the office hoy,the deceived was as popular ass man could be and no-one knew how useful be was to the company. They deeply sympa- thised with Mrs. Congreve is her tragio loss.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>CONDOLBNCE FROMRI3NCORN BOARD OF</head>
        <p>CONDOLBNCE FROMRI3NCORN BOARD OF</p>
        <p>GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Runcorn Board of Guardians hold at Dutton on Tuesday afternoon, Mr. James Davies moved a vote of condolence with Mrs. Congreve in the death of her husband. He said that she was a member of the Ladies Visiting Committee of the Dutton Workhouse and the inmates were always glad to see her.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Hallows seconded and said that Mrs. Congreve was the acme of kindness and she deserved much</p>
        <p>sympathy in her sad bereavement. The Chairman (Mr. J. Spruce) mid they would all be agreed on the motion. They were all very sorry at the sad calamity which had come upon such a worthy lady. The motion was carried in silence, the members standing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>AN APPRECIATION.</head>
        <p>AN APPRECIATION.</p>
        <p>The disaster which has cut short, with such terrible suddenness, the career of Hubert Congreve has caused deep grief in circles quite unconnected with his profession. His distinction as an eagineer, so lately attested by his appointment to the very important post he held, will doubtless receive due tribute from the proper quarters. As one who knew him chiefly in other cap icities, I should like to express se mething of what be was in these. A childhood spent in Italy,l and subsequent years in Fiance had given Mr. Congreve a basis of French and Italian culture rare, to say the least, among Englishmen. Both languages he spoke fluently, an accomplishment frequently of service when foreign engineers visited the Canal, and he had a connoisseur's familiarity with their arts. For many years it was his custom to spend his brief summer holiday in cycling, with his wife, am: ng the cathedral towns of France. These were times of the keenest zest to him, which he would communicate to his friends on his return, aided by the excellent photographs which he every- ' where took. He knew the special traits in the style of each cathedral; in particular he gloried in the old stained glass, where it hale survived the Revolution. To discover a French cathedral which Congreve did not know was rot absolutely impossible, such is the limitlees wealth of France in great churches, hut it was a matter for triumph. It was only within the last two or three years that this strenuous mode of holiday-making had given place to locating on the Norfolk Broads.</p>
        <p>Strenuousness was indeed one of the notes of CongreveM full and abounding nature. But it was strenuousness that never suggested effort or weari- ness ; rather the strength of a strong man joyously put forth day by day, in great things and in email. His hearty and genial presence breathed vitality wherever he went. His talk was Mll of anecdote and allusion, and no man had a keener judgment on a picture or a sonata. IN hether as guest or host he was a delightful companion, often the scut of the company. . . .</p>
        <p>But under this genial presence lay a deeper btraio of which not many, perhaps, of his associates were fully aware. Ile was a nephew of the late Dr. Richard Congreve, the earliest English disciple of Auguste Comte, and for many years the leader of Positivism in this country. In this faith Hubert Congreve was brought up, and in this, with some reserves, he remained to the end. Some of his closest associates were to be found among the adherents of the Religion of Humanity in 1....md0n and in the North. In the work of the Church of Humanity in Liverpool in particular (whose publication of Comb's correspondence with Clotilde de Vilna was recently noticed in these columns) he ft equeutly took part, and nowhere will lie be more mimed. He was one of those who help both to interpret and to justify Coutte's dictum, to many so strange : L'homme derient de plus en plus rebgteux. C. Mllsaroan.—"Manchester Guardian."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>FUNERAL.</head>
        <p>THE FUNERAL.</p>
        <p>The remains of the late Mr. Congreve were cremated at the Southern Cemetery, Manchester, on Tuesday morning. A touching scene, which demonstrated the great respect in which Mr. Congreve was held, was witnessed on the removal of the body from Latchford to Manchester by road on Monday evening. Some 200 men employed on the local sections of the Canal, assembled at the house of the deceased, and reverently followed the hearse in which the remains had been placed some distance on the Knutaford-road, subse- quently lining upon each side of the road whilst the cortege went by. Afterwards the men reversed and walked back to the house where Mr. Murray of Edinburgh (Mrs. Congreve's cousin) and Mrs. Congreve herself came to the door and thanked them for their kind expression of sympathy. Among those preterit were Captain Wareing (canal au petiniendeut), Mr Horner (resident engineer), Mr Leigh (sssiatant engineer), and Mr Marshall (Latchford Loeb:nester). . _ . . . _</p>
        <p>At the Crematorium the service was conducted according to the rites of the Religion of Humanity by the Rev. Sydney Styla, of the Liverpool Church of Humanity. According to the' order of the service" death took place on 26 Shakespeare 57, and a feature of the service was a reading from Auguste Comte, which concluded : , In death alone can we attain s üblinte transform- ation towards which our animal nature tend. The dead alone represent humanity in truth ; they constitute humanity. The mourners were Mr Arnold Congreve (brother) Mr Bill (cousin), Mr Geddes (cousin), Mr. Patrick Murray, Edinburgh, and Mr J Faulkner Armitage, Altrinohatn. .</p>
        <p>Otheis preaent at the service were :—Manchester Ship Canal Company: Alderman Wilson, Alderman Plummer and Mr J 1) Williams, direstors ; Mr E Latimer, general superintendent; Mr H M Gibson, chief traffic , superintendent ; Mr John C Willson, superintendent ; Mr F B Greenwood and Mr W Johnson, principal assistants to chief engineer; Mr NV Fox, mechanical engineer; Mr J Kershaw, Mr 11 Kershaw, Mr AI J Riley, solicitors ; Mr J D Weill., Mr D Dunlop, land agents; Mr Cl Leader Williams, Mr M B Lewis, resident engineers ; Mr F A Eyre, secretary ; Mr II Leycester Hunter, representing Mr W H Hunter, consulting engineer, formerly chief engineer ; Mr J Oldfleld and Mr John Leigh, and a large number of the Uri from all departments. Representing the Reform Club, Manchester, and</p>
        <p>Bu ildi ng Committee.—Messrs J Wilk inaoo, Tice- president ; J C Clanahon, chairman ; Sam Luke, treasurer ; J Harold France, member of the committee; and F Mimes, secretary ; .1 Arnold Thompson. Representing "95" Club.—Mr F Willett, secretary ; Mr (I Birdsall.</p>
        <p>Representing Manchester Association of Students of the Institution of Civil Engineers.—Mr Arthur C Demo, Whalley itnnge. R.-presenting Knutaford Division Liberal Association —Messrs Alfred J King, late member for the Enutsford DIViBiOP ; Wet Bell, vice-president • J A Thompson, J Splitli, lute secretary nod agent ; If 'Cliff°, agent. Repreventing the Church of Humanity, Liverpool, Mr E Rathbone and Mr John Fraser.</p>
        <p>Representing Manchester Chamber of Commerce.— Mr W Speakman, secretary. The funeral arrangements were carried cut by Mr I sac Lawton, of Moore, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>AMONG THE ESPERANTISTS.</head>
        <p>AMONG THE ESPERANTISTS.</p>
        <p>By " Arthan " B.A Oxon.</p>
        <p>In the beginning the accents and cadence of one universal tongue echoed along the secluded, shady paths beside the waters of the four streams of the nursery of the human race. Holding undisputed sway, untroubled by the shadow of a rival it con- tinued to serve as the common idiom of Sethites and Cainites though all vicissitudes, found a refuge in the Ark when that tremendous cataclyson deleted all vestiges of the primeval world, and survived all perils until that ill-fated project of some blindingly brilliant brain to scale the heavens was put into execution,wben —strange noises and unintellagible sounds asaniled the ears of the c ntractors and their men, and the languages of the earth were born. This was the tower of Babel where the Tongue of Eden hitherto pure and undefi'ed was corrupted and debased beyond all rec ,gnition into the innumerable dialects of the nations, who, faithfully perpetuating their disastrous origin, have gone on '' Babeling " ever since ! " All the world's a [label"</p>
        <p>The " blab:ding " state of the world is a present fact and undeniable. The original brood of Babel, traditionally computed at 70, were endowed with a remarkable fissiparous tendency and perpetuated their species with such regularity and consistency that the original 70 in process of time swelled Into many hundreds. But as the glimmerings of civilisation grew to grey dawn, passing thence to clear morning light, and as human Intercourse overflowed the limits of the family and tribe, the barrier diversity of speech has to be negotiated, and the multi-lingual problem presented itself for solution. But throughout the centuries, none but partial, palliative and temporary solutions have been found. The accents of an unknown tongue inevitably and automatically aroused bitter hatred, hostility and contempt, and the folk who spoke the unknown tongue were "babblers" and "jabberers" —" barbarians " as the Greeks and ROMBDS dubbed them, "Welsh " as the Teutons of the swamps nick- named them. The ancient and the modern world with but few dissentients, would paraphrase Shakespeare with eonsiderable conviction.—</p>
        <p>" II the world 'a a Babel, And all the men and women merely babblers ! BABEL AND PENTECOST.</p>
        <p>In the absence of systematic effort, mankind can always fall back upon the hoary policy of muddling through. There have not been wanting utopian seers and dreamers in various ages and lands, who pictured a time when all races, tribes and nations would adopt and employ one common universally accepted idiom, but no one imagined that this state of concord could be founded outside the milleniurn. The remedy for the diversity of tongues was the same as for the diversity of states and politics, viz.. the subjugation by some one power of all other powers in the world, and the compulsory superimposition of the language of the imminent power upon the subjugated races, and in orthodox circles, this is the political philosophy of the twentieth century. And se, the world has seen the transisnt vogue of quasi-international lingos, mainly, not entirely, for commercial purposes Greek, Latin, Fiench, English, each hashed or is having its day. Each inevitably depends for its" universality" upon the political predominance of the people whose proper language it is and of that there can be, of course, no lasting guarantee. Political entities are subject to the universal laws of organisrue—they rise, culminate and fall ; hence there can be no per- manence about such quasi-international idioms as have hitherto been current ; at the best they can be but modish and temporary, sub- ject to all the fluctuations and vicissitudes, the incalculable changes and chances of mortal existence. Moreover, the language which because of the political power behind it, acquires some sort of a quasi-international character, is marred and maimed by Nemesis's law of compensation, which punishes aught that transgresses the bounds which it shall not pass. The Greek and Latin of universal diffusion were not the classical tongues still taught in our seminaries ; the very proeess of diffusion slew them, and the Latin of the Empire passed into the numerous dialects of the Romanic nations. It is only necessary to point to the same ,process in modern times as seen in " Lingwa Franca ' and " Pigeon-English." If this contention is valid, we are driven to resort to the expedient of an artificial neutral language, adaptable to all the many-sided interests and diverse needs of the whole human race. The only quasi- artificial idism that has enjoyed any sort of perman- ence end vogue is the Latin of the Middle Ages. But in the nature of things this could only be the monopoly of the learned and leisured, while the upheaval of the Renaissance and the universal diffusion of the modern spirit of nationalism have ruined for ever the prospects of Latin as the future common tongue of mankind. At beet, therefore, the solution of the linguistic problem, has been unsystematic, adventitious end haphazard; partial, provincial and impermanent, and carrying in itself the seed of its own nullification and supersession. The linguistie Pentecost of the whole race is a dream yet to be realised, an ideal yet to be compass( d... . . . . . .</p>
        <p>Anir ongst the dissolution of the mediieval linguistic hegemony, consequent upon the Renaissance and modern nationalism, many muds have essayed the task of devising a satisfactory instrument of inter- national intelligibility. Just as the beginnings of modern science were occupied in experiments upon the Philosopher's Stone and the Eliir of Life, so inventors of the 17th, 18th, and 19th centuries busied themselves with wide and impossible schemes, cumbrous, unwildly and bewildering. Indeed, one or two of the middle 19th century enjoyed some vogue and many votaries, but in the end, nought save confusion waited upon their banners. A WONDERFUL INVENTION. • - - - -</p>
        <p>But the laurels for the successful fashioner of a world-speech were waiting to adorn the brow of Dr. Yamenhof the man whose brilliant genius conceived the Esperanto system. The Grand Old Man of Esperantism was born in 1859 at Bjelostock in Poland, and after the usual gymnasium and university career, settled as an oculist in Warsaw. As early as 1878 he had thought out his language, and its birth was actually celebrated by him and his fellow-students. His system, however, was not given to the world until 1887, after a protracted testing, pruning and perfect- ing by its author. Its progress was at first very slow, as was only natural, for all novel ideas and ideals have to prove their fitness to survive by successful endur- ance of the struggle for existence, by braving the active hostility or blighting indifference of a dull world lumbering along in the deep rut of routine. But after a dozen years of anxious tending and battling, toe issue was decided, and during the last dozen years it baa waxed and thriven txceedingly, outstripping the hopes of its most sanguine espousers. Its spread in this country is but a sample of what is going on throughout the world.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>ESPERANTO IN PRACTICE.</head>
        <p>ESPERANTO IN PRACTICE.</p>
        <p>By 1905, the Auxiliary Neutral Language—to give it its full title—had made such rapid sti ides and had penetrated so many countries, that it was felt the time bad come to hold an International Congress. Hitherto, Esperantohad been mainly a written idiom, an inter- national correspondence code, the eye wan familiar with its symbols, but, except at isolated scattered groups, its sounds were foreign to the ear. The supreme test was now to be applied, men drawn from the mist diverse and widely-separated nations were to assemble togethet to converse and to transact business in the common idiom learned by them it seclusion and isolation. The result can only be com- pared to the Pentecostal unilingual phenonierion or to be miraculous argument of the translators of the Septuagint. Conversation, notices, business meetings, technical discussions, public speeches and divine services. Esperanto was serviceable at every point, and yielded to all demands. Thus did Esperanto issue triumphantly from the ordeal of its first International Congress at Boulogne-sur-Mer, demonstrating to the world the practicability of its system, the utility of its services and the genuisenewe of it, claims.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>AT ANTWERP.</head>
        <p>AT ANTWERP.</p>
        <p>L Since 1905, International Congresses have been soceeesirely held at Genera, Cambridge, Dresden, Barcelona, and Washington. Each year, the congress</p>
        <p>swells in numbers and increases in interest, imprtance and enthusiasm. In addition to the world-gatherings above mentioned, national c inferences are held annually, as, for example, in England,the Esperantiste of which assembled this year at Southport to the ' number of 600,0 r Scotland,whose conference occurred at Dundee.</p>
        <p>Antwerp was the venue for this year'slnternational gathering. The official proceedings lasted for eight days, and included divine services (Roman Catholic, Protestant and Anglican) ; general speeches and dis- cussions ; sectional sessions (medical, scientific, religious, Pedagogic, Socialist) ; theatrical plays ; concerts and entertainments and the exceedingly important conference of the Universal Esperanto Association, whose ramifications are world-wide. This last-named body is the active exponent :of Esperanto on its practical and utilitarian side, and its importance and influence cannot easily be over-estimated. It has i organised bureaus for supplying information relative to commerce, t airing, labour, study, Ac., Ac., and has established for this purpose its agencies and consuls in 872 centres scattered over 44 countries.</p>
        <p>The total number of Congresststs including local sympathisers—exceeded 2,0(6) drawn from over 40 dill, rent nationalities. Some 27 g,vernments and commercial and profetrional bodies had dispatched official representatives to investigate the possibilities of Esperanto and to report upon its practicability. Over 600 congressists hailed from the British Isles, 104 of whom were from " bonnie Scotland" while there were found 10 Welsh speaking Esperantists to stand up for "Kimrujo " " Gwlad y Gan " at the gathering of the nations. It was wonderful the ease with which one entered —glided, as it were --into conversation with men and women of various nationalities. I myself conversed freely with people belonging to 10 non-English- speaking nations. And as this intercourse proceeded from day to day, in train and restaurant, on ship and street, one realised with emotion that the Chinese wall dividing the nations was no longer insurmount- able, and that the hitherto impassable gulf had been bridged by one of the greatest coestructive inven- tions of the nineteenth century, capable of infinite application to the solution of the problems which beset the toilsome path of humanity to fraternity, peace and concord.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>HOCKEY,</head>
        <p>HOCKEY,</p>
        <p>scarcity of players a match has been arranged with Manchester Y.M.C.A. Ist.. The Latchford team is:—A Hill, goal; F AlJanson, F Tole, hacks; A Starkey, J B Wilkinson, A Knight, halves; R Dale, J Pearson, D Travis, H Parker, E Banks, forwards; referee, Mr Frank Grounds. Bully-off 2.45 p.m. The second eleven v. Northern "B" at Crosby is :—II Wright, goal; T Dixon, R Parkinson, backs; A E Bradshaw, F W Isherwood, It Walsh, halves; H Fletcher, H Harding, W Fletcher, W H Minards, H Colton, forwards; referee, Mr W Eccles. Train from Central Station 1.50 p.m. • ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</p>
