      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>LATCHFORD CLUB.</head>
        <p>THE LATCHFORD CLUB.</p>
        <p>The First Eleven won at Clayton last Saturday by 5-2. The ground, unfortunately, was in a poor con- dition, and the bell was moat difficult to control, the result being that the hockey was of a spasmodic order. Latchford led at the interval by 3-1 and then increased their land. Clayton got through again from a scramble near the goal mouth, but another goal came to the visitors which made victory certain. Travis scored four goals for the second week in succession, having some good passes served up by the other forwards. Wilkinson end Tole did well in the hack division, Parker scoring the remaining goal. The Second Eleven met Bowdon " B " at Latchforl sod owing to Bowdon sending a very strong teem down it took the home team all thrir time to Ws. Latchford posaessed a 2-1 lead at half time, Minards sod Harding baying scored. Then Bowdon equalised, but the same two players gained a lead of 4-2. The visitors played up otroogly and got their third goal. [hey also put on much pregame for soother to make • draw but the home defence held out end a tight game ended in favour of Latchford by 4-3. 'rhe Bowdon wing forwards were very speedy and clever and their centre-half played a floe gams. Pickles was good at back for Latchford. The first eleven should have played St. Margaret's this afternoon but as that club has diabended owing to</p>
        <p>, , 4 .....- . _ People who say they don't like cocoa 4' have probably never tasted the tßowntree Flavour MAKERS TO H.M. THE KING. It is delicious! ...-4, : ..------------- , —....—..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>"Tantalising Tommy."</head>
        <p>"Tantalising Tommy."</p>
        <p>One of the smartest and beat-played comedies seen at Warrington for a long time is " Tantalising Tommy," now occupying the stage at the Royal Court Theatre. It has had a great run in London, the 254th performance only being concluded at the Playhouse a year ago ; and the provincial tour looks like being equally successful. " Tantalising Tommy " is the creation of Paul Gavault and Michael Morton, and a winsome damsel she is. The story of her adventures is brief, but bright and breezy. We are first of all introduced to Messrs. Jimmy Cottenham (of the Colonial Office) and Harry Killick (artist), as occupiers of a very rural cottage in the New Forest. Boredom has so far made its appearance that the twain are driven to playing bridge with the maid-of.all-work, and the evening has almost come to a dreary conclusion when "Tommy," the daughter of the wealthy Thomas 11. Pepper, tea merchant, appears suddenly on the scene. 'The bang of a bursting motor-tyre is heard without, and a moment later the heroine and her chauffeur enter sans reremonie. Our two heroes have just retired, and re-appear in various stages of dishabille, Jimmy to expostulate with the intruder in mild indignation, and Harry to encourage her to stay by all the means in his power. One very successful device of his is to surreptitiously puncture another tyre, and eventually " Tommy " insists on staying. She commandeers" Jimmy's" bedroom and his best suit of pyjamas, in which latter she makes a fleeting appearance downstairs to view her unwilling host sleeping uncomfortably in an ancient arm-chair. More might be made of the pyjama episode, and the play-bills suggest that the young lady's appearance in unconvential garb is a feature of importance. Evidently the modesty of the playwright has overcome his dramatic instincts. The following day," Tommy "is still an uninvited guest, when Jimmy's future wife aad father-in-law appear on the scene. Complications of the most laughable character ensue, and not only is Jimmy's engagement broken off,but " Tommy" sees fit to give the immacu- late Lord Enderby his congrf at the same time. Then there ensues the brilliant comedy of Jimmy endeavour- ing to " freeze off "the tantalising "Tommy " and Harry egging the young lady on. The denouement is perhaps a little tame, but it accords well with the perverse character of the heroine, and comes within the bounds of the acceptable happy ending. One has nothing but the highest praise for the artistically complete pourtrayal of the immaculate, effeminate but likeable " Jimmy " by Mr. Vivian Gilbert. He never over-does the sweet simplicity of his role in the slightest, and shows a sense of humour as refined as it is infectious. Mies Mary Forrester makes an admirable" Tommy," vivacious, wilful, and tantalising to the last degree, leading everybody, her father included, a very merry dance. Mr. Arden° Linden does the "heavy " father very well</p>
        <p>and subscribes a full share of the humour of the piece. As Harry Killick, Mr Chas. Mammal displays considerable histrionic skill. His study of the irresponsible artist, living in "a blue haze,, in lifelike. Lord Enderby is another quaint conception of the invertebrate, super-polished nobleman, and is excellently pourtrayed by Mr. Valentine Glynn. Mr. Tatham, the one-time prospective father-imlaw, Jennings the chauffeur, and Bertha the country maid- servant, are all clever conceptions and do credit to Messrs. Arthur Keane, J. H. Willis, and Miss Mead Garth. In fact, there is not an inefficient member of the company, and the comedy is preduced in a style which does excellent credit to all concerned. We advise our readers to take a last opportunity to-night of a hearty laugh at "Tantalising Tommy.' ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>HEAD AND FACE MASS OF SORES.</head>
        <p>HEAD AND FACE MASS OF SORES.</p>
        <p>" About seven years ago, I got eczema and have had it nearly every winter, off and on, since then. My head and cars, nose, lips and chin were one mass of sores, and I was ashamed to let anyone see me. My head, ears and face broke out with scabs, and when I touched them the water ran from them. I could have torn the flesh off myself with scratching. " I read o' the Caticura treatment in the paper, and I thought I would try the Cuticura Soap and Ointment. I sent for some and after I had washed myself two or three times and applied the Cuticura Ointment, they eased the itching. I continued with the Cuticura Soap and Ointment for about three months, which I found cleared my skin wonderfully. I have suffered seven years and had tried ever so many things all to no advantage, until I tried the Cuticura Soap and „ Ointment, and was entirely cured. They made my skin soft and beautiful, everything being cleaned away. My skin is now quite clear, and there has been no indication of the sores coming hack. I shall be glad for any sufferer from skin trouble to hear of this, for Cuticura Soap and Ointment certainly cured me." (Signed) Thomas Barry, 10, Reservoir-street, Wardley, 'Durham, Jan. 13, 1911. Post-free, from F. Newbery and Sons, 27, Charterhouse Sq., London, samples of Cuticula Soap and Ointment, with 32-p. book on skin treatment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>Danger of Flannelette.</head>
        <p>The Danger of Flannelette.</p>
        <p>An inquest was held by Mr. F. A. Jones, the deputy coroner, at the Police Station on Faraday, touching the death of Thome t McCarthy, the three years and eleven months old son of !bee Ann McCarthy, 21, Philip-street, who died in the Infirmary from the effects of burns on Sault*. The evidence showed that on Saturday the husband went to work at 4 o'clock in the morning. A boat S o'clock the mother of the deceased went downstairs leaving the deceased • .. • .</p>
        <p>upetairs and taking another. boy down;ith her. Stirs</p>
        <p>made the kitchen 'tire and placed the fireguard around it. She afterwards went into the yard and • few minutes later heard aecreant. She immediately went into the kitchen and found the deceased with his nightshirt in flames which she extinguished. A woman named Alice Co. apoke as to talonq the child to the Infirmary, where it died the same night. The deceased was extensively burned about the body. In reply to the Coroner the mother of the deceased said that the deceased'. nightshirt was made of flannelette. She placed the fireguard around the fire before going into the yard but upon her return she noticed that it had bees pulled aside. The Coroner emphasised the danger of children wearing flanne!ette material. The jury returned a verdict of " Accidental Death."</p>
      </div>
