        <p>(Loud laughter.) 'no they took him into the family business, and after being there ten years he decided to go in for the Par. He sold the interest be bad in the businees, gifting angiciant for it to keep him during Ws studios rein. It one ambitii n that prompted him to do thnt ; he felt he ought to he doing something more then he wan doing. Having lived in a groat cif) and being the son of his father it was not unnatural that he turned to politico, not to the capitalist clots, but to the wierir g class of Birk placed, sod be was for many yearn the chairman of OD eDODDI,IIB political organisation Liman as the Birkenhead Workingmen's Conservative Aisochition. Ila learnt a lot about the condition if the working clarees,and he only mentioned those thir g•-. to show that in his life he had bad much that had tern hard and very much that bad been unpleasant, end he was gild to say now that he saw a happier and a Inighter life opening out More him. lie sow sod arprecinted the hard lot of the working classes of Englund, and for that reason he allowed DO man to contradict him when be claimed to be, what- ever his polities. a real democrat who believed in democracy from the bottom of his soul. (Applause). On a proper occasion they might disagree with the mode he was adopting to reach tho goal, tut he hoped that no one In Narrington would ever say that he nes not endeaveuring to reach a SOLUTION OF TIIE GREAT PROBLEMS OF OUR SOCIAL LIFE.</p>
        <p>When he came into Warrington as the Unionist candi- date he was only 31 years of age. He was very appre- hensive, but he was sufficient of an actor to hide isis f celings—he was very nervous. He made it his object to gain the confidence of the workingclasses of War- sington—not rely of the Unionist rarty—and the action of the Baker's Ur ion was the first intimation he had that be had W 35 his way into the purely non- political section of the Warrington public. Continuing, Mr. Smith referred to Trades Unionism and organisa- tion nod said that assuming for a moment— nd it was a sery wide assumption—that he represented the cai ifalista—the employers of labour—they musts.* th t it followed thut the moment the interests of the en ploys re were involved he must go on the side of the at. ployers. But could any man say that arbecever lie tare,, sts sf the eoploy era and employees clashed he hod ever rimmed on the side of the fence on si ieh tie en pliyets a ere ? lie tad always said that the vror: men's richt to strike vw s ti birthright, lien ant is, see Braille.° it sad it the views which be (Mr. Smith) held wire distasteful to employers—at ahateter mit i.e was g, irg to in his own man and or body else's. He knew that his views were not distateft.l loan ploy ei ISt</p>
