      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>MUSICAL SOCIETY'S "SAN TOY." Mr. Walford Lomax as Producer. SOME REHEARSAL IMPRESSIONS.</head>
        <p>THE MUSICAL SOCIETY'S "SAN TOY." Mr. Walford Lomax as Producer. SOME REHEARSAL IMPRESSIONS.</p>
        <p>It is now se many years since " San Toy" was performed in Wartington that something like a new generation will see and hear it for the first time at the performances which are to he given on Monday next and the following nights of the week. For this oppor- tunity of renes ing acquaintance with " San Toy" he older' glows-ups " are indebted to the opera section tf our local Musical Society, a body cf amateurs who can now lick back with pardonable pride up it seven years of nrilliant achie. vein, nts. kod privileged, as has Lees the writer, to attend the rehear-als of his, one of the most dtlightful of mulcal comedies, one is struck by the magnitude of the task which is t resented t , its producer. "Produc ion ' is, commit atively speaVng, a rnodisn term is theatrical meetet a It ttaiels for tint all- important work which is done by semebody is making a istage-performance a scene of real pulsating; lit.. A thing quite apact from the functions of coneuctor or n.us'cal director, strudel the piece happf n to c.d.' for -uch a pereon, the man she undertakes the dudes of pro inner must be in h,inself at nice a dri'l-s egeant, an art st and a dancing-master, and oily whes one comes t see the art in actual practice is it passible to realise all that it involves.</p>
        <p>The highly meritorious peiformances of '• Tom 1, Jones" by the Musical Saciety last year bring to mind the tremendous responsibilities attaching to the roach and stage-manager from the view-point ja.4 alluded to. Yet Mr. Walford Lomax, who carried members through that enterprise no brilliantly, has, in this year's " San Toy," a task if anything more difficult and onerous Clearly in Mr. Lomax's case the enthusiastic temperament counts for much. For. so far as can be seen at rehearsals, he positively revels i in the work, and years of experience n the producing of plays, operas, and musical comedies seem only to inspire him with greater zest for it. In pieces belonging to the last mentioned category it is evident that while there may be less dependence than in opera on the musical factor, there will probably be proportionately more importance attaching to the spoken word, the incident and the action. And so it is with "San Toy." Humour,— somewhat grim and sardonic in the person of that cunning old fox "Yen How,"—graceful and charming in the character of "San Toy,' with its occasional droll contretemps arising from the at times embarrass- ing masculine masquerade,—and bubbling at its lightest and funniest from the irrepressible and übiquitous "Li,"—here bulks very large, and it is evident that the art of the producer, in dealing with often altogether inexperienced amateurs in such parts, calls for a degree of tactfulness and diplomacy as indispensable to success vs is the intimate knowledge upon which alone teaching can been based. In attacking a new piece Mr. Lomax believes in the direct method of carefully explaining to the general crowd what the situate no, with their varying emotions, really mean. Thus if one is asked t put in a nutshell the secret of his popularity as a producer, a point on which he ever lays the strongest emphasis comes to mind. It is that, on the part of everyone, from the obscurest chorus member to the most prominent of the principals, there must be shown a real and live interest in the incident of the moment, however ti vial or seemingly unimportant that may be. Mr. Lomax's crowd is thus no unenlightened and unintelligent assemblage of objectless people. Every unit in it is given a share in what is going on. And thus every impulse of emotion and sentiment by one, arouses that sympathetic response on the part of all, which makes bra realism at once ideal and convincing. . .</p>
        <p>Mr. Lomax is singularly ['inmate in giZning early he °teem and goodwill of all who come in contact with him. With principals, as with the rank and file, he has the happy knack of making the most of</p>
        <p>personal potentialities which otherwise might retra'n " mute and inglorious." There is but one episode in " San Toy" in which feeling rises to a height worthy to be termed dramatic. It (011E9 the climax of the finale to Act I. and the manner of its presentment next week will, in the light of whit has been said, serve to point the moral of these notes with those readers of them who go to see the play.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>PROMISE OF " SAN TOY "</head>
        <p>THE PROMISE OF " SAN TOY "</p>
        <p>A CHAT WITH MR. WALFORD LOPIIAX.</p>
        <p>It goes without saying that the dramatic and spectacular sides of "San Toy" are perfectly safe in the hands of Mr. It. Walford tmax, whose wide expo hence cf the interesting but arduma work ok amateur operatic productioa, combined with a tactful tud tit termined personality, m tke him an ideal " coach" and stage mat ager. A brief chat with our representative was mammal h disclose Mr. LOlllad'S nab unded enthusiasm for the present enterprise and hi a complete confidence ia the ability of the opera company to give adequate and successfal stage pen f rmances of " San Toy.: _ . .</p>
        <p>• By way of prelitni.aary,Mr. Lomax mentioned some ..f the ont,Lind.ng features of the work in hand. "From the rollicking humours of old England which we gave the Warrington public last year in ' Tom Jones'" he said" it is indeed a great trans- formation to' far Cathay.' Instead of patches, powder and old brocade, we have the pig tails and gorgeous robes of the East. I may say that the costumes are being supplied by Messrs. B. J. Simmons and Co., of London, who made the costumes for the original production of' San Toy 'at Dely's Theatre, London, and each one will be an exact replica of that worn by the corresponding artiste in the metropolis." " I suppose we may expect some striking and even brilliant stage-pictures in " San Toy "? "Yes, we shall have some quite delightful characteristic and glowing scenery. The first act will be a particularly fine and elaborate set, representing a street scene in a Chinese town. On one side is the British Consulate, on the other side a large emporium for the sale of objects of art, fans, and crockery-ware such as are likely to be seen in any Chinese bazaar. There are endless quaint old gables,built-out balconies, turrets and the usual picturesque architectural devices of the country in abundance. The sceue is peopled with the inhabitants of the town, rice-sellers, fortune-tellers, the local barber at work, etc. The whole makes a particularly characteristic grouping, the people having all assembled with the object of celebrating the annual Feast of the Full Moon. After many humorous incidents and charming songs, we come to the actual celebration, when, amidst the twinkling of many-coloured lanterns, the c. lestiala pay their homage to the bright goddess of the sky. chits, I think, will be the chief spectacle of Act I, and it is immediately followed by a spirited and bustling finale. Act 11. brings us to the Palace of the Yellow Dragon at Pekin. This is a brilliantly coloured acene of the red, yellow and green so beloved of the Orientals, with many curious arches and inglea. Set after net of moat beautiful costumes will be shown in this act, opening with the Mandarins and high officers of State, the Emperor and his wives, and the Guard—all of whom will be resplendent in gorgeous eastern garments." "Then the spectacular side of' San Toy 'should be as effective as anything which the previous productions of the Musical Society opera section have given us ?" "Well, to compare it with the one opera for which I have been responsible, I am hopeful that in regard to stage pictures, scenery and general effects, the performances of 'Tom Jones,' which won such general admiration last year, will be eclipsed by' San Toy." "And as to the mw.ic of 'San Toy ' ? "Me, Crossley, our talented conductor will have something to say on that point. I am afraid however, that some of our company who thought it</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>Costuntes by B. J. Simmons and Co., London, and W. Hodgkinson, Ltd., Warrington.</head>
        <p>Costuntes by B. J. Simmons and Co., London, and W. Hodgkinson, Ltd., Warrington.</p>
        <p>too cheap, found out their mistake when they came to rehearas it. I consider all musia is good if it is the best of its kind. Non', as light onerette, ' San Toy ' in undoubtedly the best of its class. Its I melodies are light, bright, catchy and ft 3wing. There is no greater devotee of high-class music than myself. lam an out-and-out Wagnerite. His music-dramas I regard as the highest form of art. To see one of his operas is a shocr joy to me. I say ' see' because the acting and staging are just as important as the music. I I tackled them as every one should—read every avail- able book on them and, furthermore, made myself acquainted with the sources of his inspiration—German mythology Ind the Northern Sag ,s. But when I lived in London, Wagner's works, as operas, were very seldom played, and we had to content ourselves with detached numbers frem them at the orchestral concerts at the .Queen's Hall. Good as they were, a night of slices of Wagner is rather trying, and I must confess that at half-time I went down to the London Pavilion to hear Dan Leno, just to restore my mental balance. Now that is just what we are trying to do with our performances ef ' San Toy.' I came over in the spring and heard your excellent Musical Society give a more than creditable renderiug of Berlioz's ' Fau.,t.' The strenuous work the society did on that occasion was beyond all praise. Now we have the light, joyous operetta, into which they are throwing themselves heart and soul, just to rest re their mental balance —if I may be yardoned for thus expressing it —and by way of providing a light, bright and pleasant entertainment for the people." " And what is your opinion of the principals engaged in the present enterprise ? " " I think we have a very excellent cast, all the members being well adapted to the work required of them. I may say that the work involved in a musical comedy like this is far more arduous than in an ordinary comic opera, and if the society can give a first-class performance of • San Toy' I should imagine they will be able to play almost anything." 'ln what respects do you consider 'San Toy ' is exceptionally difficult ?" " rhe dialogue is spoken more briskly, each song is more or less accompanied by a dance, and the music is, if I may say so. more tricky than in most works of the kind."</p>
        <p>" Would you single out any dances for special mention"</p>
        <p>"Li and Dudley have a Chinese umbrella dance in the first act which I think will be a great success, while their dance ' get-up, ' as quaint Chinese figures in the last act, should be equally pleasing. Yen How's six little wives, and the Emperor's six wives, also have some charming dances—to say nothing of a pan sea by !Vies Marjorie Dodds which will doubtless prove t popular feature." .</p>
        <p>Mr. I;imrax concluded with an ackncwledgment of the willing co-operation which he had experienced at the hands of all concerned in the work.</p>
        <p>"I need hardly say "he observed" that in this very big production Mr. Loader, the esteemed general manager of the theatre, has given me every assist...rice and is determined to make the week at the Ilylands- street house as comfortable for everybody as he possibly can. I hops the public of Warrington, and especially th-se who care for light, bright entertain- ment, such as ' San Toy ' affords, will give Ps their generous support, for it is solely with a view to pleasing them that the society is putting 03 this popular musical work. I may say that the last time I produced the operetta was at the Irving Thestre, Seac-mbe, which is about the same size as the Royal Court Theatre. Warrington, and in six performarces we played to the excellent amount of .000, /lOU of which was &amp;ear profit. '&amp;s' Toy' will certainly belie its universal popularity if wa do not have an excellent and profitable week here."</p>
        <p>Geisha" as the pioneer in a long train of similar works which have brought that place of amusement into great popularity. The music has the faults as well as the merits of its class. While it is often light, sparkling and vivacious —and many of the tunes are of a particularly melodious and ' catchy " character,—it is always disappointingly lacking in depth and any pretensions to grandeur. Although entitled a Chinese musical comedy no attempt is made by Mr. Sidney Jones the composer to attain local colour. A melody built on the pentatonic male as a device for securing an oriental flavour finds no place here, despite Sullivan's illustrious precedent in " The Mikado. ' It is, perhaps, in the nature of things, that in musical comedy there is little !coherence amongst the different numbers. On the I other hand there is, in " San Toy," at least, a truly wondeiful array of fresh rhy;hms, and a brilliance in the treatment of its ear-catching melolies and dance themes which lulls one's critical sense in regard to many of its shortenings. Some of the ensembles are very effective—the Moon scene being particularly striking—the music, lighting and scenery being blended in this number in such a manner as to ensure a most effective stage-picture. Li's opening an " The Mindarin," cast in the miuor key, with its curiously elongated phrases on some drone notes, has a quaintness about it peculiarly in keeping with the words, and the marked characteristics of this song are paralleled further on in the fine chorus for men's voices " We're the cream of courtly creatures," these two numbers, along with the music of the Pan Seal, making a welcome break in the surfeiting and rather dreary level of jingling tunes which so persistently haunt the memory. 'lle solo dance-tuusic is divided into three sections, a gavotte measure, a value theme, and a galop subject all—especially the fist two—of a most graceful character, and, occurring in the order given, they quite naturally lend themselves to an appropriate climax. Dudley has two sings which are sure to take well with the audience," A Lady's maid," in connec- tion with which she has much droll "business" with Li, and the other, " Rhoda and her Pagoda," in which occurs a choral tag of more than ordinary attractive- ness. This latter song is noteworthy as being one of those happy after-thoughts (there are many in t e score of "San Toy") for the introdoction of which d finituio provision can so easily be made in musical comedy. It is by Mr. Lionel Monckton a well- known composer of such items. Of " topical " songs there is one good example. It falls to the lot of Yen How and its refrain ending with "I mean to introduce it into China" has only to be mentioned to suggest endler extensions of its application, in indulging in which it is to be hoped the audience will be reasonably sparing. Tha duet "When you are wed to me, between Fo Hop and San Toy, calls for mention by reason of its presenting to the composer the only opportunity which occurs of writing music with a senoussmesning, and. though it cannot be regarded as reaching any high-water mark of dramatic intensity, it at least serves the useful purpose of forming a centre of interest, and of giving relief by contrast to the music that follows. Of songs in which graceful sentiment is the motif, Poppy has a specimen far and away superior to much that is placed in this category. "A posy from over the se "is a charming number with a ralne lento refrain peculiarly tender and sweet. One pore song, ardent and thorough-going, occurs in the part of Captain Bobbie, and the mere mention of its title, " Love has come from Lotns-land," will doubtless be sufficient to commend it to the audience. There are, moreover, two love-duets, both between Bibbie and San Toy, "The A.B C.," and "A little Chios maid," in each of which will be found points of novelty and compelling interest.</p>
        <p>Much hard work has been ..lane during rehearsals and members have evinced great keenest and enthusiasm. Some, who are taking principal parts for the first time, are expected to make a great rutomes. There are runny charming dances for groups of members in addition to an immense anwunt of fun and comic "business." Altogether high hopes are enter- tained that "San Toy "will run ita length in • meaner which will redound greatly to the musical society's credit.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
