      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>WORK OF Tar VAR.</head>
        <p>WORK OF Tar VAR.</p>
        <p>...„ „, The committee reported that the Superintendent, Miss Milne, and the minium e..stf had carried cut their work In a manner which bed earned the warm apprecis- .ii n of the patientsand their frienleand the committee added their testimony to a like effect. During the year 11,405 visits were paid to :45 cases. The Ladies' Committee reported that there had been some changes in the nursing staff, butt the ladies were most thankful to say that they still retsina( the services of their valuel Superintendent, Mies 51i Ine, also Miss Ellisyn and Mies May. Mies McLachlan and Mine Terry Ind left, and thsir places ba 1 been taken by Miss leech and Mina bikinis. It had been Lusts' necessary to mime a fifth nurse, and a candidate, Min lisle, came In July. Mrs. James Fairclough is the secretary of thieoommittee.</p>
        <p>Arbog JUNtrtINTIGNIDENT'S rernt bowe I 014 the cases trautforted from lest you minthered 61, end Shit the new oases -sera 481. The Du Om of •iai's paid WA 11,115. _ .</p>
        <p>Councillor Ronan,. presented the hon. treasurer's report ehioh showed that the amount received as ,annal subicripti no was slightly better than the previous year, via., £279 16s 61, increase £5 5. Cl. The .ongrenattonel collections were an aIiVIZCO of £5 88 61., th 3 total now no received being £3l IS. 81. rile durations from societien had increnned by over £lOO, and included £265. from the' Trilby " perform- once, £6i, from the Crosfleld Amateur Operatic Society, and £lO from the proceedn of an exhibition in Mnr, chister, paid over by Alderman Portent,. The nom raised by the Working Men's C ,mmittee was over afore, making a total of £307 6,7 d. Acting upon the ;nntructions of the commi tee, he bad invested a further sum of £2OO in Corporation bonds—making £lOl to inreeted. The balance forward was £;11 01 H. It wan very desirable that the number of annuil SUSEC•ibefe sb told he increased and to that and be ,00ld very earnestly ask the co-operation of all their ftiends. Ma thanked all the subscribers, donors, the Working WI'S Committee, and all others who had in any degree helped to maintain the present satinfactory financial condi' i on of this institufion.</p>
        <p>A VVORKIN(!PEOPLE.S</p>
        <p>The MAYOR said this was an increasingly tiesfal and beneficent isstitution to those of their itifortnnate fellow creatures who required its services—for the relief of those who were not curable—in the soothing of pain, and in assisting the cure of those who could be cured. There could not be any higher aim than that for which the institution was formed. (Hear, heer.l Skilled nursing was the real hr mimaid of medicine and surgery, the valued colleagne of the physician and the surgeon, and there could be no aspiration so high, no aim no noble as those in•olved in skilled nursing. (Applause.) There were no institutions in their midst so worthy of their sympathy end sopport. (Applause). The reports which bad been submitted to rthe meeting showed how well the institution was adminlitered. Its itsefuleess was mainly in the class of Oases treated— just the elm as a rule, that required skilled nursing. Then they bad to consider the people .sho were benefited. Each case should be reorim- mended by a medical men, end he found that there were only Il out of 500 odd crises treated during the year sibich were not no rec immended. (Hear, hrar.) Then there was the occupation of the person. The largest number of any given class related to bousewlves-232 or nearly tbreedlftbs of the entire cases—and that woulo appeal t 0 their commonseese at ono-s. If a mother of a family was, unfortunately, disabled by sickness there was no one to look after her. I good mother could always nurse a child, unless it was a case of exceptional difficulty, hilt when a mother was disabled,rhere was not the same likelihood of there leg anyone in the house to look after her. All benevolent institutions—hospitals and others—were apt to be abused, and in that Onee AA much ba.m MLR sore as good, sometimes more hians ; but he was con- . . _</p>
        <p>einced that this association, was not only useful but admirably administered. (Applause.) Councillor ROBF.RTS (hon. tree/surer), said that financially the year bad been a very satisfactory one, and the fact that they had paid into the Exchequer practically £7OO, spoke volumes for the disposition of the public towards an institution of this kind which represented all parties and all absdes of religious and political opinion. (Applause) Councillor Roberts read a letter of congratulation from Mr. F. Monks, who, he said, was tbe gentlemen to whom they owed the inception of the Nursing Association. (Applause.) He bad also received a letter from Mr. T. S. Steel, the solicitor, acquainting him of a legacy to the Association of .Cl,OOO from Mr. William lease Gilman, who though not a native of this town, lived here for five years prior to his death in August. (Applanae.) They were all very thankful for the legacy and they most also thank %Ir. Steel for the kind things he must have said about the institution. (Applause.) He (the speaker) would invest the money in such a way that it would build op a sure income and put the institution beyond all possible reach of financial danger. (Applause). The MA YOU moved the adoption of the reports and statement of account' and</p>
        <p>Alderman Sat ETII URST, in aeconding, Raid they felt highly gratified with the reports the Hon. Treasurer had placed before them, and they were all pleased to hear of the legacy which glowed that the work of the association was appreciated. He also hoped that other people in Warrington would not forget the association. Councillor Roberta had done wonders since be took up the duties of the treasurership. (Applause). The reports were adopted. WORKINGMEN THANKED.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR moved a vote of thanks to the Working- men's Committee for their splendid work daring the past year. They had done jeoman service. Though they got a good deal of benefit out of the Institution he thought they had a single minded desire In what they were doing, to do good (Applause). Mr. .J. If. SAIRTIIVRST seconded, and said he hoped the committee would do a still greater work, (Hear, hear).</p>
        <p>This motion was also agreed to. ELECTION OF OFFICERS.</p>
        <p>On the motion of the MAYOR, seconded by Mr. M. llorg, the President (the Right lion. Lord Lilford), the Vice-presidente, the Committee of Managyment, Zr.o. wet, elected for the ensuing twelve months. :THANKS.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR morel that the best thanks of the meet- ing be given to the Ladies Committee, to Mine Woe (the superintendent) and the names, and to the lion. Secretary and lion. Treasurer. Mr. J. ftvaten Vous° stnonded, and referring to the legacy remarked that it was not necessary for people to die before they could benefit that Association. (Laughter). The Rev. CANON WILLI I supported and this proposi- tion was carried. THE HON. SECRETARY AND HON. TUE t WARR Colonel Fairciough and Coutmillor Roberts were elected hon. secretary and bon tviaaurer respectively. Cordial praise was accorded to these gentlemen for their efforts.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>MAYOR.</head>
        <p>THE MAYOR.</p>
        <p>Alderman BURTOV moved a vote of thanks to the Mayor for presiding and for allowing the use of the Council Charnber for the meeting. Mr' ' JOtltt FA I R, LOC 0 /I eeconded.</p>
        <p>COUROMOr ROBERTS supported and said he could, as one who bad been Mayor end knew something of the work the offica involved, soy without the slightest hesitation, that Warringtoo had never bad a Mayor, In his time at any rate, who had exoesded in loyalty to duty the work of the present Mayor in everything which mode fist the beet intereets of the bsrough. (Applause.) The Mayor had been gracefully and ably assisted in his work by the Mayors's. (Renewed applause.) The motion was carried with enthusiasm. The MAYOR in reply said their kind references 0019- peosated one for all the hard work—and it was hard work—which had to be done by a Mayor during his year of office. It soothed ona's mind to hear such words, which were not Intended to flatter, although they exceeded one's deserts.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>PITH° Y'S SHOCKING DEATH.</head>
        <p>PITH° Y'S SHOCKING DEATH.</p>
        <p>John Roughley (13), a lastier•on, of Msrk-street Mosley Co onvin, flo iths.ovo, di si on Saturday as the result of an accident at the TyWesley Coal Compaay's pit. Some waggons got loose and raa violently dews the brow, god before Roughley could jump oat of the way, they knocked laiov claw... Assistance was speedily pr,voured, bat it was falai thst hi hod frit:tared Ms skull ald brlken his leg.</p>
        <p>moMMIENNIMI</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>• 00TISFILL i„...„„ ir A TilLeTI , .4,,,,,% f 4 1 i NOTES BY " MUST.91</head>
        <p>• 00TISFILL i„...„„ ir A TilLeTI , .4,,,,,% f 4 1 i NOTES BY " MUST.91</p>
        <p>Warrington football matters are probably not the most interesting at prrsent. I was at Widnes on Wednesday and witneasel the match which is likely to be the best remembered of the Colonial tour. When you g3t four men sent off for rough play in " a friendly " encounter with visitors from the other side of the world, there i 9 something exceptional about the match. Whether the Augralians or Widnes were really to blame in the first instance is an open 'location, but the match degenerated into one of the worst exhibitions of rough play and foul tactics it has been my lot to witness.</p>
        <p>Two men from each side got " marching orders," and if the officials had been strict to the end three or lour more would have followed, and most of them from the home side. As this game progressed, some of the Widnes players seemed to lose their heads, and in the last twenty minutes I saw two pliyers who are well known to all Lancashire enthusiasts indulge in deliberate kicking and striking which were ignored by both referee and touch judges. George Aspey showed adinirah'e restraint, and it in a pity some t,f the other players did not follow his example. In my view there was more deliberate fouling by the Widnes players than by the Colonials, but this was only evident after Holloway and Noon, Darmody and Lloyd, had been sent off for fighting. -- I should hesimte to describe the Colonials as a rough team, merely on the evidence of the Widnes match. They are vigorous certainly, and it seemed at times that weight and strength were used unfairly, though rot in a way which was against the rules. The lighter Widnes men certainly got knocked about by their bigger opponents, and when left far in the rear by the score they lost their temper. The worst feature of the whole untdessant affair was the conduct of the local partisans in the crowd. The language used all round the rails was blood curdling, and some of the epithets applied to the Colonial players would have made a hardened " Town's Ender" blush. The crowd had slot to do with inciting the players to roughnees, and the numerous p lice present were neglectful of their duty in not arresting some of the foul-mouthed hooligans who thronged the rails •</p>
        <p>So far as football is concerned, there was only one -team in it. The Colonials outclassed the Widnes men to an extent that no one could have imagined, when the prowess of the " Chemicals " on their own soil is remembered. Tue winners were bigger, speedier, and cleverer all round, and some of the pulsing was 'brilliant in the extreme. Twice when Whines were attacking, the Colonials broke away and scored, and twice they turned mid field blunders by Widnes to profitable account. In the end, the Colonials won by 4 goals, i tries to oil—a convincing demonstration of their superiority.</p>
        <p>Apart from the physique of the men —few of the backs are any less than Lomas, and as difficult to tackle— the methods of the visitors are superior to our own as Northern Union Rugby players. The running and passing by both forwards and backs has been brought to a pitch of perfection never seen is Northern Union matches. It's real effectiveness lies in its variety, for unlike our own men the Colonial wings are rarely szorers. The ball may go out to the sings, bat it goes back to centre again, and neatly eveiy try is completed in the neighbourhood of the poets. Then there is the backing up of the man with the ball. Whenever he is tackled he has two or three colleagues to piss to. There is none a the breaking away with a brilliant run, only to see the effort wasted for want of someone to pass to. On a dry ground the visitors can teach us a lot, and in my opinion they are the finest combination of bmtballers which has visited the Northern Union. Perhaps wet weather and muddy grounds may level up mattershut at present Ido not see where the conquerors of the Colonials are to come from.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday next the Australians meet England at Fulham, London, and will probably win their second international, seeing that in beating Wales -they did what neither the "All Blacks" nor the Kangarocs were able to do. The Eaglish team has been selected as follows: Sharrock (Wigan), back; Batten (Hamlet), Lomas (Oldham), Wagstaffe (Huddersfield), and Kitchen (Huddersfield), three- gusrter backs; Smith (Ilanslet), and White (Oldham), half-tracks; J Clampitt (Broughton Rangers), Ward (Leeds), Jukes (Hanalei), Kershaw (Wakefield Trinity), Ramsdale (Wigan), and Avery (Oldham), forwards.</p>
        <p>And now. te -me to poor old Warrington. They are not tstting the Mersey on fire by any means, and crumpled up badly at Leeds last Saturday. Ihe fault was the same as in previous matches —weak back play and poor S 3 tring power. The forwards, notably Thomas, played a rate game, but there was nothing behind. Cusworth and Daintith were terribly weak, and it is obvious that the Club will do no stood until we have a couple of first-class centres. Oh where, oh where can they be —got from?</p>
        <p>The League table up to date ie as follows: For. Against. Per- P. W. L. D. G. T. P. G. T. P. ent'ge Wigan lialifax Hull K. ft— 6 5 1 0... 21 14 84... 5 3 19 83-33 , Broughton Hoddersfrld 7 5 1 1... 20 34 14'2... 8 12 52 78.57 Wakefield T. 6 4 1 1... 15 24 102... 6 5 27 75-00 Widnes Hunslet 6 4 2 0... 15 17 81... 9 4 30 66:66 St- Helens 6 4 2 0... 11 21 85... 7 8 38 66416 Keigbley Oldham 7 4 3 0... 13 25 101... 17 23 103 57 14 Warrington 7 4 3 0... 7 11 47... 11 15 67 57:14 Salford Rochdale II 6 3 3 0... 9 14 60... 8 19 73 50.00 -Dewsbury 6 3 3 0... 8 12 52.. 11 17 73 5000 Leeds 7 3 4 0... 9 19 75... 15 21 93 4285 Barrow 'York Hull ...... 3 5 0... 17 28 118... 13 15 71 37-50 Batley Leigh 6 2 4 0... 11 14 64... 11 14 64 33 33 Runcorn 6 1 4 1... 1 7 31... 11 16 70 2500 Coventry Swinton 6 1 5 0... 4 7 29... 11 12 58 16.66 Wendel, Ebbw Vale G 0 5 1... 7 7 35... 17 26 112 8-33 Bradford</p>
        <p>I am afraid public interest will wane if things do not improve, and much depends upon whether the Wirepullers can beat Broughton Rangers at Wilder- pool to-dsy. The issue in certainly doubtful, for the Rangers have been playing a strong gnme of late, and -the Warrington side does not inspire confidence. There is a heap of responsibility on the shoulders of Unsworth and Carter, who have to fill the centre positions, owing to the absence of Taylor through a bereavement.</p>
        <p>The team will be: Tilley. fall-back ; Woodburn, Unsworth, Carter and 13radilnw, threequartera ; "Brooks and Creevey, half back.; Thomas, Johnston, Shugars, Chesters, Arnold and Brozklehurst, forwards.</p>
        <p>Many members bad been living in hopes of seeing Papakura on the team to-day. He gave a startling display 14tt Sdurday for the " " team, and if be goes on training he should quickly prove of great 'service to Warrington at either full-back or centre. Ile has all the Calcnial ideas of running and passing, and mule more play at full-back than any of the backs in front of him. Personally, I don't think the team would have been any weaker if he had filled one of the centre positions to-day at home, though he is certainly not" fit" enrogh to keep the pace througohut a fits matelt.Seeiog the gate he drew last 'Saturday, it would have been worth while to have played on him the " team again to-day, rather than let him remain idle in London. Practice is just as essential as training to a man who Las been out of the game so long. --- The Lancashire Combination table up-to-date is as follows : Poiots _ _</p>
        <p>L D For flint Pls. Wigan 5 . 5 . 0 .. 0 ... 102 ... 3 ... 10 Warrington Widnes St Helena Salford Ro'dale Hornets 5 ... 3 ... 2 ... 0 ... 30 ... 44 ... 6 Wigan II igialleld 5 ... 3 ... 2 ... 0 ... 42 ... 14 ... Broughton R 5 . 2 . 2 . 1 .. 18 . 56 . 4 Leigh Shamrock 5 ... 2 3 ... 0 ... 25 ... 56 ... 4 Leigh -Oldham</p>
        <p>Runcorn 5 . 1 .3. 1 26 .St. 2 Swinton Pemberton— 4... 0... 3... 1 ... 0... 38... 1</p>
        <p>The " A" team to visit Broualaton is as follows- Jordan, fall-back; Shakeshaft, Clare, Critchley and Waywell. three-quarters; Daintith and Kenny, half- backs; Naylor, Harmer. Bradshaw, Trante,r, Skelhorn and P Boardman, forwards.</p>
        <p>Warrington's first County match was distinctly well patronised on Monday tvid the gate of £lO9 was more than I expectei Toe game itself was a huge dis- appointment, for the Lancashire men made the poorest show in their history, and were deservedly beaten by 23 pointa to 7 points. The Cumberland men played fine football of the old-f ishioned order—speedy dribbling, fast following up, and deadly tackling. Moot of the Lancashiie men were lazy, and only Sharrock and Brooks, of the backs, merited any praise. I could not detect a single forward who ployel up to Club form. Yet several bare been chosen for Wednesday's "inter- national." If the union executive had done their duty they would have picked men who were making reputations, and not those who were playing on them ' The following are the matches for tii-dav : - COLON!</p>
        <p>Australisians v St. Helena, at St. Helene. PIRK.IIIRE CUP. _</p>
        <p>Huddersfield v. Batley. Bull Kingston Rovers v. Wakefield Trinity. Brumley v. Dewsbury, Lreds v. Kieghley. THE NORTHERN LE kEtE. _ _</p>
