      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>Poor Display by Warrington.</head>
        <p>A Poor Display by Warrington.</p>
        <p>The Wirepullera went to Leeds on Saturday, and sustained a pretty severe beating on the Headingly ground. Changes had to be made In the team from the previous week, Unsworth and Daintith taking the places of Carter and Creevey. The Leeds side was strengthened by the return of " Bob" Ward from Bramley, and the inchnion of a Welsh international forward,. W. G. ' • '</p>
        <p>Evans. Fine weather favoured the feature, which is -; popular one with the Leeds public, and a gate of 8,000 assembled. Teams and 0103ials : Leeds—T E Thomas, back; J Sanders, F Sullivan. W Jarrinn, and le Burros, threeisarter backs ;It Ward and C E Haycox, half backs; F Webster, F W G Evans, W Harrison, W Ward, and. T Waptaff, forwards.</p>
        <p>Warrington—Tilley, back ; Bradshaw, Taylor, Urns- worth and Woodburn, threequirter-backs ; Brooks and Daintith. half banks ; Thomas, Johnston, Sting era, Cheaters, Arnold and Brockleheirst forwards. Referee : Mr. C. Platt, Swinton. The opening stages of the match saw Warringten aggressive, but owing to a mistake by Brooks, the Leeds men were the drat to get into an attacking position, but there was too much kicking by the backs on both sides, and the passing was often crude and erratic. Brooks frequently broke away sad gave Ws backs plenty of chances,and Bradshaw,Tayler,linsworth and Woodburn handled the ball cleverly, driving Leeds on the defensive. Thomas was called upon several times, and get through his work with credit, and he it was who at length turned defence into attack. lie followed ups kick, and being iJined by his forwards, a smart dribble took play close to the Warrington end. Jarman and Barron tried to penetrate the defence, but Tilley played a resouveeful game, and succerde i in driving his opponents back. After this openings were created by Bard on one side and Brooks on the other, but lack of finish on the part of the three-quarters led to their undoing tinsvvorth broke away and passed too soon. Leeds tad a glorious chance when Y. Harrison broke away sad p seed t Webster, lerlio transferred to Wagstaff, as there was practically actpee. course for the line. Sanders, however, took the ball off-side, and the chance was wasted. At half-time neither aids had scored.</p>
        <p>lathe second half Leeds obtained their Brat more after &amp;hurt five minutes play. A penalty kici WAS akarded them, and Ilaycox taking the shot well out brought off a fine goal. From this stage to the end the Leeds team played a vigoroua game. The forwards combined well in the scrummages, ani Ward at half back was indefatigable in his efforts to feed his backs. As a consequence, all the men played with more dash and combination. Ward secured from a scrummage near the Warrington goal-line, and cleverly eluding Brooks, seored a try. Tne centres after this began to pass the ball out to Barron, and their judgment was justified by this clever wing player, who ran in a couple of tries. Haycox was successful with one goal shot. To the end Leeds continued to play an energetic game, and they thoroughly deserved their victory. Result r—Leeds, 2 goals and 3 tries (13 points) ; Warrington, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>RUNCORN S FILL LOSING.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN S FILL LOSING.</p>
        <p>A fast, open game was witnessed st Leigh, with Runcorn and there were 4,000 spectators. Leigh were faster and heavier, and their combination was much superior to that of their opponents. After about ten minutes' play Leigh were penalised, and Richardson placed a goal. This roused Leigh, and a capital passing movement culmin- ated in Neville crossing at the corner' but Ganley failed at goal. At half-time Leigh lei by a try to a goal. On resuming Leigh immediately set up a fierce attack, and Neville picked up and rolled over the line, Gantry landing a goal. Gallop next forced his way over, no goal resulting. Atkinson drew the opposition and tbrew to Neville, who scored lais third try, but no goal was kicked. Ganley dropped a goal from a mark by Lee. Atkinson ran brilliantly through the Runcorn team,and secured the fifth try, Genie', failing. Result: Leigh 2 goals and 5 tries (19 points), Runcorn 1 goal (2 points). Teems and offiAals Leigh—M Bolewski, back ; It H Neville, R Atkinson, T H Johnson and H Ganley, three-quarter backs ; J Jolley and C Brown, half tacks ; H Marsh, D Davies, It Barton, A Lee, R Gallop, and J Lowe, forwards. Runcorn—Stevenson, back; Cawley, Shaw, Hushes and Deakin, three-quarter backs ; Lewis ald White, half backs ; A Richardson, Padbory, Williams, Massey, Donoghue and Peacock, forwards. Referee, Mr J W Whitley, Wainfield. Touch judges, Messrs Roscoe, Broughton, and Jones (Widnes). WIDNES V. ST. HELENS; A KEEN CONTEST. Played at Widnes, before 0,003 spectators. Early on the" Chemical." got on the attack, and a strong for- ward movement resulted in Sam Aspey scoring. Jones improved. It was not long before St. Helens became aggressive, and a combined movement by the backs led to Manchester naming an unimproved try. Swinton next scored for Widnes from a croas-kick by O'Gara, and Jones again improved, St. Helens were penalised, and Jones kicked another goal. Half-time :—Widoes, 3 goals and 2 tries (12 points); St. Helene, I try (3 points). St. Helens forsares showed improved form in the second half, and got possession very often. After Jones had pieced a fourth goal, Manchester scored at the corner, and Mavity ran in a third try under the posts. Turtill improved. Result :—Widnes, 4 goals and 2 tries (14 points) ; St. Ilelens, 1 goal and 3 tries (11 points). Teams and official. ; Widnes—Jones, back ; Laws, Lally, Reid and Swin- ton, three-quarter backs ; Noon and O'Gara, half backs ; S Aspey, G Airway, Lloyd, Rigby, McCarthy, and Johnson forwards. - . .</p>
        <p>St. Relen.s.—Turtill,beck ; Plan/neap. John Ackerley, Molyneux, and Manchester, three-quarter backs ; Tren• with and &amp;holes, half backs ; Briers, Mavity, P3pe, Lee, Butterfield, and Prescot, forwards. Referee, Mr A flestford, Broughton ; touch judges, Mann W 0 Johnson, Salford ; and .1 It Lawton, Oldham.</p>
      </div>
