      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>AUSTRALIANS AT WIDNES. ROZ'OII GIME-FOUR MEN SENF OFF.</head>
        <p>AUSTRALIANS AT WIDNES. ROZ'OII GIME-FOUR MEN SENF OFF.</p>
        <p>The visit of the Australasian team to Wines on Wednesday, wan pr 3daative of sensational happenings. Early on, the respec. iye packs of forwardt got at loggerheads, and there were same very unpleasant CMOs of at3;king and fouling before the match closed. The refer Mr. Hirst, of Dewsbuvv. had t a send Holloway and I. -mody (Australia), and Noon and Lloyd (Widnes) ! off th, eld. The spectator', of whom there were fully '5,000. also behaved in disgreceful fashion, and the ' match was na credit to the Union. As far as play is concerned the Colonials ware vastly superior to Widnes i and won by 4 goals, 5 tries (23 points) to nil. The fiat try was obtained by Gilbert inside twelve minutes, Hallett picking up after some fumbling by Dilly in mid-field and giving Gilbert the easiest of tries, Francis soalei Just afterwards Widnes made a raid, but a forward pass by Lally when hemmed in spoiled the effort. Then Broobam sco.ed, leaving the opposition behind after Noble had made the opening. 1 Francis goaled. This try resulted tram a bad kick by the home pask. Then O'Gara dropped at goal when his side were almast on the visitors' line but failed. Taus at the interval the Anstr damns led by ten points to Di!. In the first minute of the resumptioa McCue raced over after a swerving run by Darmody, and Francis goaled, while shortly after Courtney added another try as the result of same capital play on the part of V. Farnsworth and Francis. The hatter goaled again. Another incident, irrespective of the roughness, caused much hooting hereabouts, when Mr. Hurst mis- takenly penalized Reid for offside after he had inter- cepted spans ant had only Fraser to best. Just after - wards the Galloway and Noon incident occurred, and not many minutes later Lloyd and Darmody followed in their wake. Courtney added th 3 fifth try ten minutes from the end, Francis's shot at goal hitting the post. Staff,l t--</p>
        <p>oaled I i</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION COUNTIES CHAMPIONSHIP.</head>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION COUNTIES CHAMPIONSHIP.</p>
        <p>Lancashire Badly Beaten at Warrington</p>
        <p>For the first time in the history of the Warrington Club since they became affiliated with the Northern Colon they were favoured with a county match on Monday afternoon, the Wilderspool enclosure being the venue of the game with Cumberland. Locally the honour was much appreciated. There was one altera- tion in the Cumberland side, Taylor taking the place of Kitchen. The ground was in excellent condition, and the game was witnessed by about live thousand Spectators, 'reams : Lancashire—Sharrock (Wigan), back ; Miller (Wigan) Wilde (Broughton Rangers), Jenkins (Wigan) and Reid (Widnes) threennarter-backs ; Brooks (Warrington) and Thomas (Wigan), half-bucks: Wiostanley (Wigan), Rana/Wale (Wigan), Avery (Oldham), Logan (Barrow), Briers (St. Helens) and Aspey (Widnes), forwards. Cumberland—G Davidson (Broughton and Aspatria), back ; H M Hooch (Broughton Rangers and Aspatria), J Lomas (Oldham and Mamport), Taylor (Keigbley) and Treloar (Barrow and Millom), threequarter-backs ; W Dixon (Oldham and laftryport) and T Pries (Barrow and Millom), half-buck.; J Furguson ',Oldham and Brookland Rovers). J o.vens (Oldham and Seaton), J H Clampitt and R Clanipht (Broughton Rangers and , &amp;Minim), D Clark (Hirlderdeld and Brookland Rovers), I and W Ward (Leeds and Egremont), forwards. Referee, Mr. F Renton, ilunslet..</p>
        <p>Flom —the start Cumberland had matters nearly all their own way,and Laocashice never lookel like making a good fight of it, and were outplayed at almost every point. In the trat half tries were scored by Bosch, Trelosr (two), aid Ward, from one of which Ferguseon kicked a goal. The failure to follow up in the first half was still prominent in the Laocashire play in the second half; the Camberland men were altogether too nippy and fast for them. After a time, however,Lan 311- shire began to put more life into the game, and from a pass by Brooks, Thomas scored a try, which was con- verted. Cumberland again asserted thtdr superiority, and Bosch added two more tries after file piny by Lomas. Then Fergusson scorei a try, which Lomas converted. Near the close Thomas kicked a penalty goal for Lancashire, and following quickly upon this, Lomas .scored a try for Cumberland. . . _</p>
        <p>Final : Ceraberland, 2 goal, 6 tries (29 PolOth) Lancashire, 2 goals, ltry (7 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON " A " V. PEMBERTON-PAPAKCRA'S FINE DISPLAY.</p>
        <p>Warrington " A " handsomely defeated the s'rong Pemberton team on S durday at Wildarspool, the result being largely due to the clever and forceful play of Papakura, the Maori full back, who made his second appearance and proved that training bad greatly benefited hire. Early on be transferred play to the visitors q sorter with a powerful kick which found touch, but the Pemberton men were very energetic and rushed the btll to Papakura who threw off a tackler and punted clear in fine style. A floe run and pass by him gave Waywell a footing in the Pemberton half. Here Jordan punted across and Wa ywell fielding smartly raced round the posts, for Critchley to convert Half-time: Warrington, 5 points ; Pemberton, nil Turning round, Papakura defeated many attempts to invade the home qoarters and a strong rim down the open, in which the opposition were brushed aside in unceremonious manner by the Maori, was seconded by the forwards, Skelborn going over for a good try, which Critchley again convertel. The visitors played vigorous football and on one 03C481011 the players appeared to lose self-control and two players were ordered off, together with Naylor of Warrington. Later a great run by Pap tkura was applauded to tha echo and several of the Pemberten players were cleared in transit With a depleted force the visitors were htrd pressed until the close. Papakura continued to be the shining light in the home baclfs, and placing his side in a good position, Tranter scored from a rush. Near the close Dickenson put on the finishing touches to a capital display, and with Critchley placing a goal in both instances the final score read as follows :—Warrington 4 g ials, 4 tries (20 points), Pemberton oil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>MANCHESTER AND DISTRICT LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>MANCHESTER AND DISTRICT LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>RYLANDS' RECREATION v. SALFORD TRINITY,</p>
        <p>The Recreation achieved a substantial victoryover Salford Trinity on Saturday on the Gorsey-lane enclosure. Although the visitors had to play against stiff breeze during the initial stage they put up a bold show for • quarter of an hour. The home forwards. however, asserted themselves, and, following on a rush Mason scored. Soon after Plumptnn added another try, whim Leah converted. From this point the home team were chiefly aggressive and tw,) mare tries were scored prior to the interval, at which period Rylands' Recreation led by one goal four trim (14 points) to nil. In the second half the visitors were nearly always on the defensive, the home team adding 23 points. The visitors notched a penalty goal. Result : Rylands' Recreation, 31 points ; Salford Trinity, two points.</p>
      </div>
