      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>FROM LABOUR'S STANDPOINT. 1</head>
        <p>FROM LABOUR'S STANDPOINT. 1</p>
        <p>N.".,..-"..:,::. 1</p>
        <p>By Philip Snowden, II P. THE RELATIONS BETWEEN LIBERALISM AND LABOUR.</p>
        <p>The remarks made by Mr. Winston Churchill at Dundee with reference to the running of a Liberal candidate for that con- stituency in opposition to the sitting Labour member have given rise to a good deal of controversy. The only obvious construction that one can place upon Mr. Churchill's observations is that they constitute a threat to the Labour Party to the effect that if the Labour Party pursues the policy of attack- ing Liberal seats that reprisals will be made. I The Liberals appear to be particularly sore 'about Kilmarnock. Why that should be so it is difficult to understand. The result of that election demonstrated the overwhelm- ing strength of Liberalism in the constitu- ency, and this should have been welcomed by the Liberals.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>SCOTCH EXPERIENCE.</head>
        <p>SCOTCH EXPERIENCE.</p>
        <p>The Scottish Liberals have never given a welcome to the Labour party. There are three Labour members sitting for Scottish constituencies, and each one has had to fight his way into the House of Commons against the opposition of both Liberal and Unionist candidates. Mr. Winston Churchill appears to have spoken in ignorance of the fact that the sitting Labour member for Dundee beat a Liberal in 1906, and it was only when Labour had demonstrated that it was a power in the constituency that the official Liberal accepted the situation and allowed the Labour party to share in the representation of the borough. Precisely the same thing happened in Glasgow in the case of Mr. George Barnes. There are only three Labour members from Scotland, and, as has been pointed out, none is indebt&amp;l to Liberal generosity for his seat.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>RECOMPENSING FACTS.</head>
        <p>RECOMPENSING FACTS.</p>
        <p>- - --- There is no need to get into tempers in discussing this question. There are a few plain, and simple facts which it is well to bear in mind. The first is that the Labour Party has come to stay. However much the Liberals may resent a new party coming into competition with themselves, it is a development which cannot be prevented. The extension of the franchise made the rise of a workers' party a certainty sooner or later. This country cannot escape from world-wide tendencies. The three-party system is a fact in all the continental countries with Parliamentary government, and in our own Australian colonies. The resentment at the intervention of the Labour Party is foolish and childish. Not only can it not be I prevented, but it is not right that an attempt should be made to prevent it. The Labour Party has just as much right to put forward a candidate where it thinks fit as any other political organisation has. A political party is not a legally constituted organisation possessing a monoply, and a sole right to appeal for electoral support. LABOUR'S RIGHT. Not only is it impossible to prevent political competition, and to suppress the use of a worker's party, but it is not right to do so from another point of view. It is tyrannical to attempt to suppress the expres- sion of any political views. The essence of democratic government is that there shall be free choice of representatives, and that every school of thought shall have fair and adequate representation. For the Liberals to attempt to suppress the Labour Party is a violation of every principle for which ' Liberalism is supposed to stand. While ' denying in practice the right of Labour to representation, the Liberals in theory profess to favour Labour representation. They have just passed Payment of Members on the ground that it will help to make the return of Labour members easier. This Liberal Government has a Bill before Parliament at present which has passed its Second Reading which is to enable trade unions to organise and finance a political party of their own. What is the use of giving Payment of Members, and of passing the Osborne Bill, if the Labour Party is to be denied the right to try to increase its Parliamentary strength?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>FURTHER FACT.</head>
        <p>A FURTHER FACT.</p>
        <p>A further fact which may be mentioned is that Labour representation is praised by both Liberal and Unionist Statesmen. It would be interesting to collect and publish the eulogies upon the Labour members which were uttered during the debates on Payment of Members. The Liberals were most fulsome in praising the labour Party land in bearing their testimony to the value of Labour representation. Mr. Austen Chamberlain said that there was not one member in the House of Commons who would vote by ballot for the reduction of the Labour Party. If the Labour Party is really such a valuable addition to the House of Commons why should the Liberals be so unwilling to allow its number 3 to be increased? The last six years have shown that the Liberal government has nothing to I fear from the Labour Party when the govern- ment is engaged in promoting legislation of al democratic character. The Labour Party has exerted an influence for good in Liberal legislation, and every genuine Liberal who wants to see his party really progressive ought to be anxious to keep the Labour Party in a strong and influential position. A CERTAIN CONSEQUENCE.</p>
        <p>If the Liberals adopt the foolish advice of Mr. Churchill the result is perfectly obvious. If the Liberals oppose the return of all the sitting Labour members most of them will not be elected. But Liberals will not take their places. The Labour vote is strong enough in practically every constituency to turn the scale against a Liberal candidate. At the most the Labour Party can only lose some thirty seats, but the Liberals can lose a hundred and office also. This is the certain result of the adoption of such a policy as Mr. Churchill recommends to the Dundee Liberals. If the Liberals think that is a wise policy let them adopt it. They might at the same time remember that they are in a minority in the present Parliament—even with the help of the Labour votes in the constituencies.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>BETTER WAY.</head>
        <p>A BETTER WAY.</p>
        <p>There is a better way than this policy approved by Mr. Churchill. It is gratifying to see that one of the leading Liberal journals sees this better way. The Labour party will continue to fight elections, but neither it nor ony other party, surely, wants a man to be returned who does not represent the wishes of the majority. This situation should be faced by the Liberal Government, and they ought to pass a measure for the establish- ment of a system of transferable vote or pro- portional representation—the latter by preference. Proportional representation has been established in several of the continental Icountries, and it works well. The recent 'General Election in Sweden was carried through for the first time on this plan. The 'result is that the Socialists have doubled</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>LUCKY PEOPLE.</head>
        <p>LUCKY PEOPLE.</p>
        <p>Gocd Health Recovered Through the use of</p>
        <p>Vi-Cocoa.</p>
        <p>Since first we introduced Vi-Cocoa to the working people of Great Britain, many thousands of letters have been addressed to us telling of good hea'th lost and won. Invariably they have begun with stories of bird work, long hours, close confinement, or exposure to sevete weather. Invariably they have gone on t the health ttt 11:-31f sadt he ewflriTtt:rssuchAcno,ritd invariably, yve :La:, an concluded IT saying that good health hat been won back by the good influence of Vi-Cocoa. Lucky people ! We congratulate them, and assure them that the more Vi-Cocoa is drunk the more healthy men and women there will be. Dr. Tibbles* Vi-Cocoa is real nouriahmenr —not only an ideal beverage, but a healthful, invigorating, sustaining food. In addition to Cocoa, it consist% of three other ingredients—Kola, Malt, and Hops—all of which have a direct influence upon the health. Thera is no wawa whatever. _</p>
        <p>Give \ -Coca a trial. Never mind what you've been drinking at breakfast or supper in the past, start drinking Vi-Cocoa to fhty—you'll frel the benefit of it at once.</p>
