        <p>,</p>
        <p>I the difference. Every groew sells packets and 9d. end Is. M. ties. •</p>
        <p>their representation. If we had such a system in this country there is no doubt that the Labour party would be able to return more than a hundred members to the House of Commons. These members would be more independent than now, for they would not be dependent upon the votes of any other party for their return.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>HINT TO THE GOVERNMENT.</head>
        <p>A HINT TO THE GOVERNMENT.</p>
        <p>Proportional representation will give each party the number of members in proportion to its actual strength in the country. This is a fair thing, and one to which no party ought to object. The Trades Union Congress passed a resolution in favour of 'Proportional representation. As the Government are going to take up the question of electoral reform they might give us this system. This would put an end to the recrimination and compromise which are inevitable under the present system.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>ARREARS OF CHILD MAINTENANCE. --- Education Committee's Prosecution.</head>
        <p>ARREARS OF CHILD MAINTENANCE. --- Education Committee's Prosecution.</p>
        <p>A WARNING.</p>
        <p>, As a warning to parents who create a great deal of trouble to the Education Authority by refusing to contribute regulaily to the maintenance of their children in various institution., an interesting prosecution was brought before Mr. J. W. Hayseed and other justices at the Warrington Borough Court on Wednesday. -The defendant was Peter Curraboy, 56, Dallam-lane, who was represented by his wife, and who was summonel for neglecting ta contribute to the maintenancs of his daughter Ellen. in the Oxford-street school for Deaf and Dumb, Liverpool, on an order made by the Court ia June, 1909. Mr. 12. B. Roberts, assistant director, prosecuted on behalf of the Education Authority, and said that although they regretted the necessity of taking those proceedings, the action of the defendant in so per- sistently ignoring the order of the Court, had left them with no alternative. The order was made over two years ago by which defendant had to pay Gd. a week, the balance of 9c GI. for the maintenance of the chile being borne by the toes. The Atrhority had been very forbearing in the c ,se, having tried persuasion and threatenings, but all to no purp tr,. The wife was brought before the Court, when she made a promise to contribute is. a week until the arrears were cleared off. Def.mdant had not, lacwever, paid anything since the previous May and was now 10s. in arrear.,, representing 38 week's contributions. The defendant had been discharged from his employ- ment through drunkenness and if he would only throw . off that habit he would be in permanent work. The Authority asked that, filling the payment of arrears, the defendant should be committed to prison. , Detective Clough said that when he went to sone ' the summons he found the defendant in a drunken condition. Ile was brought to the Police Station but persisted in his refroal to accept the Puurnona, stating that he would neither take it from him (witness) nor fr m the Chief Constable, and that he was never going to pay anything towards the maintenanes of the child. Defendant was ordered by the Bench ether ti pay the money or go to goal for 11 days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>1.,,,,,,.,,,„,,:•2 CHILDREN'S CORNER, conducted by UNCLE CALEB. t. —i</head>
        <p>1.,,,,,,.,,,„,,:•2 CHILDREN'S CORNER, conducted by UNCLE CALEB. t. —i</p>
        <p>THIS WEEK'S STORY. MYSTERIOUS KNOWLEDGE.</p>
        <p>Some years ago an expedition from the University of Pennsylvania was sent to one of our southern states for the purpose of observing a solar eclipse. The day before the event one of the professors said to an old coloured man belonging to the household wherein the scientist was quartered: "Tom, if you will watch your chickens to-morrow morning you'll find that they'll go to roost at eleup o'clock." Tom was, of Merge, sceptical; but at the appointed hour the heavens were darkened and the chickens retired to roost. At this the man's amazement showed no bounds, and he sought out the scientist. "Professor," said he, "how long ago did you know dem chickens would go to roost ? " "About a year ago," said the professor smilingly. "Well, ef dat don't beat all !" was the man's comment. "Professor, a year cgo dem chickens wa'nt even hatched." HOW THEY KEEP BIRTHDAYS IN JAPAN.</p>
        <p>In Japan the children do not keep separate birthdays. Instead there is a festival for all the girls in the month of March, and for all the boys in May. If you were a little girl and lived there, your friends and relatives would give you presents in March, all on the same day; so would your girl cousins and the little girls you knew, and there would be big birthday parties going on everywhere. Then in May the boys would have their turn. Their's is observed more curiously and is an event of greater importance. Their's is called the " fish festival." hvery family that has a boy sets up a big staff, like a small flag staff, over the doorway of the house. On the top of the pole is a gilt ball, and flying underneath from the pole is a string of fishes made of oiled paper or cloth. This display is meant as a kind of parable or symbol. The golden ball signifies a treasure which the fish is supposed to be trying to reach. They have their heads upwards and as they flutter are supposed to be struggling for the golden ball. The mean- ing is that the boy will have to strive if he is ever to attain anything, and will have to battle his way through life as the fish swim against the tide in struggling up a river. It. is a sort of little lecture to the boys, urging them to be ambitious, earnest and persever- ing; and a very good lecture too.</p>
