      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>27/6</head>
        <p>27/6</p>
        <p>Exceptional</p>
        <p>FA ra. Air •</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY, OCTOBER 14, 1911</p>
        <p>The Trades Union and THE GOVERN- Labour Party at MENT AND Keighley, it seems, has LABOUR. decided to make a three- cornered contest for the seat wNch recently became vacant in that constituency. We must assume that the proceeding is one that has received full and careful consideration, which must have resulted in the conviction either that a Labour candidate is practically certain to win the seat, or that a third candidate cannot by any possibility carry away as many Liberal votes as would hand over the representation of Keighley to the party which is opposed equally to Liberalism and to Labour. We do not find it possible to believe that a third candidate is being put forward in a spirit of indifference as to whether the Conservatives win or lose for it is impossible to realise as existing among English working men -a state of mind whichy could regard, without something approaching to terror, the return to power of such a party as was commanded by Mr-1 BALFOUR previous to 1906. There may be those who think, with the prototype of ROBINSON CRUSOE, that it is better "to live in the midst of alarms" than under a Liberal Government, that costly and unnecessary wars are desirable, that Conscription is the only way of national safety, and that the taxation of food is the one means of benefiting those who complain that food is already too dear; but we do not expect to find these among the working men of Yorkshire. Those who assist even one more Tory Member into the House of Commons shoulder an immense responsi- bility—nothing less than the whole future of the country and the Empire, in fact—and, -therefore, we take it for granted that the Labour Party at Keighley is not acting either from anger or imperiousness ; but after full consideration of the case in all its aspects, and in full view of the possible consequences.</p>
        <p>Simultaneously with the news that it had been resolved to oppose both the Liberal and the Conservative candidate at Keighley there was disclosed to the public another branch of the great scheme of reorganisation by which the Government hopes to benefit labour, and, through labour the nation at large. It is using mild and inadequate language to say that no Government which ever existed has done, or even attempted, so much during its whole career, however long that might be, to improve the condition and the prospects of the millions who toil as has been accomplished by the existing Administration in a period of six strenuous years. The preposterous representation that it does not matter to labour whether a Liberal or a Tory Govern- men is in power has, to appropriate Mr. GLADSTONE'S famous phrase, been smashed, pulverised and destroyed. Under the Balfour regime trade unionism, as everybody must remember,was being placed under the harrow a perilous position from which it was rescued only by the Trade Disputes Act of the present Government. The foundations of the defen- sive position which labour has made for itself were giving way all round. The status quo has been re-established in that respect, at any rate and beyond this we have had important measures of practical and material benefit to working people alike in town and country, not one of which would have been passed had not the Liberal Party been in power.</p>
        <p>The Insurance Bill has become a certainty and now we have the great experimental proposal the object of which is as far as possible to put an end to strikes and lock-outs as a means of settling disputes as to hours and conditions of work, and rates of wages. To say that it is a matter of indifference to the working man whether a Liberal or a Conservative Gouernment is in power is equal to affirming that there is no difference between black and white or red and green. What may come of the new Industrial Council, must for the present of course, be matter of speculation; but certainly nothing but good can come of it, and there ought to be no opposition by any sort of Labour Party to a Government which has had the excellent idea of giving so important and far-reaching a scheme a fair trial. That the plan should already have become ripe enough for publication and for the appointment of representatives of capital and labour is one more among many proofs that Ministers have not made a holiday of the recess. The foremost of them, at any rate, have been working very hard, and, as we believe, with results that will loom large the industrial history of our country.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
