      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <p>We. are pleased to learn</p>
        <p>HONOUR FOR MR. that it is proposed to ROBERT PIERPOINT. confer the freedom of the Borough on Mr. Robert Pierpoint. If the Tory party has become wilfnfly indifferent to the claims of its one-time Parliamentary representative and champion, that is no reason why the town should not recognise the services which Mr. Pierpoint has rendered to the community during a long term of years. As Conserva- tive candidate we have had occasion to COM I ment on those sins of omission and commission which we deemed Mr. Pierpoint to be guilty of politically, but we never hesitated to acknow- ledge the intrinsic worth of his character, or the honesty of purpose which was evident throughout his career as a public man. The courtesies of party warfare were a cherished jewel during those strenuous years when Mr. Pierpoint represented War- rington, and ultimately gave way. to Mr. Crosfield, and we are inclined to think, that, coincident with Mr. Pierpoint's callous relegation to a sphere of inactivity, his party's methods lost some of that amenity which did much to exclude bitterness from our political contests. Mr. Pierpoint is to leave Warrington—and Warrington will be, distinCtly the poorer for his loss. But there, is consolation in the thought that as a cotn-' munity, and a borough of ancient lineage, we are not allowing him to leave unhonoured and unsung. Robert Pierpoint's name will worthily fill a place upon the scroll of Warrington worthies of this and by-gone generations.</p>
        <p>For some reason or LIBERAL WOMEN'S other, Warrington is: CONFERENCE, not often chosen as the venue of important, .conferences, and considerable pride and satis-, faction Wits felt by local Progressives at the holding of the Council meetings of the Lanca- shire and Cheshire Union of Women's Liberal Associations in our town on Wednesday. Delegates from all parts of the two counties discussed in business-like fashion a for-' midable agenda, embracing most of the urgent topics of the day, and the speeches throughout reached a remarkably high level which would have done credit to any gathering The days when it was customary to look askance at attempts by women to take an active part in political and public life, have happily, long since passed into obscurity. In this enlightened age few indeed would deny women the right to interest themselves in movements and questions which concern them quite as directly as wan. The 1 Insurance Bill is a case in point ‘and the feminine point of view on this far-reaching and complicated measure found adequate ex- pression at the conference. Particularly interesting were the addresses on Home Rule, an array of convincing arguments being put in concise and telling style. The local arrangements for the conference could not have been improved upon and the Warrington Women's Liberal Association's welcome tot-the visitors wits vbry haptlily and neatly expressed by Mrs. A. H. Crosfield.</p>
        <p>The National Service</p>
        <p>GILDING THE League is apparently CONSCRIPTION PILL going to emblazon social</p>
        <p>—refoi-m on its banner, and rescue work in the slums is to make conscription more attractive. Lord Roberts has issued a long manifesto setting forth the urgency of social reform, and explaining incidentally that the Unionists are the only party which can accomplish that great task. Good housing, good food, good air, good education for the dwellers in the slums form an admirable programme, but Lord Roberts is not a quite disinterested philanthropist. He is thinking of his recruits, and his social reform is only a preliminary for the establishment of a national Army. Evidently the National Service League (says the London correspondent of the " Liverpool Daily Post and Mercury ") finds that it is not making satisfactory headway, and as Tariff Reform has not helped the cause, social reform is to be enlisted. Lord Roberts. indeed, admits that tens of thousands of workers will have nothing to say to national service, and he ascribes that disinclination not to a reasoned belief that any such sacrifice is unnecessary, but to the conditions under which they work. Improve the lot of the working man, he says in effect, and he will cheerfully serve in the national Army. If Lord Roberts can do anything to improve social conditions, any success he may achieve will be welcome, but we may take leave to doubt whether social amelioration will bring conscription nearer.</p>
        <p>Harold _Smith,</p>
        <p>GORY POLMCS. M.P., is avowedly on</p>
        <p>of the Carson crowd of fire-eating, " die-in-the-last-ditch " poli- ticians, and as such we have little fear that he will make much impression upon a level- headed electorate, and particularly, if he continues to give expression to the kird of sentiments which fell from his lips at the Conservative Club last Friday evening. Mr. Smith gave us his views on Home Rule—and they were so carefully modelled on the wild utterances of Sir Edward Carson and " F.E." that the Member might have saved a lot of valuable time by referring his hearers to those more eminent politicians instead of echoing their remarks. Mr. Smith was candid enough to admit that "a majority in Ireland desired Home Rule" and he was also foolish enough to say in the next breath, "that there was another voice. . . . a smaller voice. . . . but it was the voice they would listen to —the voice of Ulster." According to Mr. Smith, the small minority, and not the majority, has to rule in this case. Why? Because Sir Edward Carson and "F. E." have said so But the member for War- rington was not content till he had gone the "whole hog 'S and committed himself knee- deep. The following is his dangerous, nay traitorous prediction. . .</p>
        <p>" . . If they thrust upon Lister a measure of Rome Bole . . . . the streets of Belfast and Londonderry would run blood as they had done before, and when the fight came the coneti- tuente of hie notice city noted crone the Channel by thoeteancle to take up acme On behalf of all that was best iu the Irish nation."</p>
        <p>The italics are ours but the words are those of the Member for Warrington, and we hope they will not be forgotten. Evidently if Mr. Smith and his brother had the leadership of "the constituents of his native city" we should soon be witnessing a "Jameson raid" from Liverpool. In that calamitous event it is conceivable that the Government would</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <p>deal more severely with the traitors than on the earliek:dovession. But, as Mr. Crosfield said on 'Marklay at Latchford, this preach- ing of rebellion to a minority is a dangerous :and, ddeploraiple , 'things Warrington must surely be accounted unfortunate in having elected a representative whose views are as subversive of law and order as those of a brigand or filibuster !</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>NOTES AND NOT)!Ili</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NOT)!Ili</p>
        <p>"The Poems of Luke Hamilton Talbot," arranged by Alderman Bennett, have just been published at 3s. 6d. We propose to give an extended notice of the work hext week. • • - The Rev. A. M. Mitchell told tiM Board of Guardians on Wednesday that he aver ate any breakfast—a statement which had-a very hollow sound and caused intagns4 qualms. • * .</p>
        <p>At the County Court on Thursday. Barrister to Irish witness: What did you do with the money you had ?—I sent it to my orphan children in Ireland. * •</p>
        <p>We should think our contemporary has reported every word that Mr. Harold Smith, M.P., has uttered of late—unless he has been talking in his sleep when, conceivably, the "Guardian" might be caught napping.</p>
        <p>Notwithstanding the evil prophecies of the Infirmary's fate when the Government Insurance Bill comes into force, it is doing pretty well at present, and the tide of good luck shows no signs of turning. In addition to the legacy of £3,000 just received, we learn that a legacy of £250 from the estate of another gentleman will shortly be forth- coming. * * *</p>
        <p>Mr. H. Ford, a Warringtonian now in New Zealand, sends us a letter which contains much interesting information on the lengths to which State Insurance, and other measures of social importance, have been carried in Newlealand. For instance, a contribution of 3s. a week by a lad of 16, ensures him ultimate benefits which should make the thrifty British workingman's mouth water. Then there is the Worker's Dwelling Act which enables a man to become the owner of a £5OO house on payment of £lO down, and £25 a year for twenty-six years and a half. And here in this country we should pay £3O a year rent in perpetuity for the same house, and not own a brick at the end. Truly, our colonies can teach the Old Country something in the way of legislation for the benefit of the people. * *</p>
        <p>The surprise benefactions to the Infirmary and Nursing Association have aroused con- siderable speculation as to the characteristics, employment, and full identity of the late Mr. Gilman. He was not known in life—at least to the townspeople generally—and the story of his practical interest in these beneficient organisations is rather unusual and interesting We learn that although a comparatively wealthy man, Mr. Gilman (who was a buyer for a Manchester firm of warehousemen), lived in Warrington and Altrincham for some years in extremely humble circumstances. He lodged with a small shopkeeper in Winwick-street, and practised the economies of an ordinary work- man. He spent little or no money on clothing, and his sole recreation each week was a visit to a picture palace, a ticket for admission to Which he obtained free from his landlady, who exhibited bills in her shop window. During his lifetime Mr. Gilman displayed no particular interest in charities of any description until a few years ago, when his landlady, during an illness, was attended by a nurse from the Altrincham Hospital. Gilman was then so impressed with the nurse's service and the information she gave him about the institution that he announced his intention of leaving what he termed a' trifle to the hospitals. In subsequent con- sultation with his solicitor, Mr. Steel of Warrington, the above legacies were decided upon.</p>
        <p>LOCAL PARS</p>
        <p>British and Foreign Bible Society. The Centenary Bible Sunday of the British and Foreign Bible Society will he celebrated on October 22nd, and a reat public meeting will be le-1.1 in the Parr Hall on Thursday evening, October 26th.</p>
        <p>Warrington Dramatic Society. The Warrington Dramatic Society announces that perforrcances of Mr. V. lismonds celebrated comedy • When we were twenty-one" will be given at the Royal Court Theatre on January Bth, 9th, and 10th. An exceptionally strong cast will include Mr. Harold Smith, M.P. Warrington Bridge.</p>
        <p>Last i7eek's interesting ceremony has been followed Ly further preliminary work on the site. The first two piles of the new bridge have now been driven right down into the river bed, only the heads being visible. The canstruction of a footway for pedestrians on the east side of the bridge will shortly be proceeded with.</p>
        <p>Mayor and Mayoress at Mansion House Ball. The Mayor and Mayoress (Councillor Dr. and Miss Joseph) were present at the ball given by the Lord Mayor of London at the Mansion House last evening. The Mayoress was very beautifully attired. An lunder-dress of shell pink satin, veiled with palest blue ninon, served as the foundation for a handsome tunic in pale blue beads. The bodice, made crosswise, was edged wi,h skunk far, as was also the hem of the tunic. The gown was made by Mesrrs. W. Hodgkinson, Ltd. Palace and Hippodrome.</p>
        <p>Vernon ‘Vatsi;-n-, impersonator, and Mendel, the marvellous blind pianist, jointly hold the premier place on the bill for next week. Other terms will be :—Edna Latonne, the well known male impersonator ' • Sergeant Brennan, world's champion diabolist; Fred Maitland &amp; Co., in' Roots, Rash Chi mist"; Julian ROB 4, light comedian and actor vocalist ; Doris Duce, a dainty little Eirl : and Eileen Hall, the p- polar Eoprano. Town Planning Lectures.</p>
        <p>A course of three lectures on " Town Planning in practice" will be delivered by Mr. Henry It Aldridge (secretary of the National Housing and Town Plannin i g Council), n Parr Hall on Tuesdoys, October 17th and , 21th, and November 7th. The first lecture, on Tuesday next, will deal with the methods of town planning, from the days of ancient and medieval cities. The Mayor will preside. 'lb. lecture and lantern exhibition of plans and pictures will occupy an hour. Half-an-hour will then be devoted to the answering of questions. Admission will be free, and tickets can be obtained from the Town Clerk. Crosfield's Operatic Society • . .</p>
        <p>The members of the Crosfield 'Operatic Society have recently decided uptio their opera for the present season. Anxious to follow up their spl.ndid success in 0 Les Cloches de Corner'.lle," they had some diffi- culty in selecting an opera which would be bath comic and classical. However. they have nov chosen The Mountebanks"— a tuneful comic opera, with ltbretto by the late Sir W. S. Gilbert and music by Sellier, of " Dorothy" fame. 'This year Dr. Law, who has such a great Shakespearean reputation, is embarting upon the production of light opera, and has very kindly agreed to superintend the rehearsals of Messrs. Croefield's employees. Considerable enthusiasm has already been aroused and the, membership of the society shows a considerable advance. We confidently expeet another triumph for these promising amateurs at the performances in February next.</p>
        <p>Music In the Park. ~ The League of the Cross 'Rand will °lay the following programme in the Park on Sunday, under the conduotorship of Mr. T. Hynes. Hymn, Selected; march " nips.," J. 11. White; selection " Gustavus," Auber; overture " Prince and peasant," H. Round , selection Maritana," Wallace ; eelection " Don Giovanni," W. A klnzart ; Gnd save the King. VACUUM CLILANERS free the home from dust. Price 2to 7 goroeas. Try one on hire.—J. S. GARLAND, 51, to 55, Bridge at, Warriugton.—Advt. Lecture on Consumption. Professor Delepine, the distinguished Director of therublic Healrh Laboratory of Manchester University is to lecture en Monday, 23rd October, at the Co-operative Hall, under the auspices r,f the Guild of Help. The lecture is one that will be a ldressed to a geneial audience of layman and the Professor is sure tamale his subject thoroughly interesting. The full , title will be " Causes at.d effects of tuberculosis con- ! sideted in relation t the spread and prevention of the disease." There will be lantern illustrations The Mayor has kindly consented to preside at 7 30p m. Mr. W. Peter Rylands, chairman of the Infirmary Committee, and Alderman James Evan., chairman of the Health Committee, will speak after the lecture. Anneals will he free, but a certain Lumber will be reserved for ticket-holders until 7.25. Those who desire reserved tickets should apply to Mr. Wilfrid [lowlands, Winmarleigh Chambers, Springfield-street, as soon as possible.</p>
        <p>National Service League. The success of the Perfection Soap Works Band in the Crystal Pal .ce championship contest has undoubtedly produced a great accession of interest in the pub] c meeting of the National Servica League which will he held in the Parr Hall on Wednesday evening next. We understand that already there has been a large demand for tickets and it will be neces- sary to go to the Parr Hall early in order to secure a good seat. During the evening the Perfection band rill play the test piece with which they carried off the 1,000 guinea trophy. The meeting will 1,9 pre- sided over by Lord Del by and the speakers will include , a famous admiral, the ex-secretary of the League, a Manchester Liberal journalist, and a prominent Bvdford Radical. The combination of entertainment and !instruction should certainly prove a great avraction. From 7 to 8 pm. the Perfection Band and the com- bined Warrington Male Voice Choirs will give a choice musical programme. FIRE GRATES. If you want a new grits our large and varied stock will interest you.—J. S. GARLAND, Bridge st., Warrinton.—advt. .</p>
        <p>Warrington and District Sunday School Union. The Inaugural Meeting of the Teachers' Training Circle was held in St. John's Presbyterian Church on IThursday evening The president, Rev. Wm. Reid, pre&amp;ded, Mr. Nicholson, secretary of the Manchester Sunday School Unicer, delivered an inspiring addreEs and described the work and aims of the international Bible Reading Association. Mies J Dewar, Miss V M Taylor and Mr Lewsey gave reports of the Sunday School Conferences held in Live pool last month. The, President then gave a very lucid exposition of next Sunday's International lesson, and di scribed the methods to adopt in teaching the lesson t, seniors, intermedi Ars ar.d juniors. As one teacher left the meeting he was heard to remark "this is what I hare needed for years," so evidently the meetings are filling a long-felt want. The next meeting will be held on November 9th. All teachers are cordially invited. The committee gratefully acknowledge the gift of tl Is. towards the Union Fund from Mrs. Ziba Armitage.. ANTI! RAC ITIC STOVES, Ecomonioal, clean, cheerful. Price from 37/6.—J. S. GARLAND, 51, to 55, Bridge St. Warrington.—Advt. Co-operative Society's Concert</p>
        <p>The Education Committee of the Warrington Co-ot:evative Society are again to be commended on their tuccessful endeavour to provide entertainment file of charge, and the fact that their action is appre- ciated was evidenced by the large audience assembled in the Birchall—Rathbime Memorial Hall, Cairo-street on Thursday evening For the mo 4 part the programme was in the hands of the Warrington Male Voice Choir and under the able leadership of Mr. S. . .</p>
        <p>Hassell their singing was of first rate quality. The items they contributed were " Cro.sing the plain," "Franklin's dog," "Destruction of Gaza," " Give a rouse," and "The soldiers' chorus." ruis Sara Silccek was in sacellent vo:ce and her singing of " Flowers of all the world" was received with hearty applause. The following quartettes were ably rendered by Mrs Sephton,MissSilcock, Mr. Sephton and :lir,W.Duttou " With the budding blooms of May,' '"lit this hour of softened splendour" and "Good-night." Mrs Sephion sang 'The valley by the sea" very effectively, and the duet by Messrs Sephtni and Dutton entitled " Battle Eve" was very popular. Mr J Foster was an able accompanist During the interval in the musical items Mr. Rowland delivered an address on the Guild of Help movement. Mr T Wadsworth, president of the Co-operative Society, watt in the chair. Warrington Wesleyan Cycling Club. ... . . .</p>
        <p>Ten members turned up Ix the last run of the season 1911—the last on the card, on Saturday September 30th. There is little doubt that the afternron would have bees spoiled if we bad attempted the advertised run to Halton against the , north-west gale that was at that moment doing such haven on the North Sea. So a southerly route wasl taken through Wraverham to the Plough Inn at Whitegate. The wind following, a very quick joutney was due, and a pleasant tea including home-made gonad and rasp jelly was served at this excellent little rest-hause with six days licence. When we com- menced the return via Salterawall; the wind had largely dropped, and little difficulty was experienced in reaching town by 9.30. The season closed on Thursday, October sth at the Winmarleigh Cafe. Here 72 members and friends enjoyed a most delightful social of games, music, recitations, and supper Miss Kershaw's " Down the vale," Mr. A. Dutton's talented renderings cd Dickens'" Fagan "and " Uriah Heap," Mr. G. Shaw'e humorous recitations and Mr. F. Kershaw's two humorous songs filled up the enter- tainment part of the programme, and "Should said acquaintance" was not sung till the large boors of the evening. CUTLERY, Rodgers celebrated knives end forks, ,t 3. —Agent, J. S. GARLAND, Ironmonger, Bridge at., Warrington.—Advt. Parish Church Bowling Club.</p>
        <p>In connection with the Warrington Parish Church Bowling club. the annual prize distribution took place on Thursday evening at the King's Cafe. A large number assembled to do justice to a hot pot supper, to celebrate the event. After supper a smoking concert was held, presided over by the Rev. A J. Bratt. Songs were rendered by Messrs. Walter Dennett, W. Hopkins, Wm. Dennett and B. J. Squire whilst Messrs. F. M. Darby and W. Btandreth gave a good account of themselves with two pianoforte duets. During the evening the chairman presented prizes to the follow- ing —Messrs W. Yates, A. Gresty, T. J. Hopkins, and Wm. Dennett. The special prize given by Mr. J. Craik for the best average was won by the captain (Mr R. Antwis) whilst the captain's p: ize went to Mr. T. Yates. Vety hearty votes of thanks mere accorded to the Rev. A. J. Bratt for presiding and the inde- fatigable secretary. Mr. S. Tilling. far the excellent atrangements made, on the proposition of Messrs. F. H. Darby and Walter Dennett. The singing of Auld Lang Syne brought a very pleasant evening ta a close. " °SRAM " AND TANTAI.,,t "LA NI rs.--Agent. J. S. GARLAND, 51, to 55, Bridge it.. Wstrineton.—Advt. Warrington Temperance Guild Inaugural • Meeting The at'endance at the first monthly meeting of the Warrington Temperance Guild augurs sell for the future of this. new organisation. Almost 200 young people assembled in the Caironstreet schoolroom. under the presidency of Mrs James D,Alds and enjoyed an inspiring address IT Mr. Elsom of Manchester and also songs and recitations. The following items were contributed :—Pianoforte duet, Misses Amy Spencer and Nellie Rowe ; songs, Misses Elsie Hewitt, Era Hackett, May Isherwood ; recitation, Miss Maude Kirkland; duet, Misses Spruce and Jones. Miss Spruce ably accompanied for the solos and hymns In the course of a brisf address Mrs. Dodds said that that Guild should be a very great blessing to the members and teethe community. They stood for the principle of temperance in all things, for they believed in people having sound bodies and sound minds. Mr. Elsom delivered an address u psn the cultivation of the body, of the mind and of the spiritual forces. Be said that if they wanted to.get the best out of their bodies they must let alcohol clone, for be believed that nine-tenths of the rained bodies of to-day were due to strong drink. Nobody was ever better for drinking liquors. Some of our greatest boxers, swimmers—and they must remember that Bargees who recently swam the channel did it with- out the aid of alcohol—explorers, such as Neilsen and men who bad led strenuous lives had done it without</p>
        <p>taking atrong drink. After condemning impurity the speaker urged them to lake an active interest in gam's and exercises —A rote of thanks was accorded to the speaker on the proposition of Mr. Thomas Dale.</p>
        <p>Football Team Changes It was announced last tight that Papaknra and Warren will take the places of Utter and Woodburn (injuthd), against Broughton at Warrington this afternoon.</p>
      </div>
