      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>DEATH OF SIR WILLIAM CROSSLEY.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF SIR WILLIAM CROSSLEY.</p>
        <p>It is with profound regret that we announce the death of Sir William Crossley, Bart., which occurred on Thursday in a Manchester nursing home, Sir William, who was 67 years of age, was one of the founders of the well-known firm of Crossley Brothers, gas engineers, of Openshaw, near Manchester. During the past few years the firm have devoted their attention to the building of motor-cars, and in this direction have done much to capture the motor industry from the hands of the foreigner. The late baronet was treasurer of the United Kingdom Alliance, a freeman of Manchester, and director of the Manchester Ship Canal. His benefactions were numer- ous, and included the building, at a cost of</p>
        <p>£70,000, of a sanatorium for consumptives in Delamere Forest, Cheshire._</p>
        <p>In 1903 Sir 'William Crossley became Liberal candidate for the Altrincham Divi- sion, and at the General Election of 1906 he won the seat from the Conservatives by a large majority. His opponent, Mr. Coningsby Disraeli, had held the seat for 13 years. In January, 1910, Sir William defeated Viscount , Bury by 901 votes, but at the December election he was himself defeated by Mr. J. R. Kebty-Fletcher, this result causing consider- able surprise. In the House of Commons Sir William was very popular. Sir William is succeeded in the baronetcy by his son, Kenneth Irwin Crossley.</p>
        <p>.</p>
        <p>Photo Lafayette. THE LATE SIR W. J. CROSSLY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>FUNERAL.</head>
        <p>THE FUNERAL.</p>
        <p>Tne body of the late Sir William Crossley was removed on Thursday evening to Altrincham. The funeral has been flied for Monday afternoon at Bowden Parish Church. The procession will leave the house at 2.15, and the interment is to take place at 2.45. The Lord Mayor and gorporation of Man- chester will attend.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>MR. ROBERT PIERPOINT-.-</head>
        <p>MR. ROBERT PIERPOINT-.-</p>
        <p>PROPOSED FREEDOM OF THE</p>
        <p>BOROUGH.</p>
        <p>We understand that the Mayor, in response to very strong representations made to him, has placed a notice of motion on the agenda for consideration at the next meeting of the General Purposes Committee, to the effect that the honorary freedom of the borough be conferred upon Mr. Robert Pierpoint, in recognition of his eminent services to the town. Mr. Pierpoint represented Warring- ton in the House of Commons for fourteen years and has always taken a keen interest in the welfare and progress of the borough and its many institutions. It is proposed that the resolution of the Council should be engrossed on vellum and presented in a handsome casket : The freedom would be conferred at a meet- ing of the Council specially called for that purpose. Previous recipients of the honour—the highest which the borough can confer—have been : Joseph Davies... ... ... 1891 John Crosfield ... 1891 Charles John Holmes ... 1894 John Richard Pickmere ... 1894 James Fairclough ... 1897 Joseph Charlton Parr ... 1897 Frederick Monks . . ... 1897 John Burgess ... 1897 Joseph Harrison ... ... 1897 The only surviving Freemen are Messrs. J. Charlton Parr and Frederick Monks.</p>
      </div>
