      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>LOCAL WEDDINGS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL WEDDINGS.</p>
        <p>Gibson—Sankey. A very pretty wedding was solemnized on Wednes- day morning at the Parieh Church, the contracting parties being Miss Mabel Sankey, eldest daughter of Councillor and Mrs. Sankey, and Mr. 11. Percy Gibsan, second son of Mr. and Mrs. J. Gibson. both of Church- street, Warrington. The bride having been closely oonnected with the Church and Sunday School for a number of years, and both parties being well-known, the ceremony attracted a large congregation. The service, which was conducted by the Bev. Canon 1% illis was fully choral and most impressive. The anthem, " Sweet is Thy mercy Lord " (J. Smithy) was beauti- fully rendered by the choir during the eigniog of the register, the soloiA being Mr.J. H. Draper. Mr. F M Darby presided at the organ. The bride, who was given away by her father, was attended by her two sisters Misses Beatrice and Leonora Sankey, Dud the bridegroom by his brother, Mr. Lincoln J. Gibson and Mr. J. lb race Sankey. After the ceremony a I reception was held at the home of the bride's parents Thehappy pair left soan afterwards for Derbyshire. The presents were a numerous and handsome collection.</p>
        <p>Reading —Jennings There was an iliterr,ting wedding at St. Paul's Church on Thrusday afternoon when Miss Eliza Jennings, daughter of Mr. James Jennings, licensee tf the White Bull Inn, llorsemarket-street was married t Mr. Joseph George Rowland Rending. win of Mr. T. Reading, of West Bromwich. The Rev. W. Bracecamp vicar officiated. Mr. E. Wilkes presided at the organ and played appropriate a lections including Mendelsaohn's Wedding March The bride, who was given away by her father, was attired in a cream pearl costume with • satin hat to match. ller IyAmnet consisted of chrysanthemums and lilies of the valley. She wore a handsome gold bangle the gift of her father. The following acted an bridesmaids : - Miss Amy Jennings (sister of the bride), Miss Freda Cartenter %niece of the bride) and Miss beading (sisterof the bridegroom) They were all dressed in pale blue silk with black silk picture hats. They carried bouquets of chrysanthemums the gifts of the bride, and wore gold brooches, the gifts of the bridegroom. The duties of best man were discharged by Mr. Robert Carpenter (brother-in-law 'of the bride). The reception was held at the home of the bride's parents. A splendid selection of presents was received.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>FREEMASONRY.</head>
        <p>FREEMASONRY.</p>
        <p>Gilbert Greaten. Lodge Installation:</p>
        <p>The 42nd festival of St. John, in connection with the Gilbert Greenall Lodge of Freemasons, No. 1250. took place on Tuesday in the A:sembly Room of the Lion lintel. There was a large attendance of brethren. Bio William Price was installed Worshipful Master by Bro T lull assisted by BroW It Rose, P.P.O.O.D.C.The new W.M. invested bin officers as follows :—Bros F Rose, S.W. ; W Rogers, .IW.; Rev B Percival, P.P.C.G., chaplain ; TRill, P.M., treasurer: E Rose, P.M. de.. 'secretary ; W Hainsworth, S.D. ; T C Wadsworth, J.D.; A Hale, organist; 0 C Howard, LG. ; If Maddock, S'S. ; H Taylor, 11 Colliu and C Dioundeld, assistait. stewards; and F.l Dell, 1,3 ler. The ceremony was of a very impressive character. In the evening the usual banquet was held, Bro Price presiding. A handsome P.M.'s jewel was presented to W. Bro Joseph Birchen in recognition of his aervices in the chair during the past twelve months. An excellent musical programme was provided by thy Cambrian Quartette of Liverpool. W Bro C W Bridson, P.Pr.G. Ore., being the accom- panist. The catering of W Bro John Lee gave every eat isfactioo.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>WIRRAL SHOW.</head>
        <p>WIRRAL SHOW.</p>
        <p>We are informed that' Mr. Arthur Edwardson has tendered his resignation as Seoretary of the Wirral and Birkenhead Agricultural Society, to the Council, after having occupied the position for 25 &gt;ears, and the Council has elected him the first honorary life member of the society.</p>
