      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>WARRINGTON MOTHER AND DAUGHTERS</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON MOTHER AND DAUGHTERS</p>
        <p>nth OU</p>
        <p>CHARGED. tt St. He'ens on Saturday, Elizabeth Haines, and bee sister Ada, who has been living I le time at 33, P.nners-bro w, Warrington, were charge stealing coetomes and patterns from various local semen. The girls' mother and. man named Thomas sres Thompson, were also charged with e silar offence. The Chief Constable det eller number of attempts a hich were made is ober last by these people to obtain costumes an ing. In the case of Messrs. Boyden Bros. th got two costumes, but they were delivered at ti e by the firm's messenger contrary to the orders manager, who had told him he must have ey first. Clerk ( Mr. Brewis) advised the melees - .00se of larceny ...inst the girls / goods be b-</p>
        <p>,ch€rge4</p>
        <p>Thomas</p>
        <p>trate ti</p>
        <p>v a nano( obtaining go, hi fraud otgerwiie than-by false pretences, but be had very carefully considered the matter, and he was clearly of opinion that the bench would not be justified in finding the girls guilty of larceny. The Chief Constable argued the cage for some time, bat Mr. Breams suggested a remand as the girls had feinted in the dock. The Chairman promised to get some solicitor to take up the case and look after tbe girls' interests. The girls were remanded for a week, and the same course was adopted in the case of their mother and the man Tbomjson. CONTEMPT OF COCEtT ?</p>
        <p>- - - At the County Court on Thursday, before his Honour Judge 11. V. Stanger, K.O , Mr. Worthington, solioitor, ltlnneheater, on behalf of Messrs. Orme and Sons, biHiard table manufacturers, made an application for coMmittal for contempt of court against William M'Neil Sloane, of Orford-lane, Warrington, formerly a traveller in their employ. Mr. Worthington said an order was made in that court on August 17th for the specific delivery of a railway contract between Liver- pool and Manchester and • ticket for the Royal Exchange, value in all ut £l2. The defendant had not obeyed the order of the court. His Honour remarked that the order ought to have been obeyed. Mr. Worth- ington said defendant refused to deliver up the tickets even when the bailiff executed the warrant On September 6th. His Honour said that the tickets had been received at the court that day. An order for attachment would be Mined and the defendant would have to pay the costs. The execution of the warrant would be suspended to give the defend at a further opportunity for explanation. - -</p>
        <p>Ixi; v 0 ; / RTS ir _ ......., / Nr / 1 : rf t s II toidesi ifilir sip&amp; a;num nt 0 ' ober c thie g Is , h se t It Del iv</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>MATRIMONIAL DISPUTES</head>
        <p>MATRIMONIAL DISPUTES</p>
        <p>END IN POLICE COURT PROCEEDINGS. Matrimonial disputes were the cause of two eases appearing at the Borough Court on Tuesday, before Cruncillors It Roberts in the chair), and C Jackson Holmes, Messrs .3 Rymer Young, W Richardson, T Locker, and Dr Naden, when Thomas Pearam Forbes, Elaine-street, summoned his wife, Mary Forbes, for wilful damage ton silk hat and frock coat, in the first instance,while Mary Forbes summoned her husband for desertion in the second case. Mr. Steel appeared for the husband and Mr Brown represented the wife. Mr. Brown stated that with regard to the warrant having tube imued for the arrest of the e ire, she had no knowledge that a summons had been issued as she left the town for Scotland the night before it was served Lot as soon as she d'd know she came hack and surrendered. Mr. Steel stated that in consequence of his wife tearing Lim he had to engage a housekeeper on March stb. At the time he was suffering from sciatica and had to go to Buxton on April 27th. On that day the wife sent to the house with her sister and, after throwing a cup of tea in the housekeepers face and giving ber two black eyes, she seized her husband's silk hat and throwing it on to the floor jumped upon it. She then took the frock coat and cot it with a pair of scissors. Sarah Swift, widow, and housekeeper for Mr. Forbes, corroborated the above. Mr. Brown said that the wife bought the hat and coat vritk, her own money but the husband denied this. Ile 'siert that the Bench should reserve their decision until the hearing of the second case, and this they consented to do. Continuing, Mr. Brown said that it was the pemistent crudity of the husband that caused the wife to leave hiss ,L coupled with his extravagance. He received 3Ss. a week of which be only eave his wife a Mall amount. She bad been engaged at the Hippodrome as a box °tilers keeper for three years but was dismissed through the husband's persistent annoyance of the manager. On February 28th a farewell supper was given to Mr. Eric Longden, the late manager at the Hippodrome, at which the wife attended and the husband also was there for a short period. Upon their arrival home Forbes made certain allegations against his wife which were utterly untrue and then thrashed her. Or. another °Cession he threw his wife out of the house and told her to get a living on the streets. She then got a situation at ,Edinburgh. Mrs. Forbes corroborated. In reply to Mr. Steel she adtititted on one occasion deliberately breaking a Imbed Oddfellowe emblem hut she denied having iised the poker and tongs to her husband. Mr. Steel said no case of desertion had been made out. The bother had arisen through Mrs. Forbes persisting in keeping this employment which there was no reason for her to do. Mr. Forbes was quite willing to take his wife back again. The Bencb retired and upon their return the Chair. mac stated that they hai decided to convict Mrs Forbes for the damage to the amount of 15s. including costs while in the second case an order with 10s. a. week alimony was granted to the wife, each side to pay tbstle own coats and the husband to have the custody of the children.</p>
        <p>tlorough Police Court. - Sacrum:lat.—Before Mr. J W Hayward (in the chair) Alderman Tinnion, and Messrs A J Jolley, R T Fairclough, l' Pemberton and J Upson. ALLEGED STATION THETT.—Walter Eas' hero, 8, Wright-street, Howley, was charged with stealing the previous day a hind quarter of I stub, watts 12s. 6d. from Bank Quay Stati in the property of the Loader) and Noqh-Western Railway Company. The Chief Constabiejbaij.thnt owing to a number of complaints received at Warrington station of parcels being stolen Detective-Sergeant Clarke, of the Railway Police, and another detective cenceeled themselves the previone night at the low level station. The prisoner went along the platform with a goods basket and took the bind quarter of lamb and tore the label off. Ills coat way in the basket, and he put the lamb on the coat and went to the other side where he was seen to put the lamb away. Shortly after he left the station with his coat on his arm, and the ofllzers followed, and prisoner threw the lamb away. H;s house bad been searched *ince hie arresq and ts number of articles, believed to be-Che property of the Railway Campsny, had been ft iind(rWiskted for an adjournment of seven days.— The application was granted. t'sloater.—&amp;fare the Mayor ((n the chair), Aldermen Fo hew, ,itilitmethurst, James Evans, Councillor W elshmen:Messrs W Furness, W Roberts, S Wright, H norntoo, and T Locker. Fo 'ED ON ENCI.OSED PREMIEES.—Edward Payton, residaseg at Orford Barracks, wan charged with being found us the enclosed premises of 76 Loin-street with intact to commit a felony. Chief Inspector William- son in asking for a remand stated that at 7 o'clock the previous evenings young woman named Mary Elizabeth Cattell who resides at the above address,in consequence of previous annoyance canned by men hiding in the sub. pit, etre.* a mateh end found the prisoner there. She Immildintely ran into the house and informed her brother-in-iaw. A search was made but nothing was seen If the prisoner until about nine when be was reco4kited in the street, but refused to give his name and 4dreas. lie en deavoured to strike a witness who was hplding him. A constable was informed and being al wit net the prisoner stated that he did not leave 421M9 GIB elan ectoek. The Inspector asked that the case ight stand adjournel until Friday.—The Bench gran this' request. Nit LECT Ow YAMILY.—James Mather, a striker, !said g at Irlam, was charged with neglecting bin four ildiedwbviehby they became chargeable to the Cele Mr. B. Risearer, said that the children were edmi ito the 17nion on August 12111. Two otber child a hedbalqo been admitted but owe hid left, while 1 the fe had been in and out many timee. The cost had °tailed nearly £OO. Prisoner was previonely work g at Hallam Forge but left upon his own some t. His wages were then one guineas week. It 'Ad a ascertained that the prisoner had been work- Mis new Weeks at Irlam.—The Bench sentenced ..sovihr to a month's hard laborer, the Chairman remariing abet they were sorry that they could not ' - m a longer term. tc .titot:ai dertermael.T.Fo.uarnced steswoocf .der:n of afirt:ludiiosogrdweerlry. : ohast*.—ltlißeettortithisMceasourtre.J Rymer Young (in the sa )W Roberts, A J Jolley, W T Williams and Dr. tat itC 11-701.1-11 GE OF A HORSE.—James Worrell, a iv of Litene, was charged with being drunk in rg of a hope In Thelwall-lane.—A constable elated t saw thekipOioner in charge of a pony and trap Tie snail-lane. le was helplessly drunk.—Prisoner 1 d nothing, teeny seas fined 5s and costa (8s lid) , e alternative of 7 days imprisonment. it. t. Ell THEE? On SOOTS.—E limbed, Lavin, 21, All d-street, was charged with stealing a pair of -toots lee Gs 5i from a shop, 101, Orford-lane the 1 prope yof Mrs Jeakide—Chief Inspector Williamson said t t the boots were seen veto in the shop at I p.m. on th atiketwhewswaThe name evening the prisoner offered the boots for pledge at Messrs. Jacksonn but it was known at the shop that the name and address the</p>
        <p>bad , log al pried!</p>
