        <p>prisoner gave was false and enquiries were given to the police. When arrested prisoner admitted the theft. The police asked that the case might stand adjourned as there was another case to be brought against the prisoner for stealing some rueat.—The case was remanded for 7 days. DRIVER FINED FOR DRFNE ENNEsS.—John Charles Dobson, a larme,, of Preston Brook, was charged with being drunk in charge of a pony and trap in Bridge- street on the 28th ult. Detective Holland in proving the case said that the prisoner was in a very drunken condition. Defendant made a statement that he was at Altrincham Show where he had guts drop of drink. He had to engage a man to drive him home. The Chair- man of the Bench said that as the defendant had treated the Court with contempt in not appearing they would tloe him El and costa with the alternative of one month's imprisonnmt. The defendant afterwards appeared and the Bench reduced the line to 103. and costs. . .</p>
        <p>BENEFIT OF THE Dor BT.—John Pemberton, 30, Wilkinson-street, was summoned for furiously driving a horse and trap in Bridge-street on the 5.6 inst. A constable stated drat at 11.30 on the day in question he saw the defendant driving a hors, attached to a trap in the direction of Market-gate. Defendant was lash- ing the horse making it go at a very fast speed which was most dangerous to the public. Witness put his hand up for the defendant to stop but he did not take any notice. When near to the tram terminus and on the wrong side of the road he dashed into another cart and knocked the pony down and did not stop until he had gone about twenty yards. Defendant's horse was sweating very bad and was thoroughly done up.—A witness corroborated the a, ove.—Defendant said that hi• horse took fright at the tram starting. He utterly den;ed using a whip or anything to lash the horse with. He was not allowed to do so.—The Bench gave the prisoner the benefit of the doubt and dismissed the case MISCELLANEOUS.—Eight youths, named Tnomas Flanaghan, George Dickinson, Joseph Wilde, Thomas Hickey, Thomas Devine, George Daisley,John Corbett, and George Rankin, were each fined 2s. 6d. for playing a game of chance called " Head it "in Bewsey-street. Cuarles Stewart, John Forster, William Bibby and Frank Lee, were similarly dealt with for playing foot- ball in the public highway. Two cases of drunk and disorderly ; two cases of fighting and one case of indecent language was also heard at this court. F'RlDAY.—lletore Alderman Forsbaw (in tha chair) James Beans, Councillor Wilkinson, Messrs. S. Wright, H. Thornton, G. Pilling and A. J. Jolley. " A LAZY WORTHLESS STUPID SCAMP.'—Edward Pri}ton, Orford Barracks, was charged on remand from Monday, with being found cri the enclosed premises of 76 Laira-street for an unlawful purpose. Ile pleaded , not guilty. The father of the prisoner had been inter- viewed and stared that prisoner left the house about 638 p.m. Prisoner had been refused admission to the house and only went there when the father was not at home. He would not work and the father had described him as "a lazy worthless stupid scamp." A number of previous charges having been proved, prisoner was 'sentenced to a month's bard labour.</p>
