      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>Daresbury Petty Sessions</head>
        <p>Daresbury Petty Sessions</p>
        <p>Derasbury Patty Sessi ns w .re held at the Hell Csfe, Stockton Heath, on Tueed ty, before Mesire. It C. Parr (presiding), Ja net Hepherd, W. Fletcher, G. Dekin F. W. Mona), and H. Roberts.</p>
        <p>AN UNPROTECTED FIRE.—John Thomao, Whitley Hall Lodge, Whitley, was summoned for being in charge of certain children, and not having a fireguard.—Tbornas Bell of Whitley said that on September 22nd whilst near the defendant's residence he heard a child scream- ing and then new a young girl run along the highway in flames. He took or his coat and wrapped it round the girl and estinguished the flames. The girl was very badly burned and died from the effects in the Warrington Infirmary the aims day.—the Bench ordered the defendant to pay the costs ss. the Chairman remarking that although they sympathised with the defendant for the death of his child, the case was a very serious one. LOITERING WITH Furnley, of no settled abode, was charged with being a convicted thief and with loitering with intent to c ommit a felony.—P.C. Whalley, stationed at Walton. said that shoat ten o'clock on the 4th lost whilst near to Walton school he saw the prisoner acting in a suspicious manner. Prisoner WAs walking in the direction of , Daresbury from Warrington but after he had gone a short distance he returned end then went in the direction of Runcorn. Witness called to the prisoner but he at first took no notice. Prisoner stated that he was going to Runcorn. The constable noticing some- thing under his coat requested him to produce it and he stated that it was only a pair of ladies' slippers. When questioned he made a statement that he hod purchased the slippers from a woman in a public-house for 21. He refused to give his proper name and address when requested to do no. Witness afterwards ascertained that the prisoner had called at the Walton Arms enqniring the time that they closed. Prisoner stated " he was as innocent as a child."—The Bench sentenced tho prisoner ton month's hard labour. SMART PENALTY FOR A SERIOUS OFFENCE William Kitchen and John Parker of Norton were charged with being drunk while in charge of two horses and a lorry in Walton Superior on October 2nd, William Oeen Jones, stationmaster at Preston Brook Station, said that on the day in question while engsged in the office he sew the lorry with a load of straw crossing over the railway bridge. He thought that the lorry had fallen over, but upon going to the spot he found that it had collided with the wall of a stable. One of the shafts hid gone through a window. Both the defendants were drunk.—A countable stated that he found the defendant Kituhen in charge of the horses. He was very drunk and not fit to be in charge of them. The other defendant he found !Weep in the stable. Kitchen made a statemet.t that Parker fell off the lorry. and that he went to pull him away from the wheel. The heavy load that was on the cart compelled it tigo down the incline at a List rate where it collided with the wall. E ich defendant was fined El and cost', the Chairman remark- ing th it if anybody had happened to meet the horses it would have meant certtin death. . _ .</p>
        <p>1111sc ELL aNEOUS.—The following were each fined 2s. 6d. for various offences i—Joseph Minney, Moss- lane, Stratton, for allowing his horse to stray on to the highway ; Robert Bennett, 21, Farrell-road, Stockton Heath, for allowing his dog to be at large during pro- hibitive hors ; Wilfrid Simpson, 16, Lovely-lane, Warrington' for having no name anl address ou his cart ; Barker Ifstton, Greystone-road, Per.kath, for riding a bicycle without a light ; and Herbert Wilson, Allen-road, Paddington, for being cosh a distance from his horse and luny, as not to !lava cinitrol over it, in Elle mere-road.</p>
