      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>TRAGEDY OF THE SHAKERS.</head>
        <p>THE TRAGEDY OF THE SHAKERS.</p>
        <p>I could scarcely believe my eyes when I read a few daya ago' that two members of the Shaker Community, in Florida, had been arrested on a charge of murder.</p>
        <p>For nearly a hundred and fifty years, no criminal charge of any kind had ever before been made against any member of the most picturesque, communal sect in the world. But the facts, as cabled from America, again reveal the Shakers as a strange and loveable people. The facts are simple. A Shaker sister, suffering the worst and final agonies of galloping consumption, begged a brother and sister Shaker to put her out of her misery. In all civilized countries, people have frequently debated the question : Is it right in any circum- stances to shorten human life? But while the world talked, two members of the most unworldly community in existence, settled the question in their ova hearts with dramatic swiftness.. . . .</p>
        <p>It was their reverent Molds that gave her the anmsthetic—a dose of chloroform—that sent her to sleep for ever; it was their loving desire to release her from suffering that supplied the beautiful motive for what most people may call murder !” And who are the Shakers? They are the successors of the original followers of an English woman, Ann Lee, who founded the sect in the second half of the eighteenth century. The Shakers derive their name from the idea, founded on taut, that the members would shake will emotion, or ecstscy, when telling their , experiences of spiritual life, at their gospel meetings. ! Their religion is of the early Quaker pattern, and , their sacred songs are often accompanied by • stately dancing, such as it is supposed Miriam performed after the crossing of the Red 'Sea. Both the men and the women Shakers live celibate lives, and their numbers, though dwindling, are recruited I only by the accession of converts. It is the habit of the Shakers to adopt orphan children, who remain with them until they have reached years of discretion. Then they have to choose between going out into the world, or remaining to live the austere, self-denying life of the Shakers and to profess their simple faith. All property, of every kind, among the Shakers belongs to the community, the women having equal rights with the men. The motto of these simple folks sums up their character " Our work for the world, and the rest to God " ! They rise early, are as hard-working as even the Carmelite monks, and devote themselves to agri- cultural and a few manufacturing pursuits. What business sffsirs they have are discussed at the general meetings of the Shakers, and authority is vested in two men and two women chosen, as trustees, by each colony. The two junior communities in Florida and Ohio, have little commercial contact with the outer world, but the parent community, at mount Lebanon. New York, is largely engaged in cultivating, or gathering, shunt a dozen different herbs which &amp;fib not grown elsewhere, its only British customer being • London firm which uses the herbal extracts obtained by the Shakers in the making of a remedy for indigestion which has a world-wide reputation as Mother Seigel's</p>
        <p>Syrup. In favour of the Shakers, it meet be admitted thst their few manufactures are of superior quality, and their industry, cleanliness, fair•dealing and peace-abiding habits are a pattern for the world. Such are the simple folk, two of whose number are now charged with murder, and the world is asking : "What will the verdict be? "</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>• cp.— 81.44 WARRINGTON "2" /si •) t • rC r /</head>
        <p>• cp.— 81.44 WARRINGTON "2" /si •) t • rC r /</p>
        <p>\,</p>
        <p>The New Church.</p>
        <p>" No Divorcement of Life and Religion. " The essential doctrines of the No* Church were ably and interestingly set forth by the Rev. W. H. Claxton (of Keanley) in a lecture which he delivered at the Masonic Hall, Bold-strott, on Wednesday evening. There was a good attendance and taking as his subject " Man's life, hem and hereafter," the rev, gentleman said that 'Whereter the New Church was in the world it had one doctrinn, which win based upon the Scriptures, drawn from the law, and was in agreement with human reason, reconciling the teaching of the Divine Word with itself, with the demands of rational thonght and with the teachings or true science. It was a doctrine which did not claim on anybody's part blind faith, but it claimed to beroflt any human mind into which it found an entrance. It was a doctrine colculsted to make this world a great deal more like Heaven than it was. Men seemed only content with what they could see, feel and enjoy and they seemed to beware of examining too curiously into the future. That was its unwise, irrational ant injurious state of mind and was absolutely foolish. That state of mind was not capable of making the best of this world or the next just because it did not recognise the intimate relationahip between this world and the next. The result of Godly men and women going into monasteries and convents entirely away from temptation had produced a number of weaklings, hot-houee plants, who could not exist safely in the storms of the world. That was A COWARDLY RELIGION for it left the world to look after its owe salvation and had very little influence for good on other people. This was followed by a doctrine which revolted against the idea of works having any connection with salvation and it did not produce a race of strong. vigorous Christian men and it had its nemesis in the idea that religion Rft9 one thing and business another. He was afraid that for some time to come they would have t 3 bear that unwholesome and unnecessnry divorcement of life from religion, merely considering religion to be a ceremony of forms and doctrines which had no connection with ,tlhs, practical</p>
        <p>activities of the day. By example and Irj precept Christ urged combination of life and religion—He went into the people's homes, healed men of their diseases and conferred upon His disciples similar power. The New Church not only considered that the true teaching of the Lord, but that it should be brought back into the world. It was the only teaching that would solve the problems that were affecting the life of to-day. People should be MOTE thoughtful of the things of to-day and to-morrow than they were,for when Christ was reported to have said, " Take no thought for to-morrow " he meant "Be not anxious about to-morrow," for He knew that anxiety would be and was the cause of so many nervous disorders and illnesses. They held that this world was intended by God to be a training ground for the real life which was to come and that death was hot a penalty of the soul, but simply an inevitable order in the development of humanity. 11 AN WAS NOW IN THE BIOL.:LH, in the making, and what man was made here he would be for ever. They held that man would be judged according to his works, as well as his faith. Christian men should prepare to suffer by only doing the right thing under all circumstances and if all Christians would stand by that there would not be the corruption in the world that we had. We should be able to feel that when we went to buy an article we should get our worth. It might be said that they of the new Church were not going to transform the world in live minutes. No, but it would never ba transformed at all unless they made an effort. What the Church wanted we-e men who would prove their faith by their works. (Applause). Next Wednesday the Rev. W. H. Claxton will lecture on" Death, Resurrection and Judgment."</p>
        <p>s•</p>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society. Th=re was a fair attendance on Sunday, presided over by the Secretary, who spoke upon the hymn previously song. " All the good that we can do no the day* are going by." The days seemed to pass slowly in youth and manhood, but afterwards in a busy life, they appeared to fig away. All days, early or later, were right for i.oing good. A smile and a s3mpathetio look would often do more good than words. Mr. Kneen, of Liscard gave the address upon " Sun." lie said aspirations after the beautiful, grand ideals, seeking through nature, to behold God in all things, were holy and good, but to seek to gratify the lower desires of human nature was sin. " The wages of sin in death" temporally, morally and spiritually. Mr. Josep'n Owen and Mr. W. Keswick also took part in the meeting.</p>
        <p>Colborne-street Baptist Church. Large congregstions assembled at Golborne-street Baptist Church last Stiodsy to welcome the Rev. H. Ogden who commenced his ministry then. Taking as his lost,' Study to show thyself approved unto God, • workman that needeth not to be ashamed rightly dividing the word of truth," 2 Timothy ii., 15. The rev, gentleman gave a very helpful discourse upon the, Chris, Lin as a workman. In the evening he based his remarks upon the words " I ask therefore for whit intent you have sent for me," and he outlined some of the duties of the Christian minister. He said the Christian minister's duty was to serve the Lord, preach the Gospel, champion the cause of the oppressed, watch the young and in every way endeavour to bring the kingdom of God upon the earto. At this service Miss Emily Barnes rendered a solo and the choir also contri- buted an anthem. In the afternoon the Bewsey-road Women's Bible Class gave a Service of Song entitled " Little Minnie "in a very able manner, Mrs. Critchley !being the reader. At the week levening service held on Wednesday the Pastor spoke upon the suij.ct of trust.</p>
        <p>COUNCILLOR PLEABERTON SUED. Humour at the County Court. An in:,esting case, with plenty of humourona incidents arising therefrom, came up before his Honour Judge Henry Stanger, K C., at the County Court on Thursday, when Mr Richard Miller, contractor, of 28 Caoreway-avenue, Warrington, claimsd .Cl 5 from Coun- cillor John Pemberton, builder, of 14, Fairfleld-road, Stockton Heath, for a quantity of white glazed bricks purchased from the plaintiff by the defendant on the 6th March, 1911, and for some rain tubs purchased in April last. Councillor Pemberton entered a counter- claim for general damages for not taking the bricks from his yard, his defence being that he did not order the said bricks. Mr Arthur Brown represented the plaintiff and Sir E A White appeared for Councillor Pemberton.</p>
        <p>Mr Browce said that after advertising 3,530 hest white glared bricks for sale Mr Miller met Councillor Pemberton and the latter asked the plaintiff to send him a sample of the bricks advertised. Mr Miller took the sample of bricks to Mr Pemberton's foreman (Mr John Bennett) who is now dead. The plaintiff told the foreman when he handed the bricks to Lim that be would sell a similar kind at £6 per 1,000. He arranged to call on the next day to see whether Mr Pemberton would take them. Whrn he called the next day the plaintiff stw the foreman who told him thtt Mr Pemberton would take the bricks if be could have them at a penny per brick. Thereupon Mr Miller agreed to do so and was told to sand the bricks to Mr Pemberton's yard in Warburton-street, Stockton Heath. He dil so conveying 3,3:16 brieks in seven lurry loads. The same night Mr Miller sent en invoice for the bricks amounting to £,3 lfla to Councillor Pemberton. . .</p>
        <p>Mr. White: We never have had such an invoice. Mr. Browne : One was sent. Three weeks afterwards Mr. Miller called on the defendant for paying for the bride and Councillor Pemberton told him that all his money waa in property and be could not aff Jrd to pay him then. He also asked Mr. Miller if he would try to sell me property for him. Mr. Miller agreed to wait a little time for payment and then be got an order from Councillor Pemberton's for four rein tubs on April 51b. They were supplied and costing .£1 2s. that made the amount owing to the plaintiff .£l4. Mr. Miller saw the defendant many times about settling the account and Councillor Pemberton said that he could not cube was short of money and had been compelled to borrow money to pay wages. Councillor Pemberton said that if plaintiff could not leave it over Le could have the bricks back again end be would gve him 10s. for expenses. However, the plaintiff declined and later put the matter into the hands of his solicitors (Messrs. Browne and Co.) After correspondence with the defendant Messrs. Browne obtained a service of summons against Councillor Pemberton and he evaded service until Mr. Clare a clerk of the firm, happened to go to Bank Quay Station on the day of the strike. Defendant happened to be there and Mr. Clare went up to him and said 'I wanted to see you. You know what about." As soon as he saw Mr. Clare produce the summons</p>
        <p>COUNCILLOR PEMBERTON BOLTED and Mr. Clare ran after him and when he touched him with it, of course be had served the summons. be Plaintiff corroborattd Counsel's statement and in eros-sexamination by Mr. White denied that Mr. , Pemberton only gave him an order for 300 biicks. In opening the case for the defence Mr. White said that Councillor Pemberton only ordered 900 bridal and the resson for the 3,336 bricks being delivered was because of the plaintiff's brother telling his foreman that Councillor Pemberton had ordered them, when as a matter of fact, he had not. With regard ta the rain tuba Conneillor Pemberton had paid the money for those into court and also the money for the 300 bricks. It was perfectly true that Councillor Pemberton ran away from personal service of the summons. me thought it was silly but he did not think that that should affect the merits of the clue. Mr. Browne ought to have asked the decadent for the name of his solicitors and sent the summons to them. _ _ . . . _</p>
        <p>--- Councillor Pemberton, in the box, corroborated Mr. White's statement. - -</p>
        <p>Mr. Browse : What did you run away from the service of this summons for ?—Because I did not know what lo was. As a rule most people I have had to deal with have asked me for my soli,itor's came. Mr. Browne : But you hive bad no many solicitors? —There are only 7 or Sin the town. Mr. Browse: There are 38. Continuing, he asked Councillor Pemberton why there was no mention in the correspondencs about the order for only 300 bricks, and why no mention was made of the fact when the action was entered ?</p>
        <p>Councillor Pemberton replied that he did not know that an action bad been begun. What is a solicitor's letter for ?—Bluff, sometimes. Have you ever run away from service of a summons before ?—ilaa that got anything to do with this case. The Judge (to Councillor Pemberton): You had better answer the question. Councillor Pemberton: Yes. Mr. Broene: Did you go throush the Counel Chamber window once no Brit you would not be served? (Laughter).—Yes. Did you once succcei in evading a summons by going oat by a back door and keep my clerk waiting a quarter of an hour?—l did not know it was your clerk.</p>
        <p>What did you run away for on this occasion ? Became I did not know what Mr. Clare was going to give me. (Laughter.) I saw something In bin band so I asked him shit it WWI and be would net tell ire. (Renewed laughter.) How far did you run?—l don't hnow.</p>
        <p>You were a good sprinter a few years ago? —I never won any prizea. (Laughter.) Mr. (Aare ciught you up ?—f do not know that he overtook me.</p>
        <p>Tonaway?—l was not going to facilitate any proceedings against me. A man named Bennett (son of the late foreman). said ,1 that his father told him there were only 300 bricks ordered. He did not like taking the other loads, because he was not certain that Councillor Pemberton bad ordered them. .</p>
        <p>George Houghton, employed by Councillor Pemberton corroborated. In giving judgment, his Honour said that the evid- ence was no conflicting that be had th 3 painful duty to consider where there bad been deliberate falsehood. As be had before him a copy of a letter alleged to have been sent by Councillor Pemberton to Mr. Miller, atating that be only ordered 300 bricks, be felt that the plaintiff had not satisfied him as to his case and berm. judgment for the defendant.</p>
        <p>43</p>
