      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>S.P.G. Meeting. Difficulties of Missionary Effort hi South Africa.</head>
        <p>S.P.G. Meeting. Difficulties of Missionary Effort hi South Africa.</p>
        <p>There was a larger attendance than usual at the annual meeting of the S.P.G. held on Tuesday evening at the Parr Hill. The Mayor (Councillor Dr. Joseph) presided and assembled on the platform were :—The Yeti. E.H. Shears (of Natal),Rev. Father Norton, S.S.NI (of South Africa), Canon Willis and local clergy an laymen. The MAYOR said that even in the laws of our land, although some of them were not always admired, there was mach that helped them to lead a good Christian life. It was so even in regard to the legislation that was being enacted now. They ought to pray. for and help that noble band of warkers in foreign missions— those who had left home and Mends end were now practically in exile,submitting to hardships and daegers to health and life. The foreign missionary needed to be a facto-turn and by a great stress of imagination he thought there was an analogy between foreign missions and the Warrington Health Committee and Guild of Help, for they called themselves misaionaries. lcut they were bound down by the iron hand of legislation; they could only go a certain way and do certain things as regarded the health of the people, bet they could not touch them socially unless they wriggled out of the iron hand of legislation. They were trying to do good, as far as they could, without breaking the law and he thought they were succeeding. They were trying to improve the people's life,teaching them how to train the young,and how to make their homes brighter.Contiouing the Mayor raid that those who had the Gospel in their midst and did not take advantage of it—he was not alluding to those who from their pride of intellect delibera•ely rejected God and Corist—were generally use'esi both with their hand and brain. Those pen pa who neglected the Gospel were in a very much worse position than those who had not gat it within their a rasp. The Rev. Father NORTON spoke of Christian work in South Africa. He said if they did not do evangelis- ing work there other people would do it. Even now Frenchmen were doing more than they, and they bad to take a back seat. The reason for that Wan because of lack of money. Already the Ethiopians were working in South Africa, because we were not in the field. Even if the Government did not like the Ethiopians there were the Mohammedans waiting to come in, and ifs nation got into Mohamrcedaniam it was very bad, lansuse though Christians had tolerated certain wrong things, Moha:umedans had sanctified slavery, polygamy and tyranny. He asked them to support the S.P.G., because it looked after English people who went abroad and endeavoured to keep them true to their religion, and he thought the audience would admit that the higher their standard the better the people, would be. (Hear, hear and applause). It was necessary that the men who went out to do mission work should be well trained. They wanted men who could appreciate native custo ns and look at things with a aide mind and take all that was good in the native life and custom and use it. (Applause). The Venerable E. H. SHEARS (formerly Archdeacon of Natal) said that by far the first most important work of the S.P.O. in South Africa was that among their own colonists—among the professing Christians; the second among the natives, and the third among a large popula- tion of natives of India there. Ile said that if they wanted to bring the heathen in the grandest thing was to send missionaries first to professing Chriitinne because one of the hot means of converting heathens waa to show them good men and women. Missionaries had been working in Natal for over a hundred years and they h.d gone on well, but the great diffioulty had Leon in impressing upon the Kaffirs the existence of Gad, as they had no word in the language which they could use, so they bad to teach them what is meant. Now the missions had taken a considerable hold in Zululand, althouith there WM an enormous amount of work yet to be done. If the people had not got religion before they had got a good deal of superstitiou, and one of the results of Christian work was that the witch doctors could not do what they used to be able to do : now they had to go beyond the range of the missions, because the power of the Devil was crippled. At the same time it should Ire admittel that the English Church was nowhere in the mission field. The Nonconformists and the Roman Catholics beat them all over the world. They had pnly a seventh of the cumber of missionaries in India that</p>
        <p>the Nonconformists had, and if India was ever to be converted to Christ, apart from the Roman Catholics, who had an Enormous mission there it would be mainly—unless they made an enormous , effort—by the American Noncsnforruista We were nowhere, we English people. It was well for on to take that fact in, and not imagine we were doing very well, because we were not. We must not be content to lag behind other bodies, but to wake up. (Applause.) If we did not raise more money in South Africa, so fat from expanding our work we should have to give up what was being done now. It was essential that they should have ten times as much money and elso must TL was necessary. The Rev. E. C. E. CARLETON proposed a vote of thanks to the Ven. E. H. Shears and the Rev. Father Norton and this was heartily carried. On the motion of the Rev. CANON WILLIS the Mayor was thanked for presiding. Mr. W. H. RANDLES presided at the organ. At the close of the meeting the MAYOR announced that the collection amounted to EIO 2s.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>St. John's Preshyteriau Church.</head>
        <p>St. John's Preshyteriau Church.</p>
        <p>On Monday evening SL John's Church Guild began its winter session, the evening's programme consisting of ri aeries of holiday pipers, contributed by the members. Papers were read giving interesting accounts of holiday ramblings, incidenti in the Isle of Man, Wales and the East Coast of England. One member</p>
        <p>ri-x-r.r—at.Td--;;;;;;m-using -erperieooe. a which befel him whilst taking bis wife and family for a holiday tour by motor. General satisfaction wan expressed with the weather conditions of last summer for holiday maker. One or two member, were loud in their praises of the beautiful sunsets they had seen, but honestly admitted they bad not been able to oSiterve any sunrises.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>RELIGIOUS e- SO C/ A L</head>
        <p>RELIGIOUS e- SO C/ A L</p>
        <p>IRISH AFFAIRS. NOTES FOR IRISH READERS.</p>
        <p>THE HOME RULE CAMPAIGN.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>LORD PIRRIE'S THUNDERBOLT.</head>
        <p>LORD PIRRIE'S THUNDERBOLT.</p>
        <p>The campaign in support of Home Rule has opened well. Mr. John Redmond's reception as the guest of the Ninety-five Club at the Manchester Reform Club was one of the meet enthusiastic occasions remembered in that historic building. He prefaced a cogent and powerful statement of the Irish demand by saying that the friends of Ireland were now commencing a great public discussion on the question of Home Rule. But a few hours previously the discussion hail ken most auspiciously inaugurated at a gathering in Belfast, where the Postmaster-General and the Master of Monk were the guests of Lord Pirrie. The speeches were of a notable and highly significant character. Referring to the visit of the Eighty Club the "Irish Times "—that organ of a doomed. cause-- - .</p>
        <p>made much of the opposition which the visitors met with on Home Rule from the "grim workmen of the North," and stress u-as laid on the apprehension felt by the ill- informed Orangemen of a corner of Ulster that their livelihood as well as their liberties would be jeopardised by thg establishment of a Parliament in Thiblin. Now at this critical juncture nothing could exceed in importance and interest the considered statement of long and deeply-formed conviction of the gentle- man who more than anyone else is responsible for the conspicuous success of the great business of this world-famed shipbuilding firm. Lord Pirrie's speech has dropped like a thunderbolt. in the camp of the Unionists, and these infuriated opponents of his Lord- ship's alleged amongst other things that, having regard to the coming of Home Rule, Lord Pirrie had considerably diminished his interests in the firm. Lord Pirrie has nailed that lie. His interests in the firm, he publicly declares, were never greater than they are now. His speech is so valuable a contribution to the great public discussion now current on the Home Rule question, that we are tempted to make the following ex- tract :</p>
        <p>THERE DOES NOT APPEAR TO BE ANY PERSON WITH A STAKE IN BELFAST, OR ULSTER. WHO IS ABLE OR WILLING TO LEAD THE OPPOSITION TO HOME RULE.</p>
        <p>It has been necessary to adopt as a leader a gentleman who has no material in- terest whatever, that I know of, in the country, beyond, perhaps, a feeling of gratitude to the Tory Party for the fact that sonic years ago that party employed him as a law officer of the Crown to assist in repressing his fellow-countrymen from committing acts of disorder and violence. Were not those acts far less serious than those which his friends at present advo- cate should be adopted by the alleged " loyal " population of Ulster in the etent of Home Rule being granted? Ought not such men to be restrained from inciting their followers to rebellion and disorder? (Applause.) I wish my right honourable friends, Thomas Sinclair and Thomas Andrews, and others who speak with them, would bear in mind, before in- dulging in such threats of insurrection or reprisals, the fairness and generosity of the other side to them. In particular I would commend to them, as worthy of emulation, the noble sentiments of good- will towards those who differ from him, to which the leader of the Nationalist Party gave expression in his welcome to the Eighty Club in Dublin, and on sun- dri other occasions. (Applause.) I am convinced that PROTESTANTS AND CATHOLICS CAN WORK TOGETHER IN HARMONY in matters of government as well as in purely local affairs. (Applause.) We have had many instances in connection with County and District Council govern- ment, with Belfast institutions, and in business concerns, where Protestants and Catholics works amicably together. Why cannot it be so in matters of-State!'</p>
        <p>This makes, to our mind, not —oni.;:-.a fine vindication of a political career, often mis- represented, but it contains a destructive criticism of the forces which, in the name of the moustry of the North of Ireland, pro- cb.ims that self-government spells the ruin of that industry. A more insolent claim could not be made. Lord Pirrie, on all hinds accepted a; one of the best business minds in the world, refutes it, and forther declares that in his examination of the Tory case he finds no person with a stake in Belfast or in the province who is able or willing to lead the cause of opposition to Home Rule. And so this unenviable distinction falls to a law officer of the Tory Party whose fame rests on his activity against the people in the Coercion Courts of the eighties. Mr. Samuel's speech should be read in con- nection with Mr. Redmond's. Every state- ment, every hope of the Irish leader is con, - firmed in the speech of the Liberal Cabinet Minister. Mr. Samuel rejoiced in the aboli- tion of the unlimited veto as one of the greatest achievements of his party: and in a series of arguments showed that Ireland under the present system had no government worthy of the name, and that justice could only be done to her and the Empire by the concession of such rights as are enjoyed by Canada, Australia. and South Africa. Next year, said Mr. Redmond, the present Government will introduce a Home Rule Bill, and' it will pass the HOMC of Commons by an over- whelming majority and, despite the House of Lords, will become law in this Parliament." The Postmaster-General, at Belfast. was not less definite or less confident as to the course of events than Mr. Redmond was at Man- chester.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>W. RUSSELL'S TRIUMPH.</head>
        <p>W. RUSSELL'S TRIUMPH.</p>
        <p>Mr. T. W. Russell's return for North Tyrone is a most timely incident in the Home Rule campaign. The staunchness of this t3pical Ulster constituency to the Home Rule cause in face of the seriously adverse in- fluences which have-been exercised there, has made a great impression, more particularly as the result follows so closely on the bluster- ing of Sir Edward Carson at Craigavon. Mr. Russell made Home Rule the central issue, and his splendid triumph makes it quite evi- dent that North Tyrone at any rate will have nothing to do with Sir Edward Carson and the men who claim to speak for the province of Ulster, in which t bat can,t it ours is situated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>PERFECTION BANDS TRIUMPH.</head>
