      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>POPULAR SCIENCE.</head>
        <p>POPULAR SCIENCE.</p>
        <p>Vros's Cola O.</p>
        <p>A non-gullahle nail has been invented France. she point is sIA through its centre to nearly two-thirds the diatance to the heaO, giving it a similar appearance to that of • split cotter. When the nail is driven into the wood the two parts separate and curl into um wood.</p>
        <p>To litAxx PHONOGRAPHS CLIARRR.</p>
        <p>The horn shown in the sketch is an Amen. can notion for use on phonographs, mega- phones, and the like. The devices amplifies the original sounds by means of the vibrat- ing etr:ngs which are carried within the</p>
        <p>horn, and which take up the vibrations of the original sound, thus producing a clearer and stronger tone. The means provided regulate the pitch, and break up objectionable echoes that produce those conflicting sound waves which impair the ordinary device.</p>
        <p>„ , atm MUSICAL HORN.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>NEW SMOKE CONSIMER.</head>
        <p>NEW SMOKE CONSIMER.</p>
        <p>A smoke consumer and fuel economiser, said to be the result of twenty years trials, has been successfully tested at Sheffield. Coal was fed into the furnace of a boiler generat- ing steam for running the works, with the usual result—the emission of black smoke from the chimney: then the consumer was out into action, and in place of the black smoke there was only a slight trace of the products of combustion. The change is effected by an arrangement of a series of de- vices so placed in the flues as to intercept the smoke, and cause it to be ignited by the flames. Two air circulators are so placed as to allow the desired quantity of external air to be circulated among these devlces, causing the smoke to be propeily ignited, and con- sumed around the boiler flues before entering the chimney. -- MoTOU Fon Boron ROAM.</p>
        <p>roads of Costa Rica, notorious for deep ruts which make touring with the uiusl type of motor-ear almost impossible, are respon- sible for the paculiar type of car shown in the illustration. The car might prove just as useful, however, in other countries afflicted with deeply-rutted roads. It is well nigh a mechanical impossibility to gat out of</p>
        <p>many of thew, ruts with the four-wheeled ear without assistance. With the newly-designed car, however, the front -eel runs between the ruts, and makes it pnrsinle to steer out of the track. With the exception of the single wheel in front, and the slight alterations of the frame to suit it, the chassis follows th• usual design.</p>
        <p>NEW METAL FOR Toots. There is not much fear that the world will be deprived of cutting tools when the supply of iron gives out. It has been discovered that an alloy of cobalt and chromium is an excellent substitute for steel, and has in addition the valuable property—that it will not rust or tarnish. Elwood Haynes, the in- ventor, describes this interesting alloy, which he has named "stellite." It is apparently not yet in shape to be manufactured com- mercially, but has fascinating poesibilites. The discovery wan the result of a number of experimente. Mr. Haynes states that he was ultimately able to produce metal that would forge out perfectly into thin strips, which showed no tendency to check. After cooling, these stripe were as hard as mild- tempered steel, and could scarcely be en-etched by a file. A kitchen knife-blade WRS made front this material, and used for all aorta of purposes, such as are known only to the culinary art. After two years I f use it showed not the faintest sign •of tarnish- ing, and if held in the sun it prc-duced a re- flection that would dazzle the eye. In colour. the metal stands between silver and steel. and if suitably polished it shows • high lustre.</p>
        <p>THZ EVOLUTION OP lIAN. Professor Henri Danville has found that changes of temperature and habitat offer • plausible explanation of the evolution of man from the lower animals. A great and per- manent fall of temperature at the close of Secondary times seems to Irate elided the reign of reptiles, and favoured the develop- ment of mammals, which are Letter orga- nised to resist cold. Then the mammal, evolved in the two great dames of the eaters and the eaten—those preyed upon surviving arid developing in various branches. accord- ing to their resisting powers. The insecti- vore divided into animals furnished with special protection, like the hedgehog, bur- rowers, -like the mole; and flying creaturess like the bats. Of the herbivore, some, like the rhinoceros, were adapted far resistance by weight and armour; others, like the liorse sad the antelope, by their swiftnes.. One group, the primates, including the apes and finally man, sought refuge in the trees. Then came the temporary chilling of the region of some of the arasii by the Glacial epoch at the close of the Pliocene period, and the tropical forest became no stunted as no longer to support large tree - dweller+. Forced to walk, the skeleton of the animals changed, brain and intelligence grew, alai man was born.</p>
