      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>LAUNCH OF THE KING GEORGE V.</head>
        <p>LAUNCH OF THE KING GEORGE V.</p>
        <p>- - - This week Britain's s'..venlh super-Dreadnought, the King George V., was success• fully launched at Portsmouth by Princess Christian. This new fighting force is 560 feet long, and has a displacement of 24,000 lons. She will be fitted with turbines, which will develop 31,000 horse rower. Ihe armament will consist of ten 135 in. and twenty 4 in, guns, and three 21 in. torcedo tubes. The vessel was afterwards towed into the deckyard, where she will be completed.</p>
        <p>THE LARGEST PROJECTILE IN THE WORLD. The United states Government has lately carried out experiments with a 16 inch gun. This monster fires the largest and heaviest projectile in the world. The shell is five feet high, weighs 2400 lbs., and can be fired a distance of over 20 miles. The cost of fir ug one shot is neatly £2OO. The gun is now at Sandy Hook, and has only been fired a few times. Our picture shows the shell compared with the bright of a soldier.</p>
        <p>Vicount Wolmer, M.P. : I have carefully considered the Office Regulation Bill and I can say that its prin- ciple has my heartiest support. I have never been able to see why clerks should be treated worse than other employes and I will gladly do what I can to secure them the same advantages as are eojoyed by their fellow-workers. Mr. J. Rebty Fletcher, M.P. The Bill will have my beet consideration. I am convinced that there should be a very strict sanitary inspection of all offices. Mr. R. A. Yerburgh, M.P. : I will give the Bill my best oonsiderttion. Mr. A. H. Crosfleld (ex-Member for Warrington) and the British Delegate for Labour Legislation) : With regard to the second reading of the Office Regulation Bill I will do what loss to influence my Parliamentary friends to support it. There are many cases in Englaad where there is a ming need for reform in hours of work, but among the most urgent of them all is, I think, the case of the clerks, both as regards their bourn of work and fair remuneration.</p>
        <p>CHILDREN'S COUGHS Blood and Phlegm Spitting Cured by VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE. Mrs. A. Jones, 40, Malwood-street, South End, Liverpool, writes —"My: three children bad whoop- ing cough very badly, end Harold had terrible blood and phlegm spitting, besides being sick after any food. I tried many patent medicines and prescriptions, but they got no better. Then I tried Vene's Lightning Cough Cure, and at once the sickness stopped, the bleeding was arrested, and soon the cough and whoop left them. We cannot tweak too highly of your standard medicine." Veno's Lightning Gough Cure was awarded a Grand Prix and Gold Medal for purity and efficacy at the recent Paris Health Eshibition, and is safe for the youngest intent. Price 90., lc lid. and 2e. 9d. of all chemists.</p>
