      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>UNION OF CLERKS.</head>
        <p>THE UNION OF CLERKS.</p>
        <p>Local M P.'s and the Office Regulation</p>
        <p>Bill.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Warrington Branch of the National Union of Clerks was held at the Labour Exchange on Monday, the chief business being the contideration of the Office Regulation 13111, the second reading of which takes place on October 20th. The following resolution WEIS passed and the Secretary was Instructed to send copies to the Prime Minister, the President of the Board of Trade, the Home Secretary, the Leader of the Opposition and the Chairman of the Parliamentary kii.our Party : " In view of the great hold that connumpti,n and other pulmonary diseases have upon clerks owing to the unhealthy conditions they are forced to work 'alder, the Warrington Branch is of opinion that 41 offices and places in which clerks have to work should be brt right within the scope of the Factory Act as sportily as passible and requests the Oovernntent to grant facilities for the naming of the Office Regulation Bill, drafied by the National Union of Clerks and introduced into the House of C ammo DB by Mr. C. W. Bowerman, M.P." At the Septomber meeting the Secretary was instructed by the members of the Wallington Branch to welts to the local M.P.'s to ask it ttey would support the second reading of the II;11. The following see extracts from the replies : Mt. Harold Smith, M.P. : I can assure you thvt I will approach with just sympathy any measure which tends to alleviate the lot of the clerk. I will give the the measure my very close attention. Colonel Alan Sykes, M.P. : The Office Regulation Bill will byre my earnest consideration.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>Examiner Saturday October 14, 1911</head>
        <p>The Examiner Saturday October 14, 1911</p>
        <p>LPH,tINES ,1 OFF THE BEATEN TRACK</p>
        <p>IN SHROPSHIRE.</p>
        <p>A FAMOUS OUTLAW.</p>
        <p>It was on the last day of last year, and finding myself in Shrewsbury I decided upon an afternoon's cycle run to Ellesmere a little- visited corner of Shropshire. I had intended taking the Wem road as far as Harmer Hill, ' and then turning to the left past Myddle and through Cockshutt, but as the roads were rapidly becoming in good condition, I decided to go by a more circuitous route. Crossing the Severn by the Welsh Bridge and pro- ceeding up what we call the Mount,' and which is a mount in reality, I found m7self once more on Telford's great road to Holy- head. I soon arrived at Shelton. _ _</p>
        <p>A FAMOUS OAK TREE. At this junction of the roads, in some private grounds to the right, is the famous Shelton Oak, from which Owen Glendower is supposed to have watched the progress of the battle of Shrewsbury. I entered the interior of the trunk of this giant oak, which is hollow to the very top, say about 30 feet from the ground, which, by the way, is paved with small cobble stones. The out- side is covered with a fine growth of ivy, which, with the aid of a stout oak prop at one side, will probably help to keep the tree in position for many years to come. The top post has the appearance of the head of some antediluvian monster with horris and looks</p>
        <p>—.— rather weird even in the day time. After leaving Shelton and continuing on 1 the Oswestry Road there is a good view of the Severn Valley to the right, and Berwick Hall is plainly visible in an elevated position on the opposite bank of the river, with its well-wooded background, including Harmer Hill. I could also see Grinshill and the spire of the Clive Church in the distance, and on the left the road follows the boundary of Onslow Park. Passing Bicton Church the road descends to the level of the river, which it crosses at Montford Bridge, 41/2 miles from Shrewsbury. Here a good view of the Shropshire hills to the left, and the Breiddens in front, backed by the long line of Welsh Hills, is to be obtained. I passed through a pleasant open country, and at eight miles from Shrewsbury I came to the fringe of the wooded hill of Nesscliff, the home and dwelling of the famous outlaw Humphrey Kynaston, of whom it was said and sung.' He takes from the rich, and gives to the poor. And he lives in the cave</p>
        <p>over yonder! HUMPHREY_ KYNASTON, OUTLAW.</p>
        <p>Briefly his pedigree is as follows: 'Sir Roger Kynaston, keeper of Knockyn and Myddle castles, married as his second wife Elizabeth, daughter of Henry Grey, Earl of Tankerville. Both Grey and Kynaston were Yorkists, and at the Battle of Blore Heath the Lancastrian general James, Lord Audley of the Red Castle, fell to the hand of Roger Kynaston, and both he and Grey compounded for their lives by the forfeiture of their estates in the short Lancastrian triumph. The younger son of Sir Roger Kynaston was Wild Humphrey, who remained as tenant of Myddle Castle after the death of his father, ' till, in the time of Henry VII. he was compelled to leave his home and seek a refuge here. He was outlawed in 1491, but whether the cause was debt or politics is not known.' AND lUS WONDERFUL STEED.</p>
        <p>I was told that formerly there was a quarry here, and that Wild Humphrey was so kind to the men working there that they helped him to make the cave in the face of the rock, where he and his wonderful horse resided, and they also made the 70 steps leading up to it, and protected and shielded him from his pursuers as far as they were able. Like Dick Turpin he was possessed of a horse which, when a colt, he saw jump over its mother's back, and its shoes were always put on backwards so as to conceal the direction in which he rode. There is no doubt it was a wonderful horse, but as time wore on its capabilities became so magnified that it became a magic animal on which Kynaston had jumped from Nesscliff to Breidden Hills, and an old inhanitant remembers that when a child the children were afraid to pass the cave for fear they encountered this miraculous steed. THE CAVE. _ _ .</p>
        <p>The way to the cave is opposite the little inn, itself marking the spot where Fulke Fitz-Warine slew Mende Fitz-Roger de Pow is in the reign of King John. Here a lane turns up, and taking a turn to the right, and then a path up the wood to the left, one is soon at the cave. A long flight of steps cut in the red sandstone leads to the entrance of the cave, which is in two compartments, the outer said to have been the stall of the , famous horse. Two niches, for larder use, are the only sign of sculpture. Wild Kynas ton's seat, taken from this cave, passed into the possession of Mr. John Doveston, of West Felton, Owestry. To prevent disappointment visitors should write beforehand for permission to inspect the hill and the cave. Personally, I think the narrow road lead- ing past the old post office would be the Shrewsbury-road in those old stirring times. This road, peaceful enough now, had then an evil reputation. There is a dim tradition that an earlier outlaw used to issue from this cave with his horse's shoes reversed, and fall upon his victims under the hill. The Journey under the hill was so dangerous that the Drapers' Company ordained in 1583 that no draper should set out for Oswestry on Monday before six o'clock, on forfeiture of 6s. Bd., and that they should be armed, and go in company. A farm house near Nesscliff was formerly the Wolf Head Inn, a famous resort of highwaymen. The red stones of which Shrewsbury Castle is built came from Nesscliff. Leaving this interesting neighbourhood I passed through West Felton, and at 14 miles from Shrewsbury I reached the Queen's Head where there is a junction of roads, and here I turned right, and following the line of the canal as far as Rednal Station, with bog plantations on either side, I came to the hamlet of Rednal with its large pool and isolated houses, with Woodhouse Hall and grounds to the left. I had a fine ride ol 15 miles through Hordley, in the fading light, and reached the Saxon town of Ellesmere on the edge of dark. I' la</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>LICENSEE'S FATAL FALL.</head>
        <p>LICENSEE'S FATAL FALL.</p>
        <p>Coroner Ridgeway conducted an inquiry at Runcorn, on Monday afternoon, into the circumstances attending the death of John Edwards, licensee of the Mariner's Hotel, Percival's-lane. From the evidence of deceased's son it appeared the man had been subject to epileptic fits. On Friday evening be went down Into the cellar to turn out the gas after the house was closed. A noise , was heard, and he was discovered lying at the bottom of the cellar Maps with a wound in the bead. Ile died without recovering full consciousness. Dr. M'Lerman said that the cause of death was fracture of the base of the Stull. It was quite probable that the man bad slit when coming op the steps.—A verdict of Accidental death" was returned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>LOCAL PARLIAMENT.</head>
        <p>LOCAL PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>DEBATE ON THE KING'S SPEECH CONCLUDED.</p>
        <p>The Industrial Unrest.</p>
        <p>The excellent spirit which has so far chaeoterised the proceedings of the Warrington Parliamentary Debating Society gives primalso of another highly 611CCESIIBH EEFEHOR. There was a large attendance at the adjourned debate on the King's Speech in C tiro- street Church Parlour on Tuesday evening and the many important quasi ions provoke] spirVed discussion, attention being chiefly concentrated upon the unrest in the Labour world. In the absence of Mr. James Ball. the Speaker's chair was occupied by Mr. J. Dermid. The debate was re-opened by Mr. J. BLACK (N. Donee II) who waxed sarcastic at the spooled° recently presented of three-fourths of the rank and file of the Ter): party lustily calling to their leaden "Let brotherly love continue," while the other fourth were saying "Balfour must go." (Laughter). It bad been the serio-comedy of the year. (Laughter) The Leader of the Opposition must be devo'd of a sense of humour when he denounced Irish dictatorship, after the glorious example recently net by those amh-dictators Mr. Austen Chamberlain and Mr. F. E. Smith, who endeavoured to act the parts of CMIFOOK3 and Beaus t Batfoues COMET. (Laughter). The King's Speech embodied a programme of immense importance end contained probably the oest and noblest series of social 1 reforms that had ever lees submitted to the Hausa. Contrast that progremme of sound, p:actieal and senslble reform with the airy nothingne s of the pro- gramme submitted the other night by the gentleman who wan described by the Conservative National Review" es the champion scuttler ! (Laughter) Dealing eith foreign affairs, the speaker ridiculed the Leader of the Opposition's assumption that our present peaceful relations were due to the aratchinlnesl of bin party. He hoped the Government would take the earliest opportunity of entering a strong protest against the grave wrong which WES being done to Turkey in the present crisis. (Hear. hear.) As to home policy he contended that in ell fairness the claims of Ireland should have first consideration. For thirty years they had fought the battle of Hume Rule through good and evil report and hai plated a pram'- , sent part in educating the democracy of Great Britain I as to the advantages of self-governmeneTbe Leader of 1 the Opposition had the idea that to secure a true bond of anion in the Empire they timid refuse Home Rule. 1 All history was against him on that point. In Germeny, which the Opposition so greatly admi red, over twenty federal Parliaments existed. (Applause). Mr. N. Nome (C., Newton) said the unrest in the Industrial world indicated widespreed discontent which It won imperative should be remedied at all costs if we wane to retain our position as a civilized ration and a leading manufacturing coultry. A house divided against itself could not semi nor could a country expert to 'attain that succees which they all desired unless the relations between capital alai %hour were amicable and harmonious. No-one would deny that the conditions of life were immeaserably superior to what obtained fifty years ago and yet they foes] the workers full of resentment and dinaontent. Its was not surprised at that, fir he die not think the principle of class hatred bad ever been preached no much as they had been during the lest few peers. The workers had been led to believe that they had been robbed at every turn, that they were the producers or creators of all weelth. (Hear, hew.) They had been induce] to look upon the capitalist as an inhuman monster, desirous of nothing but sucking their life's blood, and he regretted to say that responsible Ministers of State had been guilty of snob conduct. There could be only one result —end that KM to irillame the passions of those who felt they had wrongs, whether real or imaginary, and to suggest wrongs where previously none existed. It was a significant co-incidence that with the int racism- Gott of the recent type of legislailon,cheon and disorder had prevailed throuehont the country. (Ministerial Inughtere Whore len efajesty's advisers we-e wrong was in pursuing a theoretical policy of cure instead of seep' ing the more effective course of prevention. By their intention to bring in a sickness and invalieity Bill, they accepted poverty as a necessary factor of our everyday life. He had always been under the Repro, sion that the ideal of democracy was a State where poverty wee absolutely unknown. Yet they found the I Government putting a premium upon it. (" No, no.") I He would like to see bon. members opposite appealing 1 to something higher than the cupidity of the people., If we had to make progress on the lioes of Christianity selflahness would have to be fought tooth and n (Hear, hear.) The last two so:Aeneas of the King's I Speech were worthy of an eleborate frame : " A Bill 1 to provide a gooier measure of wit-government to , England, Scotland, Ireland and Wales. God save the ' King." Ile was afraid no mortal would be able to save the King, ifs Bill like that became law. (Laughter.) Why not give Horne Rule to each county or carry the idea to its logical absurdity and make every 1 man slew unto himself? (Laughter). Couucillor HAWTHORN (Home Secretary) assured ;the House that the "resurrection pie" which the Lender of the Opposition had scoffed at would contain very dainty morsels which it would have been a pity to leave on the waste heap. They ought certainly to rescue from oblivion the Scottish Land end \retention Bill, the Abolition of Plural Voting, the Licensing ism, the Home Rule Bill and other beneficial measures which they had "loved long sinoe and lost awhile." (Laughter.) They would have grave misgivings if there was not a glorious resurrection of those slaughtered innocents. The members for Truro (Rev. E. C. E. Carleton) had expressed surprise that there was no mention in the King's Speech of the acceptance of the Veto Bill by the Lorde. The explanation was that the King's Speech usually foreshadowed legislation, and did not deal with what had gone before. The Government dist not share the rev, gentleman's doleful prophecies. They were content to trust the people and the people's representa- tives. (Applause and Question.") What had the 1 Veto Bill done to call forth his maledictions ? It had , simply placed in the bands of Mr. kquith rmd bin colleagues the power which Mr. Balfour and the leaders of the Conservatives had had for generations. The Lords' Veto was never exercised when the Conservatives were in power. Having referred to the precedent of 1832 for threatening the creation of Peers as a last resort, the speaker said the Veto Bill was designed to prevent the misuse of a privilege which had previously operated against the people and in the interest of the landlord class. (Applause) Ile referred to the numerous Enclosure Acts and to the way in which the pnblic had been robbed of common land. Mr. Lloyd-George had been called a robber and other suggestive titles, but bisection with regard to the land was a restitution to the people of what should never have been taken from them. (Applause.) The member for Truro was no doubt hitting at free imports when be said cheap goods meant cheap labour. His argument was self-destructive, for it must mean that wages on the Continent were lower than in England, and yet those Protected countries were constantly held span examples to Free Trade Britain. The British workman had higher wages, his hours of work were shorter, and his spending power was higher than obtained in any Coot i nen'al country. The simmering discontent amongst the English workers meant thin they believed the wealth they were helping to produce was not apportioned in a fair menner. The Tory perty were full of sympathy towards the workers, but their sympathy ended in words. They opposed old-age pensions and now they posed as if they had given them. (Laughter.) Some of them who were now opposing National Insurance would no doubt in years to come relate how they helped to secure it. (Laughter.) Mr. HARTLEY (C Skipten), said the Tories were accused of want of trust in the pimple, but they were willing to submit many questions to the referendum, whereas the Liberals opposed that opportunity being given to the people to express their opinion. The Liberal opposition to the House of Lords was doe to the fact that that assembly had a nasty habit of referring things to the people—Gaughter)—and on one or two occasions the people had decided against the Liberals. Pesceful picketing. he contended, was largely responsible for the state of mob law whiela recently prevailed. He hoped shortly to be able to congratulate the Government on the repeal of the Trades Disputes Act—a stop which would make it illegal for any men to be denied the right to work if he could find work to do. At present half a dozen auto- cratic Socialists could dictate to the whole of the workers as to when they should stop work, anti soy individual who wished to work must do so at the risk of his life. (Laughter). He agreed that trade unions were a neoersary check on unscrupulous employers. They were a tonic to the Industrial body, but when the body attempted to live on theism it was in a worse state then before. As a solution of the " recognition" difficulty, he suggested that the men should be allowed to employ outside aleistance—say • barrister—who could place their case explicitly before the masters, without having any axe of his own to grind. Mr. H. New (President of the Lmal Government Board) said the opposition to the King's Speech bad been of the milk and water type. Although practically every measure in it had been criticised, no alternative proposals had been advanced. It was absolutely untrue for the member for Truro to suggest that the conse- (moon of the abolition of the Lords' Veto were not placed before the conntry at the last election. The Tories never tired of telling the electors that the retorts of the Liberal party would mean Home Rule for Ireland and the swamping of the Pears. A very large per- estrange of the English counties elrendy had Hoare R rile but it wants the hoses of a certain few. The iotimida- lion and serfdom that prevailed in some of the southern connties was almost unbearable. Mr. Ilarold Smith, M.P., recently attributed the Labour unrest to Mr. Lloyd George's speech at Limehonse. Never was a more glaring mis-statement tittered on a politicel platform. Toe railway companies were always pleading poverty and grinding down the workers on that pretext, but that year they paid £1e,000,000 in di•ideods. The condition of many of the railway employees to-day wen degrading in the extreme. When plural voting bad been abolished, Liberals would have nothing to fear in the counties where they were almost Invariably beaten by the plural voters.</p>
        <p>Mr. R. PERIM:ANT (1., N. Rut Manchester) said he was proud of the Liberals when they were in the weak- ness of an Opposition party, but he was sorry for them in their power. (Laughter.) Never had a Government a better chance of doing really sound, practical work but they had simply fostered class hatred and attacks, on capitalism. Lie was also sorry for the Conservatives in that house—ho never heard a weaker lot. (Laughter.) Bad as the Government were, he did not think the Con- servatives would ever beat them. (Laughter.) The members who introduced the kings Sperch spent all their time in talking of higher things. They might have been in a prayer meeting. (Laughter.) The Government dhl not emit the hicts, be-. dodged round them every time. If the Veto Bill WAD right, why waste time defendieg it now? The Government were a poly of Free Traders and yet they modelled their insurance scheme on the same lines as Protootionist Germany. The measures passed by the Govern-nest had not dne toe good for the moss of the people that was claimed for them. Who was paying th 3 lend tines? Tears could be no doubt that the wealthly laudowaer would aid the tax to his selling price and it would mean increase, rents. Tent was broadening the basis of taxation. (Laughter.) The Government professed to ben party of social reformers, but he called them a party of meddlers and muddlers. (Laughter.) So long as baleen nature remained as it is, they would never get internati snel peace. As Bismnrck said, God was on the side of the big battalions, and they bad to k sep their fighting forces ready. Mr. S. A. H. ITICKEY (C., N. Fermuitgh) poiuted out that the ttndical pithy had made no attempt to Glee with the alien questi so an I E Wend co thinued to be the dust heap of Eurape, where diseased, ii ith and vocally people could come without hindrance. The Government only tried to expel them with flue engines and gutrdamee. (Laughter.) With regard to the con- stitutional question, the preamble of the Veto Bill was a mere falsehood and the ij,vernunest halo, iuteation of noting upon it. Mr. P. O'llairer (Chief Secretary for Ireland) said hi,. Huai I Smith, M.l'. in a race It speech had alluded to the injustice of the trs of great no iicipehstien being voted epee in Parliament by mea who had no koowleige of or inaere it in them. That argument applied Neatly to Ireland and to strengthen the case for Home Rule. Every matter ietroducsd by the Nationalist patty for the last thirty years had hoes oonseded by Lire Conservatives. They .passed the Irish Land 11)11, not because they were anxious to doss hut because they were compelled by the trend of circumstances. The decentralization of legislative work would undoubtedly prove a gent benefit not only to Ireland but to the Empire as a whole. In the worms of Sir Wilfred Laurier, local ant momy bad not in the slightest degree hindered Imperil) unity in the colonies but had tended to promote it. The speaker laid stress upon the happier relations which now obtained between the English an the Irish as compared with say thirty years ago. To-day eren the opponents of flame Rule were not anti-Irish. They opposed it because they were afraid it would interfere with the general well-being of the E npire. Their anxiety on that score was, however, a ,warranted Mr .1 L Warburton (C. W. Bristol), emphtsised the necessity fora re-adjustment of 10Jal rating and said that Co-operative Societies, which were slowly but surely killing private enterprise, should be made to pay &amp;wordier,' to the extent of their hu 330033. Mr. .LEs.werd. (First Lord of the Admiralty) said the workers had a perfect right to picket, in their own interests. Weald the Conaervatives, who wished to abolish that right, also debar employers from scouring the country to find black-leg labour ? It was untrue to suggest that the Labour leaders delighted in foster- ing dissensions and usrest. They were to a man in favour not of strikes, but of conciliation and orhitra- tion. It WAn only when a strike was forced up RI them that they resorted to that barheroua teethed. In 99 cases out of (0) tha brealting ewer, from sliding males and conciliation schemes Was on the part of employers. re hov did not alto er intimidati ea—(Opposition laughter)—but only peaceful picketing, and if any picket attempted to coerce any workm in who desired to enter a works he rendered hr Melt Ii tble ton penalty. The address to tha Throne won tied carried with one dissentient.</p>
        <p>Next Tuesday evening a Bill to provide for a Readjustmmt ot Lc d Rating will be introduced by the Rt. lion, the Chanctllor of the Exchequer, Mr..), Jarman, Member for Burton-on-Trent.) Its provisions areas follows : CLAUSE I.—Ac IMORITIES. For the purposes of Rating the Authorities shall be for London, the London County Commil and the rates shall be uniformly levied over the whole of their administrative area. For the remainder of the United Kingdom the various Boroughs and Parish Councils shall be the Rating Authorities and the Overseers of the Pcrir shall cease t act in this capacity. CrAtIE 2.—MUNIcIitAL EXI•EN4ES. All Profits derived frown Gas, Water, Trams, Electricity or any other Municipal Properties (except- ing as hereinafter provided for bind) shall in future after all charges for intewest on Capital Outlay, Depreciation of Plant, Upkeep, Wages, Ac. have been met b 3 used for the purptee of a reduction in the various charges for etese services and shall act be taken for the purposes of reducing general rates. CIAIISE 3. —METHOD or COLLDTIoN. All rates shall in future be made for one year in advance, and shall be collected in instalments in March and September. They shall be payable by the tenant on all properties that are let on lease or for any period of three months or over. Where property is let on a weekly tenancy the amount of the rats shall be payable by the Landlord, but shall be recoverable by him from the tenant is weekly instal- ments in addition to the rent, and an allowance of 21 per cent, shall be made to the Landlord on all such payments for loss of interest and collection. ShLuld a tenant be in arrears of Rent and Rates, it shall he the duty of the Rating Authority to release the Land- lord from his obligation and take proceedings direct against the tenant on receiving written notice from the Landlord of inability to obtain rent and production to the said Authorities of the required proofs. The Amount of the Rates Collected by the Landlord shall ' he the amounts levied from time to time by the Rating Authority, and shall be separately stated in the Rent Book of the Tenant CLAUSE 4.-- VALUATION. The Rating Authorities shall ascertain. (a) The Annual Value of all Lands within their Authority, whether occupied by Buildings or not, including all waste lands apart from all Buildings or other erections thereon. Such annual value shall be the Ground Rent payable thereon or where no such Rent is payable, the value declared by the owner, or should he decline to fix such value the valuation placed upon it by the rating authority ; providing that in all cases where Land is laid out by the Owners for Building purposes the rating authorities shall in fixing their valuation make allowance for all such improvements unless and until the land is built. upon. Public recreation grounds, open without fee, shall be exempt from all rates. (b) The Annual Value of all Buildings, Cottages, Shops, Warehouses, Railway Premises, tie., now subject to rates, excluding only places of Worship and premiers connected therewith that are exclusively used for the purpose of public worship or to obtain funds for carrying on such worship. (01 The Annual Value of the Fittings, Machinery and Plant erected in such Buildings. CLAUSE S.—RATINti. The Rating Authority shall levy a Rate to be called the "District Rate" for the purposes of Public Light- ing, Paving, Cleansing, Sewering, Baths and Libraries and the expenses connected therewith which shall be chargable as follows : ON LAND. (a) 2-5 of the total sum required for the purposes of this rate on the Annual Value d all Lands as ascertained in accordance with Clause 4 A. The rate shall be charged to the occupier, who shall (if he is not the owner of the lande) be entitled to deduct the amount paid from the Rent Payable f .r such land and no agreement to transfer the liability in any way shall he legal. ON BeII.DINGH. (b) 2-5 of the total aim required on the Annual Value of all Buildings Ac, as ascertained in accordance with Clause 4 ON FIn'ING,,, Mo lINEKY. (e) 1-3 of the total sum required on the Annual Value of Fittings, Machinery, Sm., as ascertained in accordance with Clause 4 c. CLAUSE 6.—Pooa RATE. A special rate to be called the " Poor and Police Rate shall be levied for the purposes of the Relief of the Poor, Workhouses, Infirmarirs, Ifoepitale, Asylums, Police expenses and the feeding of neeeseitous children in proportions of "; and as 'tinder (a) Three fourths of the total required for the above services shall be provided by a percentage on the Sales of all Intoxicating Liquors, whether in Public Houses, Hotels, Clubs, Refreshmeut Bars or any other retail business, and no Intoxicating Liquors shall be sold other than through such businesses (except as hereinafter provided.) And it shall be the I duly of the proprietor or manager of every such bustneee to furnish a tt tie return of all ranch sales or in default he ahall pay a fine of not less than .Cl) and shall not be again allowed to condo such a business in the United Kingdom, providing that The Rate payable on Intoxicating Litoors sold on Steamships and other vessels and on any train or other Vehicle in the United Kingdom, where such Liquors are now or may be gold shall be paid t tl.e Government Exchequer and Aril be used for the purpoHce of the relief of unemployment. The rate shall be the average for the United Kingdom as ascer- tained from the 1 cal rating authorities.</p>
