      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>SHIP CANAL COLLISION.</head>
        <p>A SHIP CANAL COLLISION.</p>
        <p>Claim for Damages by Messrs.</p>
        <p>Crosfield and Sons.</p>
        <p>Sitting "iii Admiralty" in the Mancheater Coonty Cott, ton Tuesday, his Honour Judge Mellor K.C., :with the assistance of two nautical assessors, heard an I ncLion in which damages were claimed by Messrs. Joseph Crosfleld and Sons, Limited, owners of the steam barge S, a Swallow, from the owners of thv steamship Princess Ens, now in the Pomona Dock of the Manchester Ship Cann!. The assessors were Captain E .1 Woodbill and Lieutenant Partridge, of the Royal Naval Reserve. Mr A T Miller, instrocted by Messrs Alexander Wilson and Cowie, of Liverpool, was counsel for the claimants : ani Mr A R Kennedy, instruCted by Messrs Hill, Dickinson. and Co., of Liver- . . . .</p>
        <p>pool, for the respondents. Mentioning that the claim wee on- for damages sustained in a collision in the Ship Canal, Mr,M liter at once called evidence. . . . .</p>
        <p>Joseph 'Leigh, master of the Sea Swallow, described hie vessel as one of 53 tons net register, carrying a crew of a mate and engineer besides himself. At a quarter to ten on the night of February Bth last he left Warrington with a cargo of soap and chemicals bound down the Canal to Birkenhead. The weather was tine and clear, and there was only a light breeze. They were carrying all the regulation lights at the mast-head, side, and stern. All went well until about a mile below Saltport, when they came up with the steamship Largo, which was fixed right across the Canal. In response to an rppeal for assistance witness helped another vessel called the Joseph Thompson to tarn the Largo round with her head towards Liverpool, but this resulted in the Largo getting ao lined along the embankment aa to be unable to use her propeller. Ile then pro• e,eeded to pull her off the bank by means of a rope. They had seen the lights of the Princess Ens, which was a large vesael, schen about a mile away, but they never dreamt that that vesael would keep on her way in the manner thst she did, when there was no room for her to pars. Seeing what was likely to occur the crew of the Largo ran to the stern of their vessel and shouted repeatedly, but the Princess Ena continued on her way and struck the Sea Shallow just forward of amidships. There were two m:n on Ste starboard bow of the Princess Ena who should have given their captain an. alarm, but who said nothing es far as Ise could tell, The Sea Swallow was cut to about six inches ahoy. water-line, and the knocking down of a lamp set tbs cabin on fire. Thinking that the vessel was strikine he shouted something to the effect to the menus th Princess Ena, but got no answer. The Princess Eta went straight ahead at full speed. Cross-examined by Mr. Kennedy, the witness denied that the collision was due to the Sea Swallow suddenly emerging from between the bank and the Largo, and in front of the bow of the Princess Ena. Ile admitted he did not know all the rules of the Ship Canal. If it wore a role for a reatel aground to show three rod lights the Largo did not show them. Instead of giving along and a short whistle on approaching, the Princess Ena gave two long ores. It was then impossible for the Sea Swallow to avoid the collision, owing to the Largo, in going ahead, pulling at the rope end dragging her more across the Canal. It might have been wise if, when the Princess Ens whistled, he had given an answering whistle, but it could not have prevented ths . _ _</p>
        <p>----- George Roy, nut's of tie Sea Swallow, gave corroborative evidence; as did the engineer of the Sea Swallow and the pilot of the Largo. In reply to the Judge, Mr. Miller said that the question to be decided was whether the collision wee calmed Ly the negligence of one party or of both. Under Admiralty roles, if the Court found negligence on both side., each had to bear half of the other's damage. . . . . .</p>
        <p>Captain Kerr, Of the Patens Knit, said that that vessel was owned by Messrs. Lanalands and Sons, and traded between Manchester and Glasgow. He saw the Largo when about a quarter of a mile away and brought his vessel to a stop. The captain of some peening vessels told him he could pass the Largo, and he accordingly rowtarted, bin whistle blowing all the time. lle did not seethe Sea Swallow mail he got • hundred feet beyond the stern of the Largo. Then she came out in front of the bow of the Largo and across his path, with the result that a collision was unavoidable. The Princess Ens was not injured at all. At the time of the collision the Largo was still aground. He beard no hails from the Largo, and got no notice from his own look-out until he himself maw the S.. Swallow. Mr. Kennedy pointed out that before the collision whilst the Niece's Ens had adhered closely to the Canal regulations, the Largo and Sea Swallow had broken several by not ringing a bell whilst aground, by not exhibiting three red lights, and by failing to give a whistle indioating that they bad ceased to be aground and were going ahead. The Sea Swallow was therefore at leant partly to be blamed for the collision. He also submitted that a vessel working at snot per in a narrow channel in a dark night must look out and keep clear of passing vessels. The Judge said that the asaeranra agreed with him that if the story of the waster of the Sea Swallow were correct he saw the approach of the Princess Kn. from a distance and should at once have avoided the collision by getting out of the way. He, therefore, gave judgment for tbe deendenta, w i.h costa.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>THANKS TO WIDNES.</head>
        <p>THANKS TO WIDNES.</p>
        <p>Our thanks are due to the many local residents who speak out on matters of great moment. The outspoken Widnrs statement we are able to give to day will echo , the experience of many here. • . .</p>
        <p>Mr. D. Bowen. who resides at 36, Viaduct Street, West Bank, Widnes (quite near to the West hank Schools) has given us the following account of bin experience. Be says : " Fora long time I suffered from Kidney trouble an] rheumatism. I had acute pains in my legs and arms, and tometimes I was so bad that I was obligtd to take to my bed. , The backache pains just over the kidneys were of a sharp cutting rsture. and would take me suddenly if I bent down at all. The kidney excretions, too, were irregular, there being a seedy sediment in them. " I commenced taking Dean's Backache kidney pills a few months ago, however, and since I went through a good course of them I have been absolutely free of all pain. Thaw pills, right from the very start, gave me relief and I feel it is only my duty to tell others the good I have derived from Doan's pills. My opinion of them is a very high one. (Signed) D. Bos Es." 9J. a box, 6 boxes 13e. Od. ; of all dealers. Ask for DOAN'S backache lidasy pill., like Mr. Bowe, bad.</p>
      </div>
