      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <p>MIMI</p>
        <p>The Examiner Saturday, October 14, 1911</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>OUR SERIAL STORY.</head>
        <p>OUR SERIAL STORY.</p>
        <p>[ALL RIGHTS RESERVID3.</p>
        <p>THE FOUNTAIN OF BEAUTY</p>
        <p>L. T. MADE, Author of "The Rebellion of Lil Carrington." "A</p>
        <p>Sweet Girl Graduate," &amp;e.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XXI V.—(Conillice4.)</p>
        <p>One day wnen eke was out, • lady met her, who told her that R friend of hers who was staying at the Carlton, saw the Mirza with all his belongings and his vast retinue, leave the hotel about an hour after Rebecca arm Lady Jane had done so, en route for the Con tinent.</p>
        <p>Beyond doubt," said the lady, "that great, interesting, dark, inscrutable man is no longer in London. Ehatever mysterious designs he had upon poor, dear old Lady Jane have come to naught we shall pro bably hear nothing more about him." Rebecca, on getting this information, went back to Lady Jane, told her what had occurred, and said that she was about to return to 9, Paddock Row, that same day. "But is it safe, my love?" said the old lady, you can never be up to the wiles of those Eastern.. The man may be in London all the time."</p>
        <p>"I don't think so," said Rebecca. "Any- buw, I am wanted at home, and must go there. I can never thank you enough for all your great goodness to me." 'that evening Rebecca went back to the small house where she had spent her early pitith. She found two letters awaiting her, one with • foreign postmark, the other evi- dently posted from a remote part of Scot- land. Her heart beat as the two letters were put into her hand. • _</p>
        <p>"They both arrived this morning," said Lucy, "and oh! it's Cook and me who are glad to have you back again. And shall 1 give you tea in the drawing-room?" Rebecca went there. Row cheerful and homelike was the little room ! How glad and thankful she was to be home once more!</p>
        <p>Lucy brought in the tea, and then her mistress to herself. Rebecca scarcely noticed the pretty and brightly polished tea equi- page. Her eyes were fixed on the bold hand- writing of that letter which hailed from the north of the British Isles. She tore it open eagerly. It ran as follows:</p>
        <p>I 'feel that I cannot keep silent any longer. I have been here, well -and safe, for more than ten days. Nothing has occurred id the least remarkable. Your dear old ser- vant, Jean Duncan, received me with open aims. I have never once found it necessary to take possession of that hiding place which you suggested. I am now becoming hardened to the sense of danger, and, in short. must .dn the risk of writing to you in order to find out how things are. I can never, as long as I live, forget your goodness to me, nor how nobly you have helped me at the risk of per- m nal danger. I may as well tell you here that riches, as riches, are a matter of indif- ference to me, as I believe they are to you; and my one desire is that the terrible in- cubus which has seemed to come between us should bis buried in the depths of the sea.— Write to me if you can and dare.—Yours ever, with all faithfulness,</p>
        <p>GERALD LONEDALE."</p>
        <p>Rebecca read the letter once ;she read it twice. Than a deep sigh of thankfulness es- caped her lips. How marvellously easy the Whole thing had been! The simmicity of the inspiration had ensured its success. Old Jean Duncan had been her nurse a long time ago. Jean's lonely cottage and the caves by the seashore, the higher part of which were never encroached on by the tide, would afford a safe refuge. It was only to get Gerald Lonsdale there to practically ensure his safety. For, if the worst came to the worst, he could fight for his life at one of those narrow openings, and by no possibility could he be taken unawares.</p>
        <p>Gerard Lonsdale had gone to Scotland. Jean Duncan had received him, as Rebecca knew she would, with open arms. Ten days had passed by, but there was no news; there was no one in pursuit; the exciting chase had to all appearances come to an end . "Has it?" thought the girl. "Is this in reality only the calm before the storm? I don't know; I don't know; I have felt im- pelled of late to do certain things. To-day I felt impelled to come home. But is the Mimi hiding in London? It would be like him to do so; and yet, somehow, I do not believe it. I have • feeling deep down in my heart that our time of fearful anxiety is over." Rebecca was so much excited by the con- tents of the letter from Scotland that she</p>
        <p>quite forgot the foreign letter which lay on her lap. Now, however, her eyes rested on it. She started, changed colour, and her band began to tremble violently. She knew the writing. This letter was from Mary Deeps, and Rebecca could distinguish on the envelope the postmark—Lyons. She had a great dread over her as she held the un- opened letter in her hand. She began to fear some new misfortune. Even the memory of Mary was now exceedingly distasteful. Summoning courage, however, to her aid, she did tear open the envelope, and soon all her attention and all her thoughts were ab- sorbed in the closely-written words. "8, Rue de Sevres, Lyons. "Rebecca, you may as well know the worst and the best. We have all, in a measure, failed, but at least you and I, my husband, and that man Lonsdale have escaped with our lives, which, under the circumstances, is _ _</p>
        <p>much to be wondered at. I speak of myself first, as the most important person to me. I am • poor woman, but I am at least sound in health and limb. I have not committed the ghastly crime which I was almost entrapped into doing, and I am with Arnold. Arnold's future and mine need not concern you. We have • little, a very little, money still left; and when this letter reaches you, will be out of Europe. Now, I mean to tell you the end of an exciting story, and as there is no ape- cial need for further reticence, I will speak freely. "My dear Rebecca, when I found that Nadir Julaver had played that abominable trick upon Arnold and myself of substituting the false diamond for the real, I threw my- self on the mercy of another man, namely, the enemy of Nadir Julaver—Mirea Kara Fagot. The Mirsa supplied me with money to fellow Nadir Julaver, and gave instruc- tions that I was to put myself under the com- plete guidance of a Persian servant of his called Seleucus. I reached Marseilles, and there discovered that there was no Massage- re boat to take me on to Batum—the first stepping place en route for Teheran—for nearly a week. I was, therefore, obliged to stay at Marseilles for several days. "While I was waiting on the quay a dark- looking man appeared, whom I recognised immediately as one of the Minas servants— not so much by his face, which was carefully got up to imitate the face of a 'Frenchman, as by his gait, and—well, I need not trouble you with my reasons for knowing him. I saw that he was watching me, and that fact also, perhaps, roused my suspicions; for, my dear Rebecca, I am, as you know, not a pretty woman. I took off the glove from my left hand, and hie eyes immediately lighted upon a little ring which the Mires had put on my smallest finger. That ring was to be our sig- nal for recognition. He came up to me at once and spoke, and, in short, we made our plans. I need not trouble you about them; suffice it to say that this man, Seleucus by name, the Mina's confidential servant, was also seeking for • passage to Batum. He also was disappointed at the delay, for no boat would leave for that special port until the following Tuesday. The day we met was Wednesday. We therefore made our plans. "I took rooms at • hotel in Marseilies, by no means first-class, but • large rambling old house which was half empty, and must have seen—to use the old proverb—better days. My first object was to beguile the Nadir into the same hotel. His boat was expected to reach the quays at Marseilles on that same evening. Beleueus and I took spacious rooms for him—oa spec', my dear. I got an omni- bus from the hotel to come clown to the quays. I net the Nadir quite, as it were, 131 stectdent. ead...mantiosted the buts!, where</p>
        <p>was staying. •Hi -walks;cl Into the Leap very neatly, and that night he and I alept under the came roof.</p>
        <p>"A day or two passed, and I got into con- versation with him. I told hire I was going to Batum to my husband—a few tarrididdles 'sore or less did not matter, my dear Re- becca. I thought he believed me. Be seemed to, anyhow. By-and-by. I introduced Seleu- cue to him as my friend, a FreLich gentleman nainst ./ iL T.avn. The Nadir was very polite to M. Leto, and we became excellent friends.</p>
        <p>"At last a day was arranged when I, my dear—your little, quiet, prim Mail Grey that was—was to be initiated into the mys- teries of the opium pipe. I can assure you I was quite excited. So was Seleucite, for we had made such a nice little plot. A certain ingredient was to be mixed into the Nadir's opium which would ensure his sinking into a profound repose. We had already got rid of his servant; but that does not matter for my story. Poor Nadir Julaver was quite de- fenceless, but nothing could take away his wonderful self-possession and princely bear. lag. I was told that it would be my office to mix the preparation with the pipe, and I was terribly frightened, and said at one* that if it was a poison I could not do it. Se!oleos told me not to be alarmed, as all the drug would do would be to ensure a very deep sleep on the Nadir. Selemons, in his wonder- ful Eastern way, had discovered the exact place where the great gem lay concealed on the Nadir's person—just in the hollow above the hip. Extraordinary, his penetration! "Well, the day arrived, and tbe evening of the day. I put on my very prettiest dress, and made myself look as attractive as I pos- sibly could, and then I tripped upstairs to the Nadir's great reception room—that dirty old hotel must have been a palace at one time. The Nadir was waiting for me, and he Was dressed with full Eastern magnificence. The room was, of course, furnished in that dull, dirty sort of fashion which made it in itself so unpleasant. But now I scarcely noticed the furniture, so absorbed was I in watching the Nadir and in wondering at the preparations for the opium treat. There were four little lamps in the room, of the queerest shape you can imagine—quite round, with an opening at the top. Each lamp stood on a tray. There were also pipes—four pipes, and long things, sharp at one end, which the Nadir assured me were called dippers. There was also, of course, a bowl full of opium, which was of the consistency of black, stiff treacle. _</p>
        <p>"Nadir inlayer was in his pleasantest mood, and began to explain to me how I must take the opium. My dear, the process was quite extraordinary, and if I had not been so fascinated, I might have been afraid of the ordeal which lay before me. In addi. tion to the four lamps, and four pipes, and the four dippers, and the four trays on which the lamps were placed, were also four very deep armchairs, which might have been almost used as beds, they went back so far. Each chair was arranged close to a lamp. which stood upon its own tray and burned brightly, and on the tray lay, ready for use, the queer-looking opium pipe and the long, pointed dipper. "New. my dear lady,' said the Nadir to me, 'you will require very little opium to give you those pleasurable and delightful dreams which are the opium smoker's para- dise; whereas I and my friends, who are habitual opium smokers, will require several pipes; but you must be satisfied with one, and I shall have the honour of myself pre. paring it far you.' "How will you do it?' I asked. "I shall take your dipper,' he replied, 'and put it into this bowl of opium, twisting it round and round until I get a large drop of the fluid to adhere to the end of the dip- per. Still twisting it round to prevent it falling, I will hold the drop over the flame of the lamp and twirl it round and round in order to roast it. You could not do this, my esteemed friend, properly, for it requires much dexterity. When the drop of opium is roasted, and rolled to my satisfaction, I will gently heat the centre of the bowl of the pipe. Here, you can see for yourself, where there is this small orifice. Then I will thrust the end of the dipper into the orifice, twirl it round sharply, and withdraw it. A bit of opium, the size of a grain of hemp seed, will remain in the orifice. When this is done, you will put the stem of the pipe to your lips, and hold the bowl of the pipe over the lam, The heat will cause the opium to frizz and you will take three or four deep inhalations. That will be quite sufficient for you on the first occasion of your using the opium pipe, my dear madame. Thank you,' I answered, when he had done speaking. 'Thus all sounds very in- teresting. But, forgive me,' I added, 'I see there are four lamps and four pipes ready. Are, you expecting another guest?</p>
        <p>'You gave Penetrated the truth, gra- cious lady,' he answered. 'I look for a friend from London.' From London, Nadir .Julaver ! ' . .</p>
        <p>'"Tee; a very great friend. He will join us in our pipe of peaoe. We will await his arrival before we enter our paradise.' "The Nadir began to talk to me on indif- ferent matters, desiring me to sit close to my own lamp, my own pipe, my own dipper. After a time, Seleucus came in. He was dressed carefully as a Frenchman. Ilis smart and fashionable dress was made by a well-known Parisian tailor. We all began to talk in the French tongue. Then all of a sudden the Nadir said, 'Will you both excuse me for a moment, I have forgotten something.' He left the room and withdrew into his bed-room, closing the door behind him. The moment he did this Seleueus said:</p>
        <p>" 'Now is our time. The opium and the pipes are ready. Madame, you and I must share the glories of this conspiracy between us. Give me the pips of Nadir Julaver. See where it stands.'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>"I started up., I did .not.dare to disobey.</head>
        <p>"I started up., I did .not.dare to disobey.</p>
        <p>" You are riot going to kill him!' I eaül "He replied with a laugh. 'What I do will produce very deep insensibility.' k He then took a small bottle from his pocket. It wan full of a colourless liquid. He carefully wet the orifice of the pipe with the liquid, and also the mouthpiece. The colourless liquid dried at once, and the pipe looked as it had done before. I returned the pipe to Nadir Julaver's tray. " 'But who is this fourth lamp for?' he said then, 'and for whom is this fourth pipe prepared?' " 'Seleucus,' I said, 'the Nadir is expect- ing a friend from London. He will not begin to smoke the pipe of peace until his friend arrives.'</p>
        <p>" Seleucus considered deeply for • minute, then, with a smile, he crossed th. room, took up the other pipe, and administered the same colourless liquid to the orifice and to the mouthpiece of the pipe. " 'They shall both sleep soundly,' he said. 'They shall not .interfere with us.' . .</p>
        <p>"Seleucus and I were both talking plea- santly in the French tongue when Nadir Jule- ver re-entered' the room. He looked calm and unsuspicious, and sat down in his own chair, near his own lamp and pipe in the attitude of waiting. Suddenly the room door was Bung open, and no less a person than Mirza Kara Tusuf appeared. . . .</p>
        <p>"I never was so startled in my life, but neither Julaver—who I am sure now ex. pected him—nor Seleucus, who certainly did not, showed a vestige of astonishment. Nadii Julaver was the essence of politeness. Mires Kara was equally lofty and gracious. Beleu cue continued to act the part of a Frenctl gentleman to perfection. . .</p>
        <p>w "After • feiw minutes Julaver asked the Mirsa if he would not like to smoke, and it the same moment, with the most indescri. bible courtesy and • sweet smile playing round his features, he substituted his own lamp and tray for those which were close tc the Mina's side.</p>
        <p>" You will honour me,' be said. Ile placed the Mina's lamp and tray by his own side, but a little delay ensued while the Nadir, as he had promised, prepared in) morsel of opium for me. My dear Rebecca. I do not think I ever lived through • more terrifying moment, for his eyes, glittering lika dam aost...saa eauweasi an round his feel</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <p>which told me "that 'he had seen Through Beleucus and myself, took all my courage from me. When I began to smoke, however, all feeling of fear vanished. . .</p>
        <p>" 'Bra; up the contents of the pipe very, very gently madame, and take deep inhala- tions,' said a voice in my ears; and I did so more and more gently in a feebler and feebler fashion, until at last, my dear Re- becca, the whole room became dreamlike. Vieions passed before my eyes. I dropped the pipe and sank back against the cushions. There was such a thing as the ,grekt dia- mond. The 'Fountain of Beauty was to be secured. There was • desperate man whom I, strange and weak as I am, fervently loved. There was immiment danger around me. Yes; failure meant death</p>
        <p>mean t</p>
        <p>life,' and yet I cared for nothing, nothing at all at that moment but sleep. "When I came to myself, Rebecca, every- thing had changed. I was lying in a small, ill-furnished room in a very poor part of Marseilles. The Nadir's apartment had faded away. The Hotel des Ambassadeurs was no longer my dwelling-place. I raised my head, which ached profoundly. A slovenly young Frenchwoman opened the door and came in. Are you better, madame?' she asked. "I eagerly inquired where I was and how I had reached my present quarters. She in- formed that I had been brought to the house about midnight in a state of insensibility; that the landlady had offered me a small room on the condition that if I were not better in the morning she should summon the lector. A gentleman brought you, madame, n a voilure,' said the girl, ' and I make no loubt that he paid madame a handsome sum o receive you; and here's a letter which was obe given to you when you awoke.' She thrust an envelope into my hand. It was a little, twisted note, written in a strange fashion and in the English tongue. " "The barbarians west be able to read thin,' it began. • You are safe—your life is spared. Make no inquiries with regard to the Mires Kara Yusuf or the Nadir .lulaver. They sleep the sleep of the just, and their awakening will be in the Elysium of the great Prophet. As for me-1 go with the 'Fountain of Beauty' to restore it to my master, the Shah of Persia. Any attempt to 'ollow me, or to return to the Hotel des Am- bassadeurs will be accompanied by danger. You hold your life in your hand. Be thankful.'</p>
        <p>"Rebecca, that was all. What happened I rannot say, 1 can never say. I read After- wards au account in one of the papers that two high officials of the Persian Court had been found dead in the Hotel des Ambassa- deurs from the effects of a strong overdose of opium. I was so terrified when I read thin information that I avoided reading any fur- ther papers, and have not done so from that day to now. "Farewell, Rebecca. You and Gerald Lonsdale are safe. The Fountain of Beauty' brought a curse with it. Let it go back to the man from whom it was</p>
        <p>"MARY."</p>
        <p>In consequence rf this letter Gerald Lone- dale received early on the following morning a telegram from 'Rebecca Henshall. "Come back at once," she wired. He Came. They met. She showed him Mary's letter. He read it. Then he looked full at her.. She returned his gaze.</p>
        <p>"Oh, Rebecca!" he could n'it help saving. The deepest crimson dyed her face. '`lte- beeea—is it possible?" he said then. "Have r—fit losing the 'Fountain of Beau ty,' won something of a far higher price?" She made an effort to reply. He clasped her in his arms. _ _</p>
        <p>[TEN END.]</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>The Late Dowager Lady Seefleld.—Scoldand has lost one of its most distinguished hostesses by the death of Caroline, the Dowager Countess of &amp;Afield, who was a close friend of Queen Victoria, and had on occasions, entertained King Edward, King George and Queen Alexandra, at Castle Grant, the most famous of her several Highland residences. She was the daughter of the eleventh Lord Blantyre, who was killed in Belgium in 1830, the year of her birth. As Miss Caroline Stuart, she mirried in 1850, the seventh Earl of Seafleld. He died in 1881, and his only son, the eighth earl died three years later, baying the whole of his estates to his mother the late Dowager Countess These estates, situated in Elgin, Inverness, Moray and Bauff, extend to some 300,000 acres, with a rent roll estimated at about £70,000 a year, and the property has been enormously enriched by the planting of millions of pine trees during the lifetime of the deceased lady. The later earls, as a result of this settlement of the property, succeded to a barren i uteri- lance, except for the title. The present Earl of &amp;A- field is a grand-nephew of the later Dowager-Countess. His father, who came into the title most unexpectedly, worked at one line at an ordinary artisan in New Zea- land, and his widow, who is still living, joined the Salvation Army a few year ago. IheCountess of Seafleld wife of the present peer, is the daughter of a late din- ! tinguished doctor of Christchurch, New Zealand. A Long Night's Bart.—The present Lord Mayor of !London is a strict abstainer from alcohol and Sir , Thomas Crosby, the Lord Mayor-elect, is very nearly one. 'He is over eighty years of age, and a lifelong experience sea medical man, his prescription for keep- ing young is a long night's sleep, little smoking and lean drinking. Most people in vigorous old age have their own ideas auto the secret for keeping young, but these opinions differ au much often going to quite opposite extremes, that they afford no golden rule for younger genroations to follow. Some live to grow I old simply because their parents and grandparents did, and it matters little how careless they may be about !their health, while others live long as it could seem, simply because they are always ailing, sod hang on like ! the creaking door. There are a good many people who ' go to extremes in everything, and who lose more time in bed than they are ever likely to gain in longevity, ! and sleep may be of the healthful and refreshing type, , or it may be otherwise, hut, as a general rule there can be no doubt of the benefit to everybody of a long night's rest. ' Dear Prices a Popular Fallacy.—Professor Penwell in the course of an address at the Loudon School of Economics assured his audience that the present situa- tion in regard to the price of the necessaries of life, was quite normal, that the level was not high, and that it would take ten years before prices at their present rate of increase, equalled those of twenty years ago. We must take it that the professor is not 'spearing without his book, but the unfortunate thing is, that the recent increase in the cost of oomniorlities has been mainly in respect of articles of food. If we regard the price of furoiture, doors, watches, and clocks, with many other useful articles, iucluding many kinds of wearing apparel, it in true that prices in the a4gregate are leas than those of twenty years since, but in case of necessity people can economise with regard to furniture whereas it is impossible for consumers a ith limited means to economise very much with respect to food. On "doing Without—That the public can do some- thing to control prices of daily necessaries is, however, proved by the result of the milk war in London. The price of milk to the general public varies in different parts of the country. In some places it, is as low as 20. per quart, but in London the standard price was 4d., and the failures of the paatures and root crops, owing to the drought, was made the excuse for raising the price to sd. Owing to the scarcity of feed, the farmers were getting 2.1. a gallon more from the dairy- men, end a temporary increase of price to 4jd. to the consumers, would have met the case and been fair. Instead of this it seems to have been felt that " now or never was tire time to permanently raise the price to 5d." In some parts of London this attempt has been met by a boycott of milk, with the result that the dairymen have had to revert to the former charge of 4d. The power of "doing without " is thus seen to be a very potent one in the hands of the public, and tradespeople can hardly complain if, in ciroumstanoes such as thee., it i 8 used in self-defence.</p>
        <p>Autumn Modes.—There is quite • craze at the moment for reversible materials, but it is doubtful if the fashion will be of long duration, therefore, the women who buys a coat and skirt to last the winter— unless she does not objectto looking out of date before the garments have seen their best days—would be well- advised to choose a plaiu material, or one of the new tweeds, cheviots, or friezes, which this season are particularly becoming in deeign, and really charming in colouring. Manufacturers Are said to be feeling the effect of the fashion for narrow dresses. In France a loss of no lees than three million pounds has been sustained, whilst English houses compute that two millions at least represent. their loss. The cotton growers of Jarnalei and the spinhers of Manchester, are also suffering acutely from the lesser demand for the raw material, and the manufactured goods, yet this summer must have given an immense impetus to those factories that speciali. in washing OntiOn dresses, for these were sold by thousand, and the fact that some have come to regard two or three washing frocks no necessary now, where only one was formerly required, should compensate for the fulling off Is the number and the trimming of underskirts. The . tight tube nevertheless is still very much worn, many of the leading designers predict a long life for it. Another craze to be noted, that may with certainty be characterised as fleeting, is that for diagonal lines which are seen in striped materials, tunics and trimmings, which all show a slant. Materials of two colours are also much in evidenoe,and black and gold is a revival not to be regretted, for it is very handsome. Motifs of embroidery in square., triangles, and vandytee, sod of cretonne, or toils as Jolty, are</p>
        <p>extensively used in the trimming of autumn dresses, and a novel muff in which a motif of cretonne is combined with fur is quite an elegant affair. Acoessaries and the Dutch Collar—The blouse costae is a most useful I.ttle accessary to an early autumn dress, which by the way, it should match in colour, if not material. It is detachable and is often aeon over a bolero bodice. A rival to it is the shoulder °ape of silk or cloth that also matches the gown. This cape finishes inn shawl like pint down the centre of the back, and milk buttona of the same material as the cap., form the only trimming. he oollarles• dresses have brought the old•fashioned rankle:es back to favour, and the amber beads so popular in Victorian times are again much worn. Pair those women who delight io ' needlework, who also being frugally-minded, take pleasure in using up odds and ends, a very effective piece of work is a Dutch collar. To make it out the shape desired in fins in pain, then baste firmly on thin foundation, any odd motiffs of leace,embruidery, or any kind of trimming that appears suitable, and fill in with very fine lawn or muslin,then outline each ecrdp with beads. It in fascinating work and well repays the trouble it gives. About Bags.—The bag is still a feature of dress on which much might be written, and for theatre or ball- room are large but not exaggerated. Many are of velvet appliqued on thin tissue, outlined in gold and silver sequins, sod fluished with gold, silver, or plati- num lace, and fans usually match them. Needles+, to say, these hags are evens, ve, but not as costly as the green-gold article, composed of hundreds of thousands of links, each of which in soldered. All bags are slung over the shoulder by means of long colds, whatever their make or style.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>A NEW-COMER.</p>
        <p>In view of the settlemont of the Rev. H. Ogden as p icor of the Golhorne-etreet Baptist Church, I had a paragraph in which I wrote of personal acquaintance with that recently-arrived minister, whom I happen to have known from his boyhood and at whose m.r.iage with daughter of the Toivu Clerk of Torltnorden I was present. I have a'so met all those under whom Mr. Ogden sullied when preparing for his life w trk. Psincipil Marshall, of the Manchester College, I have often reported. The Rev. Thomas Cot-s, by whom Golhorne-strect'a new minister was baptised at the age of 16, was in charge of the Wellii.gtott-road Chu oh, Todmorden, some two-and-twenty years, and resigned not very long ago on his acceptance of a pastorate at Ossett in the neighb inrhood of Wake- field. As stated Lest week, it was under the tuition of this gentleman and of the Rev. H. L Staines that Mr. Ogden began to study for the ministry. Mr. Staines, then at Vale, Cornholme (in the Burnley Valley), afterwards accepted a call to Cape Town, but has since returned to this country. It boa been previously mentioned that the young minister who last Sunday formally entered upon bid ministry here, had the misfortune to lose hie wife by death about four yeare after marriage; and at the time of writing his father-in-law —who, in addition to the town dank- ship holds the position of registrar of the local County Court —lies ill from a serious heart affection. Ile ban all along been one of the foremost and most hard working members of the Wellington-road community, having occupied all the leading offims in connection with the Church and Sunday school there. Of his numerous family, some of whim are will at home, one son, a solicitor, is in the office where, in addition to his public duties, he carries on a private practicr ; a I daughter is the wife of the borough engineer and surveyor; and another is married to the son of Mr. C. Hoyle, J.P., who was first Mayor of Todmorden and filled the office for three years. THE BANQUE C.</p>
        <p>Last week's big' feed " in celebration of the pile. driving sent me afresh to an entertaining article purporting to trace the origia of communal eating and to show why the custom is nowathys indispens- able. As to the former the writer's argument was something like this : So long an man lived on casual meal I snatched from bushes and trees, there wall no reason for communal estig. And when he robbed a bird's nest or caught a fish in his hands, his altruistic impulses were not roused. He hid in the bushes to eat, or at the best shared his prize with his m tie and offspring. But when he had risen to the triumph of catching a deer, he could, for the first time in the history of the world, afford to be generous. He had no cold storage warehouse. The only way to save the meat was to eat it, and, as he could not do that alone he called in the neighbouring troglodytes to share it with him. But what of to-day's real reason? As to that, the author says the banquet is merely an excuse, superfluous but indispensable. Certain people want to get together or the host wants to get them together, and the only practical way is to give them a dinner. We have been made so wary of each other by the exigencies of our civilisati in that to become acquainted with a man you have to stalk him as you would a deer. You cannot approach a stranger and say to bins : " You seem to be my kind of a toan. I think it would be to our mutual advan- tage to become acquAnted." That would be altogether too blunt end presumptuous. Toe more modest and diplomatic way is to assume that you have no more in common than a dependence upon food, and say to him : "I have to eat and you have to eat. Let us eat together. Possibly ,between mouthfuls we may indulge in a little casual but profitable conver- sation." AFTER DINNER.</p>
        <p>So far as the latest Warrington banquet is con- cerned, however, this dues not c iver oh the ground. It appears (and to one whose lot it has beeu to attend many such this is indeed wonderful) that, while doubt- less there are Warringtonisns whose hearts would sink into their boots on the bare suggestion that they should address any assembly of the kin I, others there he whose names cannot with Ait risk of peisonal affront be omitted from the toast list ou these occasions made and provided. Said one of the former : "I suppose if I accept your invitation to that dinner you will want me to make a speech? " And the reply was: "No, my dear fellow, you see it's this way : Everybody we have invited so far wants to make a speech, and what I am trying to do now is to get together a few listeners." For myself, I shamelessly confess to a good deal of sympathy with the fellow who said the best after-dinner speech he ever heard kit was on a very different occasion, of course) was, "Let we settle with the waiter!" HACKNEYED PHRASES.</p>
        <p>On second thoughts and with a certain outstanding Mcident of last week's local banquet well in mind, I have some doubt as to whether I ought really to com- mend Mr. Grenville Kleisher and his Amerioan institu- tion, the Public Speaking Club. What I mean is that, his avowed object being to encourage diffident ones in the art and practice of platform—and, it may safely be assumed, post.prandial oratory, it would seem that there is not in Warrington so much need of or room for missionary effort along these lines as some had supprrsed. Still, after all, it is refreshing to learn that Mr. Kleisher's Club discourages the use of slang and of hackneyed phrases. As a brother pencil-pusher put it when the gentleman in question burst upon us with his ambitious scheme for increas leg and multiplying the talking tribe in this country, if he can rid us for ever of the man who " ventures to assert," who" goes a stop further, who" says without fear of contradiction," he will be doing a real service. Other phrases, occur to the mind, indissolubly con- nected with Imperialist declaration. If Mr. Kleieer, greatly daring, should succeed in banishing thews as hackneyed, our gratitude would be great enough to outweigh our fears. THE LAW OF LIBEL</p>
        <p>This is a favourite topic of mine. I believe I wrote on it in my very first contribution to this paper. Possibly I sin too strongly prejudiced, an to that the reader may judge. Once upon a time, as editor of a mid-weekly, I accepted from some gas stokers on strike a letter in answer t an attack by the Chair man of the Gas Committee. Thelatter's speech had been reported in other papers, but the ineertion therein of the aggrieved stokers' reply had been refused. I considered the contents fair and reasonable enough and took the responsibility—though it proved much heavier than I had ever before thought possible, involving as it did an action at the assizes, with retenli,ni of leading sod junior counsel, and judgment for £2OO and costs. That, ho eever, by-we- way. Thie reference was suggested by the opening para- grah sin last week's' Women:s Chat" It was beaded ..</p>
        <p>Ridiculing thelaw of libel, and writer whi had likewise " had some," bad in substitution for fictitious names of charactere obtained permission to one those if well-known public men, including those of U. K. Chesterton, who la-.t season , lectured here under the auspices of the Philo:net-Inc Society. ne novel referred to is called " Motley land Tinsel," and therein Georg. Bernard Shaw appears as a stage d. or-keeper, Plitt Ridge 'ea a call boy, G. R. Sims as a cab driver, land so forth and so on. The tale, as will be Ilgathered, is cancerned with theat it-al life, , and when it appeared as a seiial in a weekly paper, a person called Belcher was a con- spicuous character. It was alleged to he purely</p>
        <p>fictitious, bet ona of that name brought a i action for libel, and, to in my case, there was a verdict for £21./0• Judging by other resubs, I suppose we ought to be thianiful tat we got off so easily—although it cost me my situ:l46o, and drove toe to a precarious existence as a free-lance journalist. I note that iu "A man with a past," St. John Adcock explains ia the Feface that it is a work of fiction, that he himself invented all the charac:ers, and that they had no originals in real life. Doi need uot wouder at the precaution in day* when an Eoglish jury holds one to be traduced in a novel in face ot tile author's declaratiou that the whole thiog was imagination on his part. I think the MAL glaring case of the kind was that in which a Paris correepondeut of a borne journal wrote of the doiiigs of a clinic:maiden whom tie named Jones, surely of the commonest. Never- theless, one of the innumeiable multitude answering thereto, while dislaitniug ali that was alleged in the paper, complained that it described his personality, and actually recovered damages. I just now menu i 'lied Mr. Chestertoo, and I may fittiegly close these sentences with a characteristic titbit of bin on thia very subject. Concluding an article upon it a while ago, he put it thus :—A vegetarian may not say, " Meat-eating leads logically to cannibalism," without paying me a thousand pouuus for calling me a cannibal. A test faler may not say 'if you drink it will lead to beiug a drunkard," without selling his furniture to clear me of the charge of drunkenness. lie cannot, that is, say these things if he says them in good literature ; for in good literature these are field, not obviously, but with a certain relentless logical development from absurdity to absuidity. I think it is the twilight of libeity ; but I am sure it is the sunset of astute. WHY WASH.</p>
        <p>At last week's meeting of the Warrington Town Council, in moving the euniiimation aud adoption of the minutea of he committee, Alderman Bolton took occasion in C mmeit upon the COU- tem phted provision of baths at a coot of upwards of 18,000 declaring this to be one of the best things they Lad done lur the town, as by it the people would be able to become cleaner, and would . . . .</p>
        <p>'therefore benefit in health." Well but —apart from inditidual preference and prodded:on, and even from common sense if you p ease -is that a truly ectentillic deduction ? Are not our present-day ideasof cteault- peas, our fresh-air theories, and all the rest of it, so many hygiehic superstitions? Within • compare- ively recent pet iod —a matter of a few mouths almost as at seems to me—in a speech tia bacteriology tied hygiene at the theatre of the Civil Service Com- missioners, Burlington Gardens, air Almroth Wright avowed the conviction that cleanliness is not to be , recormnehded at a hygienic method, inasmuch as a great deal of washing increases the uncrobis of the skin," and sometime before that, an authority of at least equal eminence task pains to explatn that : frequent washing of the skin was positively mis- chievous and barwful because it removed an oily substance specially provided by nature for purposes ol protection. 1 cannot reproduce the *Aide in enemata but that was what the argument amounted to. So there ! THE " LIGH FAN FAS TIC."</p>
        <p>Weinesday's fancy calico- ball, promoted by the II Women's Liberal Aasocistion, may be regarded as . formally introducing another masa, of indoor recrea- tion of that pop ilar sort. A, has been repeatedly explained, last of all and most interestingly in the oilobe," the position taken up by the dancers gave the name to the "quadrille," which is literal Iteuch for "a little square " ; while " Country Dance " has no connection witu rustic gymnastics, bat is eimpy a corruption of the French contresdautie,. which has reference to the position of the couples opposite to each other during the dance. The latter seldom fiads a place on ordinary dance programmes hereabout, but with the " Lancers it is altogether differeut. It is well, therefore, to be reminded that the " Lancers " detived ita name from the fact that thin variation of the Quadrille was originally improvised by a company of Lateen, for their own amusement while heated in their saddles. The " Polka is a polish dance, and its name corned fr-m the Bohemian word " polka," meaning half, arid refers to the half istep which occurs in thin lively measure, of which din triage graceful " :Schottische inn variation ; that of the dance of Poland -the " Msz orka "- being oaths, , terms. The short steps peculiar to the old-time favourite -the " Minuet "-gave the dance its name, the Latin for " small " being " rmuutus." The " Waltz again, owee its 11.0110 to its characteristic moveureat, the German " sallies "- meaning I evolve -ezplesing the circling nu iron ol the dancers I take it for granted that my readers have to VI. extent followed what as appeared in one and . ,of the pernsliculis respecting the " Time*" ormina, entitled " Tarantisru in the Traci." I In his p 'pular weekly '• Tl':' who also visited War- ringh in last session aud is pert oually known to not a few I here, had a go" at it and many oilier writers bare</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>FREE OFFER,</head>
        <p>FREE OFFER,</p>
        <p>tali. Coup.. end Id. Warl%l6oll stamp to ZAm teed. .._ Xamluer, fer • free nample of this M brim. Oa 14. 1911 onequalled</p>
        <p>made good " copy "out of it. Bevels' of these have pointed out that 'Yjarantella " is so-called because its vigorous movements were supposed to be a certain antidote to the pole at of a noxious spider in Taranto, in Italy, where the dance is highly popular. The evolutions of the dancer sufficiently explain the term " Reel." " Jig " is from the Preach " gigue," and Breakdown " is a term from across the Atlantic, mid refers to the final r ut bef ire the break-up of a free-and-easy dancing party. Seldom, if ever, do Warrit.gtouians get nearer to these last-mentioned than the Barn Dance, and I hasten to add that this in not in such local favour as ths Iwo-step.</p>
      </div>
