      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>MY SHADOW.</head>
        <p>MY SHADOW.</p>
        <p>I have a little shadow that goes in and out with me, And what can be the the use of him is wore than I _ can see.</p>
        <p>He is very, very like me from the heels up to the head ; And I see him jump before ine, when I jump into my bed.</p>
        <p>The tuaniest thing about him is the way he likes to</p>
        <p>grow— Not at all like proper children, which is always very Blow; For he sometimes shoots up taller like an india-rubber ball, And he sometimes gets so little that there's none of him at all.</p>
        <p>Ile hasn't got a notion of how children ought to play, And c..n oily make a fool of me to every sJrt of way. He stays so clone bond° me, he's a coward you can</p>
        <p>800 ; I'd think shame to stick to auntie an that shadow sticks to ma !</p>
        <p>One morning, very early, before the nun was up, 1 rune and Lund the shining dew on every buttercup; But my lazy little shldow, like an arreot elecpy-bead, Had stayed at home behind Luc and was fast asleep in bed.</p>
        <p>I. L STEVENSON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>TEMPERANCE TEACHING. Band of Hope Recommendation to the _ Government.</head>
        <p>TEMPERANCE TEACHING. Band of Hope Recommendation to the _ Government.</p>
        <p>The forty-eighth &amp;Latium.' coolerenoe of the Lthei. shire aud Cb.hire Band of Hope Llama, the largest provincial Lemporembe association, WWI held at %theater Saturday, Aral was attended oy a large number of delegates. It was stated that the organisation now hair • totsl membership exceedieg 325,e00. Tao various gatherings were presidad.over by the piesident,tne Lady Barlow.. . .</p>
        <p>AC the ruorniog sesaion it waa decided to urge upon the (it...eminent the &lt;1.41101 lity of mailing tira present optional temperance teaching in day ach..aula oomph!). ary an all schools, at least three leasou• to be giveu each year. . ' -`</p>
        <p>At. the afternoon meeting in the ToWe Hall Mr. R. J. Hilo, • director of the N.S.P.O C., delivered a lecture on The cruelty of the drunken and ho the children suffer," cud other speakers included the Hon. S.Mauger, es.Postmaster-lietieral of AtiaLral le, Mr. J. T. 1t.., secretary of the National Temperance League, end Dr. W. Hodgson, chairman of the Chesuire Edumition Committee.</p>
        <p>A presoutation warn made to the secretary, Sir. W. Chandoa Wilson, in recognition of his term.nating 21 Jean 11l the 1161,F1G0 of tllO Union, of Wrlloll Ii yeti, had been spent in the capacity of se,ret-Ary. Lody Itsrlow also presented Slra. Wilsou with • gold leolnc</p>
        <p>L</p>
      </div>
