      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>Orford.</head>
        <p>Orford.</p>
        <p>H kIIVEST FE,TI V L.—The harvest festival services were continued at All Hallows' Church on Sunday. The preacher in the morning wss the Rev. Canon Morley- Stevenson who took for his text Psalms cxxii., I," I was glad when they said unto me, let us go into the House of the Lord." In the evening the pulpit was occupied by the Rev. W. A. Gibson of Birkenhead, who took for his text St. John iv., 57 "One soweth, another reaped," The collections were given to the Society for the Piopegaticn of the Gospel.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>Winwick.</head>
        <p>Winwick.</p>
        <p>HARVEST FRSTIVI T..—The harvf st thanksgiving services were commenced in the Parish Church on Thursday night and were continued Cu Sunday. The preacher at the Thursday evening sar•ice was the Rev. .1. Chadwick, who took for his teat St. James v., 2, " Behold the husbandman waiteth for the precious fruit of the earth and bath long patience for it." The choir sang the anthem," The Lord is loving." The Church was very tastefully decurated with harvest produce, fruit and flowers, etc. the font especially attracting attention. The preset:fir at the Sunday morning service, was the Rev. Canon Penrhyn. The choir sang the anthem " Blessed of the Lord be the laud," the tenor solo being taken by Mr. W. Sweetlo•e. The preacher of the evening service was the Rev. J T Owen. The choir sang the anthem the" The Lord is loving." The collections at all the services were in aid of foreign missions.</p>
        <p>Padgate. LOC Bovvhsm's liosorn.—Padgatoniane are some- what disappointed thst Mr C Roberta—the well-known " trundler" sod licensee of the Farmer's Arms, Ewan- head—did not manage to secure honours in the Blackpool Bowling Tournament this week. They are however, proud of bin being in the last eight of the 1,000 entrants and hope for better luck next time. WESLEY Cimm.—A very Interesting debate took place under the auspices of the Wesley Guild on Monday evening the subject being "Is the race deterior- ating." Mr J H Mason opened the discussion for thei affirmative and was supported by Messrs Alderman Bennett, Plinston and Birchall. Mr E England was the leader of the negatives and was followed on the same side by Messrs Duckworth and Appleton. Mr. Newton, who presided, was also evidently in sympathy with the aide championed by Mr England. NEW WESLEYAN SUNDAY SCHOOL —Services in connection with the opening of the new schools were held last Sunday. The preacher for the morning was the Rev. W. A. Beckett, who took for his subject " The beauty of God." The rev, gentleman spoke of God's idea of the beautiful as shown in the beauty of nature, of art and of character. lie said " there was no special merit in worshipping in a barn or an ugly building" and incidentally referred to the value of the beauty containcl in the new and commodious school premises. In the evening the Rev. Ebenezer Morgan preached to a large congregation from the words "Enoch walked with God and he was not for God took him " emphasizing the fact that holiness is possible in adverse circum- stances. This concludes the special services connected with the opening during which a sum of ETO has been added to that already in hand.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>Stockton Heath.</head>
        <p>Stockton Heath.</p>
        <p>HILL CLIFFE BAPTIST CRllRCll.—Harvest thanks- giving services were held at this church on Sunday. The church had been prettily decorated by lady mem- bers of the congregation. The liev J S Chedburo preached at the morning and evening service. A children's service was held in the afternoon, and was largely attended. Anthem were creditably rendered by the choir, and at the evening service Miss Taylor wan the soloist. A social evening and sale cf truit were held on Monday. lhe proceeds were in aid of the church fund,.</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH PAROCHIAL COMMITTEE. The monthly meeting of the Stockton Heath Parochial Committee was held at the Hall Card, on Thursday evening, Mr G Pilling presiding. There were also present r—Messrs F A Harris, H J Westbrook, W Mclntyre, J Whitley, F Rose, O J Brown, J Hallows, J McDermid, J R Wood, J Swinton (surveyor,), G F Ashton (clerk). WOULD AUTOMATIC LIGHTERS PAY ? The Town Clerk of Warrington wrote staring that the ' engineer reported that the introduction of automatic lighters would, in his opinion, effect no material economy. By the introduction of the lighters gas was consumed by the by-passes during all the hours of day- light. If, however, after further experience it was found that the lighters effected an economy, be was sure the committee would be prepared to give the Stockton Hest h Committee the benefit of it. In reply to an irquiry as to whether the Coo Com- mittee would be prepared to instal the automatic lighters forthwith, the Town Clerk wrote r Before my committee are prepared to decide on the installation of automatic lighting throughout the district, they will require a guarantee for the lighting to be continued for a term of years, on terms to be arranged. The engineer points out that the cleaning and repair of the automatic clock work costs considerably more per lamp per annum. Mr. Whitley raid the automatic scheme was not altogether successful in those parts of the district where it had been tried. Sometimes the lamps were not lit until an hour or two slier it was dark, and at other times they would be alight at 10 o'clock in the morning. Mr. Hallows : Our three lamps work very well in Grappenhall-road. Mr. Brown moved that the letter lie on the table until the nest meeting and that le the meantime the Clerk be instructed to make inquiries from other districts with regard to the system. Mr. Wood : What is the ides of thes3 clocks ? Mr. Whitley : To ge: uniform The proposition was agreed to. ELECTRICITY V. GAS. Mr. E. Bradshaw, clerk of the Parish Council, wrote atating that at the reco it parish meeting the question of the supply of electricity by the Salt Colon was dis- cussed and it was resolved : That the Pen chi's' Com- mittee ascsrtain, if possible, what demand there is in this parish for electricity, and report to the company es early as Fallible. Mr. Brown said the householders would want to know the relative cost of gas and electricity ha fore they could come ton decision. If the committee took a poll of the district by sending a circular stating what the cost of electricity would be, 99 out of 100 inhabitants would not be able to tell whether they would burn more electricity or gas. A man would not say " Yea' or " No " with regard to gas or electricity, because he would not be able to calculate the cost. Mr. Rose said the company could give them Porno Idea. He thought it was generally considered that electricity at 6d. per unit up to a certain number of lights inc house worked out at about the same price for the house:older as gas at is. 61. per 1160. The Clerk said the company wanted to know chiefly what the works would be likely to use. If they could see a prospect of a fair return from the works they would take the risk without troubling the householders. Mr. fileDermid said there was little likelihood of electricity being supplied for many °earls to come at a price that would compete with gas for lighting purposes. It wee decided that the Clerk should tale steps to ascertain the relative cost of gas and electricity, with a view to circularising the inhabitants. A HUGE LAKE." Mr. H. Cron, IS, Alexandra-road, Stockton Heath, wrote :—The above road loon absolute disgrace to any Ctrancil. I, as a resident, think it is about time you either made this road Or compelled the landlords to do NO. To-night it is one huge lake. Wecould almost supply Manchester with the water they a e short of. The way we are being treated in this matter is abominable. It is not healthy. The Surveyor sa'd the road had been cindered but when heavy reins came there was no drainage to take c IT the eurplus water. It was derided that it be a suggest icn to the owners of property adjoining Alexandra- read that a surface rooter drain be put into the road as far as built upon. ard connected with thesurf,ce water drain in Fairfield road, and that the road be further improved. The Clerk was also instructed to intimate that if the work is not carried out there will ba no alternative but to put in force the Private Street Works Act. PAYING OF lIACK PASsAt.ES. Mr. Wood 881,1 if anything had beers done with regard to the paving of back p lenges ? The Clerk : The plans are now before tie Local Goverorneat Board, to put the Private Street Works Act in force with respect to them.</p>
        <p>TOWN PLANNING. Mr. Westbrook, in calling attention to the forth- coming lecturem on Town Planning at Warrington, mid that if a Town Planning Act luid been in operation in Stockton Hesth in past y,rm they vrvuld have been saved a lot of the abortions which they suffered from to-day. (Hear, hear,. The Chairmen intimated his intention of attending the lectures and asked the m(,abers of the committee to accompany him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>t Hatton.</head>
        <p>t Hatton.</p>
        <p>The thanksgiving services in connection with St. Luke's Mission, Hatton, were concluded on Sunday. A service had been held on the previous Thursday evening, when the preacher was the Rev. T. T. Lee Jones, Vicar of Daresbury. On Sunday afternoon the Rev. W. S. Adams (curate) occupied the pulpit. Both services were well attended. The little church was very tastefully adorned with flowers and fruit, the ladies connected with the Mothers' Meeting being responsible for this labour of love. The choir reflected great credit on themselves bytbe way in w Mob they sang the anthem " His promise true" by Caleb S mpher,Miss Elsie Bannister singing the solo with pleasing effect.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>Moore.</head>
        <p>Moore.</p>
        <p>THE LATE MR. CONGREVE.—The death, under such tragic circumstances, of the late Mr. Hubert Congress, leaves a blank in the hearts of a large Miele of friends in Moore. During his 15 years residence in Moore, Mr. Congreve made many frisnda of all shades of opicion, political and religious. Ile took en active interest in the life of the village, and was for many Isarn vice- chairman of the Parish Council, and for 11. months prior to his leaving the district, was chairman of that body. Ile was also president of the Moore Social Improvement Society until this society was dissolved two years ago. When Mr. and Mrs. Congress left Moore shout six months ago, the toes to the village was keenly felt.</p>
        <p>WESLEY arm) : SOCIAL EVENING.—The first social of the session was held on Wednesday ever,ing last, and proved a thorough success. Alter the opeuiog, Mr. ' Meldrum' the genial Social Vice-president, made a few I introductory remarks. Bespoke very feelingly of the loss which everyone felt by the tragic death of Mr. Congress, and Miss Dutton agreed to convey to Mrs. Congreve the sympathy of that gathering in her great bereavement. He then went on to welcome the new president, the Rev. H. Middlewood, who was present with Mrs. Middlewood.—Mr. Middlewood then thanked Mr. Meldrum for his hearty welcome, and testified to the pleasure it gave both him ani his wife to be present with them. A mixed pro- gramme of readings, recitations and songs was inter- spersed with amusing competitions which provided the lighter side of the entertainment. Excellent refreshments were served at half-time by the ladies committee, during which gramophone selections were given by Mr. S. Baanett, Miss Clare sang " Garden of roses" in good style, and Miss R. Tanner sang " Grandma," excellently. Miss Dutton's readings are always well received and Miss Castle proved a very entertaining reciter. A hat-trimming competition for gentlemen proved a great source of amusement, the prize being carried off by Mr. James Fanner. Similarly a nail driving competition for ladies proved a very populor item, Miss Tanner winning the prize. Another competition was to guess the number of sweets in a large bottle, this was won by Miss G. Ilasnett, who was 12 off the correct number. A very enjoyable social evening came to a close al-out ten o'clock, when the Doxology was wag and tne party dispersed.</p>
        <p>Penkettr. WESLEYAN CHURCH SUNDAY SCHO3L.—The teachers' gnar.erly n.eeting was held on Friday evening the 6th lost, the Rev. Id Middlewood presiding. Mr G Dale (who hes done good work as a teacher)was presented by the officers and teachers of the Sunday school with al beautiful barometer, with the best wishes of all for his future married life. Miss C. Atherton with a few well chosen words made the presentation. Mr Dale feelingly acknowledged. LECTURE AT FRIEND,' SCHDoL.—A good number wended their way to the above school on Monday even- ing to her a lecture (illustrated by lime-light views). entitled" In Scottish Islands" by Mr. George E Thompson, of Birkenhead. Mr W E Brown headmaster presided. Giving an interesting description of a tour through the islands of Staffs and lona, the lecturer showed many beautiful coloured views of the scenery in these islands. Master Arthur Roberts proposed and Miss M Moorehead seconded a vote of thanks to Mr Thompson for the evening's entertainment. Mr. Robinson was in charge of the lantern.</p>
        <p>PERK nil PARISH COUNCIL.—A meeting of the above Council was held on Monday evening in the Council Schools, Stocks-lane. The ft:mule:a present were : Dr. Murray, chairman, Mr J 0 Withinshaw, vice-chair- man, Miss Tinsley, Messrs William Davies, Richard Routledge, James tVoodward and Mr E Foster (clerk).</p>
        <p>In openf4 the discussion on the Fernworth-road sewer the Chairmen said be presumed that the Council would pay for the sewer in the same way that they paid for the Station-road sewer, the cost amounting to something like ISO. After a little discussion Mr Withinshaw proposed and Mr Woodward seconded that the cost be spread over two hat( years—Mr Routledge asked if it could not be divided over a longer period. The Chairman said that would necessitate a loan. Mr. Withinshaw remarked that at the rate the village was developing, the day was not far distant when the ratepayers would have to face a large sewage acheme—lhe Clerk was instructed to write to Mr. J. C. Sutton stating that the Parish Council were trilling to agree to a spccial rate to be divided over two half yearn. Mr. Withinshaw put a proposition to the meeting and Mr. Woodward seconded which Waa agreed to, that the Clerk wide to the Lord of the Manor, Csptain Michael flushes, stating that the Penketh Parish Council protested against the attempt to close the footpath leading from the Ferry Inn to the Locks to which the public have a right of way.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>Lymm.</head>
        <p>Lymm.</p>
        <p>DJ/MORTAL TO MR. F. 0. Muslim —Last week a font was placed in the Wesley Church as a memoriel to the late Mr. F. G. Mouton and was used for the first tin eon Sunday. The font is a handsomely designed structure in granite and while marble and bears the words "To the Glory of God in memory of F. O. Mouton, Obit 1910." The construction of the font was placed in the hands of Mr. Levi Thomason by Mrs. Moston. During the whole of hie life Mr. Moston was honourably connected with the Wesleyan Church, filling the offices of superintendent and treasurer of the Sunday schools. The font is a beautiful addition to the interior of the church standing as it does close to the oak pulpit which was placed in the church some few years ago ea a Memorial to the late Mr. Edwin Brown.</p>
        <p>CONGREGATIONAL CHRISTIAN SERVICE BAND.—A good number attended the meeting on Monday evening when Mr A Leather addressed the meeting. He eh, se for his subject " Thankfulness" acd in his discourse he said we ought to be th Ink fel for our daily bread an raiment, also for the great leaders like the Rev F B Meyer, whom we have heard so much of lately, and for our churches. The address was molt interesting and met the approval of all. Miss Fanny Sutton rendered "Jesus help me nese the Cross," in a most excellent manner. Mrs G Wilton presided. Tne meeting con- cluded with prayer. WESLE YAN CH URCH.—The harvest thenksgiving services were held in this church on Sunday last. The morning service was conducted by the Rev Henry Iliddlewood of Penketh. The choir rendered the anthem" While the earth remaineth "(J II Maunder) in a creditable style. The evening service was con- ducted by the Rev R Colley Hutchinson, the new minister in charge. He preached an excellent sermon about Frail," which was greatly approved by the large congregation which attended. The choir rendered the anthem" Ye shall dwell in the land " (J Stainer). The collections were in aid of society funds. The church was most artistically decorated and was quite a credit to those ladies who undertook the task. On Monday evening, a recognition meeting was held in the school- room to welcome Mr. Hutchinson, and quite a large wheel ng assembled. The evening was most enjoyable. Fruit refreshments were served during the interval. Mr J Waterworth presided. A collection was taken at the close of the meeting. The fruit which was left over I was sold, realizing about .f I.</p>
        <p>Funeral of Mr. Henry Tem. Amid manifestations of great regret the funeral took place at the Cemetery on Sunday of the late Mr. Henry Teece, of the Old Ball Inn, Winwick-street, who died on the sth inst., at the age of 61 Tears. The Rev. J. Davies fficiated. The Licensed 'Victuallers Association was represented by Messrs. J. Lee, J. S. Earle, A. E. Denham, R. Walker, P. E. Lake, J. Llewellyn, W. Cherry, T. Partridge, .1. E. Shaw, Joints Payton, Davies, and E. Lcciett, (secretary of the assoc'ation). Msny fkral tributes were sent by relatives and friends. At a meeting cf the Licensed Victuallers' Assccistion held cu 'Need, a!ternn.on, a vote of c-ndolence was passed with tte widow and family, en the motion of the President (Alderman John Evans) seconded by Mr. Joseph Shillock.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>ARCHDEACON WOOD INDUCTED AT BOWDEN.</p>
        <p>Archdeacon Maitland Wood, for over twenty years vicar of Runcorn, was on Saturday inducted an sicar of Bowden, in SUCC.SiOO to Canon Clore, who has resigned the living, which he held for thirty-eight years. "I here was a large gathering of clergy, and the Bishop of Chester referred in his address to the sem ices which the late vicar had rendered to the pariah and in other places. Alluding to the new vicar, the Bishop raid he was sure they would do all they could to make his residence there fresh and freshening with all the arts of sympathy, of encouragement, and of co-operation, and he was sure they would find, the more they knew him. that he was one who could be trusted and would be valued, and who, if God gave him continued health and strength, would do solid things for that parish. RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Board of Guardians wars held at Dutton Workhouse 4.a Tuesday afternoon. Mr. J Spruce presided over a good attendance. The officers present were :—Messrs U F Ashton (clerk), W Hough (Resistant clerk), T Cooke. W It de Prez and J Pearson (relieving officers), and J Primus (master).</p>
        <p>RUSCORN COTTAGE HOSPITAL. Mr W Morris moved that the Board contribute £2 25. annually to the Runcorn Cottage Hospital. The hospital, he said, was suppotted by voluntary contribu- tions and it ins dcing a good work. It had also received one or two patients who would have had to come into the house. Mr George Pilling seconded and the motion was carr led.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>RUNCORN RURAL COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The fortnigtly meeting of the Runcorn Rural Council was licld mrced lately after the Board of Guardians Mr. T Wright precided and the officers present were : Messrs 0 F Ashton (clerk), W Hough (assistant clerk). J Swinton (surveyor', J Farrington (inspector', and Dr. Adams (medical officer of health.)</p>
        <p>MOORE SEWERAGE. The Clerk to the Moore Parish Council wrote stating that the Council wire fully alive to the necessi. yof some improvement in the sewerage of the Township but they were nevertheless much alarmed at the extent and conscquent expense of the large scheme for dealing with the matter which had been submitted for their inspection by the Council. They hoped the Council would modify the scheme no that the ratepayers would not have to suffer a greater increased rate than was at solutely necessary. The Parish Council would esteem its privilege if they were permitted to send a deputation to confer with the Council at the next ! meeting. I The Clerk said the scheme which had been prepared ! bad been forwarded to the Local Government Board and he had beard from them that they were going to ' hold an enquiry with regard to it and it would there- ! fore be well if the Moore Parish Council were repre- sented then. The Rural District Council could not reconsider the scheme now because it had already been placed before the L.G.B. Mr. Pilling moved and Mr. Jones seconded that the Clerk write to the Moore ParishCotiscil informing them of the forthcoming enquiry and suggesting that they should be represented there.</p>
        <p>GRAPPENHALL_</p>
        <p>The Clerk read a letter from the Clerk of the Grappenhall Parish Council, asking the Rural Council to erect a lamp at the corner near the " Dog and Dart." Inn. They also suggested that the Council should communicate with the Warrington Corporation asking them to make a ooncession in regard to the price of lamps. nay thought that as the rates to house- holders had been reduced the supply of gas for lamps should be reduced to £2 ss. Mr. F. Burrowes moved that the recommendations of the Grappenhall Parish Council be accepted. Ile would also like the District Council to go further than the Grappenhall Council and place two lamps, one by the " Dog and Dart" and the other between the ion and the existing lamp in Knotaford-road. Mr. Burrowes' suggestion was agreed to by the Council, and is was also decided that the Clerk should write to the Warrington Corporation to see whether lower terms could be obtained for the lamps.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN MINER'S DEATH.</p>
        <p>A verdict of" Accidental death 'was returned at St I Heles.s at an inquest on the body of John Daly, a day- age man, aged thirty-six, of 44, Derby-street, Earles- town, who died in the St. Helens Hospital on Wednes-. day last. The evidence was that on April 2lst last the man, who had worked for the Collins Green Colliery for eight years, fell in the mine and injured his right elbow. He never worked again, and was paid compensation. A tuberculous disease rapidly developed in many parts of the b_cly, for which complaint Daly was oper- ated on at the hospital. Dr. Unsworth was of the opinion that the injury to the elbow net up the tuberculosis, which accelerated death. FRICTION AT TORY DEMONSTRATION. LORD WOLMER'S MIXED RECEPTION.</p>
        <p>The Newton Division Junior Conservative Associa- tion held a demonstration on Monday night in the Eariestown Town Hall. .</p>
        <p>Mr. Tom Smith (prospective candiiate for Hyde), said that in the name of democracy Mr. Asquith had set up autocracy, and in the name of the liberties of the people Mr. Asquith had robbed the people of the last vestige of freedom they possessed. Belerring.to the Insurance Bill, the speaker declared that the design of the Chancellor of the Exchequer was to smash the trade unions and friendly societies of the country. There was an opposition element, and Lord Wolmer's rising was greeted with the singing of "For he's a jolly good fellow," and by the opposition "The Land Song," Mo. Lord Wolmer, M P., referred to Welsh disestablish- meat and disendowment, a bleb he said was the ques- tion looming in the near future. R hat good was it going to do Nonconformists ? Radicals told them the money a ould go towards social reform. There W 95 no greater social work than that carried on by the Churches of Christianity in the country. , If they deprived the Church of this money, which was being well spent, they mere not assisting a single Non- conformist body or any other Cenomination. They were merely crippling the force of Christianity as a whole. It was their duty as members of the Church to protest by every means in their power against this money, devoted by public men to the service of God, being stolen and usurped for the purposes of the State. They did not wish to claim privileges greater than Nonconformists, but they said they were entitled as Churchmen to their endowments, as Nonconformists were to theirs.</p>
        <p>The rceettng concluded in disoreer, a" scene' taking place at the back of the hall.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>CROSS-COUNTRY RUNNING.</head>
        <p>CROSS-COUNTRY RUNNING.</p>
        <p>WEST LANCASHIRE ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>On Saturday last 222 runners representing 16 clubs, took part in the opening run of the season from the headquarters of Sacred Heart Harriers, St. Helena. The count for the muster prizes resulted as follows :- 1, Sacred Heart, 42 runners ; 2, Runcorn 'rally Ho, 33; Ravenbead, 29; St. Helens Parish Church, 27; Sutton 27; and the secretaries of the two former clubs received the awards.</p>
        <p>About five miles of mixed cruntry were travi rsed bears lining up for the Senior Mile Break, which resulted thus i—LC H Speiser, Wallasey A.C. ; 2, A G Roberts, West Cheshire ; 3, J 1' Rimmer, Liverpool Police A.S. ; H J Lycn, Ravenhead ; R McCaughey, North Liverpool Gymnasium; E Harrison, Sutton. There was a rare dust up at the finish, inches only separated the first two, and the Sefton veteran was a good third after being baulked if as cpening which probably cost him the race. , The placings in the junior break over the last mile ' were: 1, H Thompson, Liverpool lb uneasy ; 2, H Robertson, Richmond ; 3, 1:1 Birchen, Revenhead; 4, J Gittens, Runcorn. A clever finish in which the latter was perhaps s littletunfortunate in just missing a prize. lhe Senior and Junior Championships will be held on February 3rd next unless venue arrangements require an alieration to January 27th. A novice team race with a strict novice definition will tube place on December 2nd ; distance about tire miles ; entrance fee Is. per team. Teams ef six to sun, four to count, two reserves allowed. Three sets of medals, also medals for flmt man home, and the first man home of an unpinned team. Clubs may enter more than one team and the venue will be decided later.</p>
        <p>STt_DELENS AND DISTRICT ASS9CIATION._</p>
        <p>aThe " Novice " members of Warrington A.C. and Rylanda Harriers twill be eligible to take part in a Team Race n hid) this Astociation have decided to bold in March next On a :ate suitable for the venue which has jet to be arranged. --</p>
        <p>NATIONAL C.C. ['MON. A very important scheme for the recotati.ulion of the Union is now under consideration, and the various sub-oommittees appointed to deal with the matter will have many animus moments before ti . different associaticns reach agreement, which they eventually will do if 'give sod take " policy is gives a chance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>LOCAL MATEBS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL MATEBS.</p>
        <p>Ry lands W.R.C. Harriers are very hopeful of develop- logs useful team this Beaton and encouragement awaits the novices, tut there should really be more taking part, in such a clean healthy rem.' hen, from a large Arm who cater well for their employees. It is under• stood that their o Council ^ are always willing to help any branch of sport and this game makes a ready appeal. Runcorn Tally Ho have largely increased in numbers and entbusistm is begetting hoye for the near future. Sutton will have many or the old brigade out again and • strergtbened team will include I. Drinkwater when available. Wartingten 4.C, have trebled their membership roll during the last few reasons and when the headquarters difficulty is surmounted they will be able to enter on the setson with high hcpes f living up to • high</p>
        <p>reputation. Their opening run takes place to-day from the Orford Hotel at 9.30 p.m., all members are requested to attend and visitors are cordially invited.</p>
        <p>MEETING OF THE NORTHERN ASSOCIATION The 'Z'Jth annual meeting of the Northern Cross- country Association was held at the Hotel de Ville, Leeds, on Saturday. Lofthouse Park was selected the venue for the cbaropiooships on February 17th, an encicsure already booked for the Leeds and the York- shire C.C. Championships. The definition of a junior runner, which proved a stumbling block last year, was amended. It was ruled that the winner of last year's junior race was ineligible and that a race confined to members of terriers' clubs I iron open race for the purpose of there competitions The Nationsl C.C. Championship is dos to be decided' in the North next March,and Haydock Park Racecourse was recommended as the most 'jsuitairle venue. It tram ruled that a recent two miles harriers handicap at Warringten was an epen event. 1</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>LEGACIES FOR THE INFIRMARY AND NURSING ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>LEGACIES FOR THE INFIRMARY AND NURSING ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>GENTLEMAN'S BENEFICENCE.</p>
        <p>Great gratstication is felt at the announce ment that both the Warrington Infirmary and the Warrington and District Nursing Association are to greatly benefit by the will of Mr. Isaac Gilman, who though not a native of Warrington, lived here for a few years previous to his death in August and was so greatly impressed by their splendid efforts that he left them handsome legacies. Mr. T. 8. Steel is deceased's executor, and the intimations of the legacies have been conveyed by him to the officials concerned. We append the letters received by Mr. W. P. Rylands (chairman of the Infirmary Board of Management), and Councillor 11. Roberts (hon. tressirer of the DistrlA Nursing Association) : £3,000 FOR THE INFIRMARY. Mr. Steels letter to Mr. Rylands was as follows : Mill-street Chambers, Warrington, _ 7th October, 1911.</p>
        <p>Dear Mr. Rylands,—Some time ago a client of mine, Mr. Isaac Oilman, when making his will, was desirous of leaving the greater part of his estate to charities. Although ha had lived to Warrington only a few years he was anxious to do something for Warringten. I brought to his notice the work of the Warrington Infirmary, ani I am plesesd to tell you that, as a result of our conversation, he has left a legacy of £l,OOO to the Infirmary, and, subject to a life interest in the residue of his estate, a further legacy of £2,000 is bequeathed by his will to the same institution. Mr. Gilman died on the 2611 August last, and lam the executor of his will. I have not yet had an opportunity of getting together particulars of his estate, and, as you will readily understand, some time must elapse before the estate can he realized, but when lam in a position to pay over the first legacy of £l.ooo I will communicate with you again.—Yoors faithfully, THOMAS S. STEEL.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>W. Peter Rylands, Esq., J.P., Chairman Warrington Wintery. Mr. Rylands replied :</head>
        <p>W. Peter Rylands, Esq., J.P., Chairman Warrington Wintery. Mr. Rylands replied :</p>
        <p>9th October, 1911..</p>
        <p>Dear Mr. Steel,—Many thanks for your eitremely gratifying and interesting communication. The bequest of Mr. Gilman is a magnificent one and will give the greatest satisfaction and encouragement to the Board of Management.—Yours faithfully, W. PETER RIMS:MS.</p>
        <p>T. S. Steel, Em., solicitor, Warrington. £l,OOO FOR THE NURSING ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>Mr. Steel's letter to Mr. Roberts was es follows : Mill-street Chambers, Warrington, _ _ . 7:11 October, 1911.</p>
        <p>Gear Mr. Roberta,—You will ne7lotW-1:;;;;Ii;iiii to hear that I hope shortly to be in the position, Co executor of the will of the late Mr. Isaac Oilman, who died on the 26th of August last, to band over to you as the treasurer of the Warrington District Nursing Association a legacy of £1,01:10, which Mr. Gilman hes left to your association. Mr. Gilman was not a native of Warrington, but had lived here for something like five years prior to his death. When he came to make his will I found that he was desirous of leaving the greater portion of his estate to charities. I had no hesitation in recommending to him the claims of your association and pointing out to him the good and great work which is daily being performed amongst the working classes of our own town by the Superintendent and nursing staff of your association, and it was a real pleasure to me to know that his wishes were that the association should benefit on his death to the extent of £l,OOO. . . .</p>
        <p>As you will readily understand, it will take some time to realize the estate, but when tarn in a position to bapd over the money I will communicate to you again. I had not intended writing you until the estate had heed realized, but as the annual meeting is being held on Monday evening I thought it might encourage the subscribers to the funds of the association to hear of the benefaction of a man who had only spent a few years of his life in Warrington, but who appreciated the the work of an association which is doing so much to I alleviate the sufferings of the poor.—Yours faithfully, _ _ _ Teomis S. STee.L.</p>
        <p>Henry Robert., E,ri 4.1• P, Kingsley, Hill Chile, near Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>WARRINGTON GUARDIANS AND</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON GUARDIANS AND</p>
        <p>THE COAL STRIKE.</p>
        <p>The fornightly meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardian.s was held at the Workhouse on Wednesday morning. Mr G Mather presided and there were present :—Mrs Simpson, Mrs Collinswood, Miss Broadbent, Rev A M Mitchell, Messrs E L Bent (vice- chairman), A J Jolley, W Furness, A Brooks, W C Brookes, J Billince,J A Rushworth, R Lee, J A Hughes, , W Flanagan, M W Baton, W Houghton, A Bottomlev (clerk), B W Peacock (deputy clerk), and J F Awbery (master). At a meeting of the HOlne Visiting Committee held on the 9h ulto it was recommended that the Master be authorised to obtain one month's supply of coal from the contractors owing to the probability of a strike by the miners. , . .</p>
        <p>Rev. A. M. MITCHELL : Is it a right thing for the Board to do that simply on Supposition? We are helping to increase the price of coal. The CFIAIRMIN : The House Committee thought it wise or else they would not have recommended it. Mr. W. C. BROOKES It in a came of the WiEre virglno having oil in their lamps. (fAughter.) t.IFTS OF FRVIT AND FLOWERS. _ . . .</p>
        <p>At a steeling of the Flouse Visiting Committee it was recommended that the best thanks of the Guardians be accorded to the Vicar end churchwardens of the pariah church of Iturtonwood for gifts of fruit and flowers also to the vicar and churchwardens of the parish church of Great Sankey for the large gift of toys for the children at the Workhouse. ASSISTANT MASTYR.</p>
        <p>At the same meeting the Clerk presented 69 applic.a- tiona for the appointment of assistant Master of the workhouse. It sAS moved by Mr. A. Brooks, seconded by Mr. A. J. Jolley and recommended that Mr. Osborne Arthur Moss, Cierk in the office of the Master of the South Manchester Union Workhouse, be requrstel to attend the next meeting of the Board when his application for the appointment of assistant marter would be further considered." Mr. Moss appeared and after the interview Use Rev. A. M. Mitchell queried—Are there any other candi- dates? The CHAIRM : Plenty. Rev. A. M. MITCHELL Why are not some of them here no that we might make a selection ? The CHAIRMAN It is Oct necessary. The matter was folly discussed at the saaetiag of the Bosse Com- mittee. If you had been Uwe you would hare heard all about tbe matter. It was unanimous. Mr. Moss was appointed to the position at a salary of LIS per ADM= THE NEW DINING HAI F..</p>
        <p>_ . . Ths CrYritic read a letter from the Local Government Board stating that they bad appromt of the plans relating to the alterations (new kitchens end a dining ball) at the Workhouse, which the Guardians proposed to carry out, but before formally Eanctioning the expenditure for this work the Board directed him (the assistant secretary) to enclose a memorandum prepared by the architectural department, drawing attention to certaia ptrts in which the specification might be modified in order that the cost of the scheme might be reduced. Mr. SEGAR OWES (the nchitect to the Warrington Board of Guardiens) said that the suggestions for modifying the scheme were very small, namely, the utilising of salt g'azed tricks of second quality instead of at quality, lie Will considered that the Board should have the first qualit'., of brizirs, and this the Bard d oid(d upon.</p>
