      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <p>M Crostield and National Service. Sin,— Mr. A. Fl. Croslield in hi, address at the Co-operative Hall on October sth, reported in your last week's issue, dealt at some length with the question of Compulsory Military Training. His remarks were received with evident appreciation by most of those who were present. There were a few, however, who did not altogether agree with the views expressed. The chief objec- tion seemed to be to the ring of finality that characterised that part of Mr. Cros- field's address—a 'sort of intimation that there was nothing further to be said—and that the weakness of the position taken up by the National Service League was so lamentable and so obvious that further argument about it was unnecessary. Mr. Crosfield assures us that "he appreciates the zeal " but " questions " the judgment and capacity to discriminate between fact and illusion of those who year after year in this district misspend their energy and fine public spirit on such a question." It is we think fair to enquire— when two persons hold different views on a question, who is to determine which is right? Each may suppose the other to be lacking in " judgment and capacity to discriminate between fact and illusion." Much that Mr. Crosfield said on the subject was obviously an echo of Lord Haldane's speech of last November, and as such carried with it the force of official authority. At the same time it is well to remember that modern English history furnishes examples of a War Office suffering from "illusions " of a very serious nature. At the present time the public mind is confused with regard to this question, mainly because one class of experts asserts that there is no danger—that invasion is almost outside the realm of possibility; whilst another class, equally eminent and capable of judging correctly, assures us that a very grave danger exists, and that the only way to avert it is to adopt compulsory military training. Without discussing the relative merits of these two classes of experts, let us assume that their opinions are of equal value. What then is our duty? Surely it is to adopt that policy that carries with it the only guarantee of security. From the standpoint of justice and fair play, can we conceive of a more reasonable proposal than that every able-bodied citizen shall give n certain portion of his time to military training, and make such prepara- tion as may be necessary to qualify him for the defence of his home and country. The National Service League does not ask one man to do the work and meet the obliga- tions of another—as is the ease under present conditions—it merely asks him to equip himself for the performance of such duties as should naturally and reasonably be associated with his privileges as a citizen. In the opinion of the National Service League such training would be no hardship but rather a help, even if there existed but a very slight probability of those partici- pating being required for active service, and the National Service League Workers in this district, considered by Mr. Crosfield to be " lacking in the capacity to discriminate between fact and illusion," are doing their utmost to convert the people to this view of the question. The responsibility assumed by those who, in these days of unrest, • make speeches of the reassuring typo is, we think, sreat. For when we consider the state of affairs in Europe. referred to by Sir Edward Russell a few days *ago as " producing a sense of extreme disappointment," it makes one see more than ever how important it is that we should endeavour to reach the highest possible standard of strength and efficiency and to regard it as the best guarantee of peace and national security. We should I think do well to consider the weighty words of Mr. Coulton in his " Strong Army in a Free State." "If through presenting an appearance of vulnerability to an invader, we tempt that conflict which a little foresight and self-sacrifice might have rendered impossible, then there must rest a heavy weight of blood-guiltiness on every soul that has not steadily faced this present question.—Yours truly, G. DALE.</p>
        <p>SIR,—Mr. A. H. Crosfield hardly did justice to his own ability in his speech to the Progressive Labour League, as reported in your columns. Was it quite fair to his audience to talk vaguely of a "common humanity" during the very week that Mr. Churchill had been forced by the European unrest to make the significant admission that "Force certainly did not play a less predominant part either in the policy of the nations or the equipoise of them? " Again, was it opportune to boast optismis- tically of the 270,000 men in the Territorial Army two days after the Liberal organ, the Liverpool Daggs Post, published the most pessimistic prophecy of the future prospects of that force which I have ever had the misfortune to read? Mr. Crosfield was, obviously, so busy last week that he missed the following sentence of failure from Sir Edward Russell's excellent paper— " Terri- torials are beginning in ever inert 'ng numbers to realise that so long as they continue to keep the present force on its legs, so long are they helping to defeat the very object they have at heart—the permanent security of the nation." Again, is it not merely farcical after the recent enormous naval expenditure forced upon us by naval rivals, to use the stale phrase of " the unchallenged supremacy of the navy? " Mr. Crosfield has overlooked the unpleasant facts that at the present minute Germany has nine complete Dreadnoughts against fourteen British, of which one is absent from our fleets undergoing repairs; that last month, owing to defects developed by certain of the British destroyers, the German fleet was actually superior in modern craft of this type; that to-day the German navy bold the record for quick coaling, and claims the record for gunnery. Is not this open competition a challenge to us not to shirk a comprehensive scheme of national defence? However, this 'naval question is one for experts, and Admiral Fitzgerald, who is to speak at the N.S.L. meeting in the Parr Hall on October 18th, has received a copy of Mr. Crosfield's speech, and will, no doubt, dispose of his naval fallacies more trenchantly than a civilian can hope to do. Lastly, is it not illogical to quote the methods used in German hospitals as an argument against a citizen army? Mr. Crosfield surely knows that German hospitals have no connection with the German army, and further that the N.S.L. bases its proposed military system, not on that of Germany, but on the National Militia of Switzerland, a country whose people are the pioneers of the peace movement, as the Geneva Convention bears witness, and whose red cross flag is known throughout the world as the banner of humanitarian effort. It would indeed be difficult to discover in the Swiss nation any trace of the " hardening " of moral character which Mr. Crosfield fears would be the result of national service in this Country. If the Liberal candidate had faced the military situation as it exists to-day fairly :and squarely, he would have spoken to the ' 'Warrington Progressive Labour League somewhat as follows:—" We have to face the fact that the Australian Progressive Labour Party, who hold political views very similar to sour own, . have introduced a system of 'compulsory military training into their *fruition., Personally, I consider this .to be a retingrittle step, but I also ,think that we •:anust consiacr seriously whether a similar system-is either necessary or suitable to this 37•.01d.e0Unt17." But no—a little fact like the Citizen artily of 'Australia is laid. aside and - instead, -autilitate4l --arguments -are. Slotted out. . T</p>
        <p>NOV I tl7rthe ED OR, _ _</p>
      </div>
