      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>NOVEMBER ELECTIONS</head>
        <p>THE NOVEMBER ELECTIONS</p>
        <p>There are at present no indications of any c3ntest3, between the tw, , leading political parties at the forth- coming municipal elections, but it is understood that several probaole candidates are being approached. The list of retiring councillors is as follows : TOWN HALL WARD: Council' T. Dirties (I). WHITECRDSS WARD : COllll6llol' IL Roberta HEWSEY WARD : Councillor James Dickenson a.). ORFORD WARD : auncillor Jamee Halliwell (L). ST. Jon's WARD: anncillor F. I'. Lashford (C). FAIRFIrLD WARD : Councilldr J. G. Craik (I). HOWI.EY WARD Councillor T. Chantler (C). Sr. AUSTIN'S WARD : Councillor O. W. JOROpil (e). LATCHFORD WARD : Councillor J. Pemberton (C). Nrminatione must be handed in at the Town Clerk's office before 5 p.m. on Tuesday, October 211.</p>
        <p>WHITECROSS WARD. In Whitecroas Ward, Councillor Henry Roberts. who has twenty-one years' service in the Council to his credit, in opposed by Mr. Samuel Gough and the campaign is being conducted with some vigour. Yesterday afternoon Councillor Roberts delivered to the Town Clerk no fewer than 21 nemination papsrs. Several members had made the suggestion to Mr. Dutton, secretary of the ward committee, that it would be suitable to put in a nomination paper for each year of his service in the Council and the necessary signatures were soon forthcoming. In his address to the electors. Councillor Roberts lays strees upon his previous work in the Council and his connection with large inch:Wind concerns in the immediate neighbourhood. He makes a point of the fact that the Council has, on his suggestioa, agreed to give the boys from the elementary Fchools the oppar- utility of competing for places in the varrens departments of Corporation serviae. Councillor Roberts says he has always contended for the removal of thoae legal and social barriers which have- so often made any substantial FUCCeSS or even progress impossible for the children of working people. Mr. Samuel Ca ugh in his address puts in a strong plea for economy in local administration. He considers the recent purchase of land in I.,.vely4ane was ultra r;res and unbusinees'ike. It was, he says, thrust upon the Council without reasonable efforts being made to obtain it at less cost or to pet other land. Mr. Gough rays the gas offices and staff in Mersey-street could easily be dispensed with and the work of collecting the accounts dore at Bank House. Other measures which he supports are: a decent living wage to workingmen under the Corporation, free gas meters, halfpenny stages on tram fares, abolition of tits wstsr rate on baths in cottage property, compulsory retirement of officials at the agent 0 years,. decrease of ths salary of highly paid officials " who are proved to be laths good to the ratepayers." TOWN HALL WARD.</p>
        <p>Councillor T Birtles, who has served this ward so well, has some representative and influential nomina- tions. In his first paper he is proposed by Mr. James Hepherd and seconded by. Mr. James Gleave. In six other papers he Is proposed and seconded by the following :—William Barton and J. Kenworthy ; James Adams and Charles Unswoith ; H Baker and John Rigby ; Elizabeth Birchall nd Sarah Jane Leigh ; Henrietta Brown and Sarah Wright. Councillor Birtles has been assiduous in attending to his duties during his three years t (office. Out of 438 meetings of Council C mmiltee. in the three years Councillor Dirties has attended 39:'t ; and in the last year 171 cut of 188. OTHER CONTESTS. _ _</p>
        <p>The only other wards in which there is a likelihood of a contest at present are St. John's and Orford, where Labour candidates are opposing the re:iriag representatives. Couccillor Halliwell ii energetically [Aiming his campaign in Orford Ward. Hie solo, in inducing I the Council to raine the wages of able-bodied worker&amp; in the employ of the Corporation to 21. psr week is ' eery popular amongst th- residen.s of thin working- class district. • EMPIRE SKATING HALL.</p>
        <p>The pie 'sures r.f roller skating will again hi era- able to the public from to day ('atorday), when the winter seas-in at the Empire Skating Hall COMM,IIOOII. This is good news to many, for " the exbiliarattng effects of Orating" have been greatly mi-ned since this beautiful and spacious rink was closed. This rink is renowned as eritml t 3 any in England from both the points of view of size and colvenienre. It has the usual gay appearance and when the lanterns flash and the skaters trot round to the merry InneiC supplied br the orchestra the scene will be one of gaiety and colour. Throughout the season there will be spacial attractions. the first being on Thursday evening tint when a confetti battle will take place. As usual the management will write the ammunition sod an enjoyable time is bound to be experienced.</p>
