      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <p>_</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday</p>
        <p>Oa of consideration tor your space I have not referred to Mr. Crosfield's other fallacious statements, which will no doubt be answered by the two prominent Liberals who are advocating national service in the Parr Hall next Wednesday.—Yours, &amp;c., B. S TOWNROE, Wa. liagton</p>
        <p>A Futfrage Challenge. S:r,— It will be remembered by pur readers that Mr. Beaumont, Anti-Suffragist, {VHS challenged in yr or columns to debate on the Women's Suffrage question in Warrington. Ile took up the challenge, but is now going abro id and unable to meet the Suffragists. I do not suppcse it is much use asking Warrington Anti-Suffragists to take his piece—they are peculiarly inarticulate—but if they would, needless to say, the local Suffrage Society would be delighted. If they could get anyone out of the whole of England to take Mr. Beaumont's place they would btill be delighted.—Yours</p>
        <p>cro MIPS Janet Pt ice, 159, Newport road, Cardiff. October Bth, 1911.</p>
        <p>L F. WARING.</p>
        <p>Things that make Coal Dear. Sir,—Nationalisation is in the air The railway- men's congress ask for railway national isation, and the mineis' congress ask for the nationalisation of land and mines. And these demands are widely supported outside the ranks of the men themselves. Of all the examples of private property in land the private ownership of the mineral deposits lying in the bowels of the earth is perhaps the least defensible and the most obviously unjust. Lord Furness stated the other day that the Brocmbill Coliieries in Northumberland had raised nearly 200,000 tons of coal in the past year, but the company had only made a profit of a few hundreds of pounds. We mny safely assume that the mineral rents, rcyalti(s and way leases amounted to £4,000 or 145,000. The receiver or receivers of that money con- tributed rot a yency of the woiking capital and certainly took no hand in the muscular work of cutting the coal at the face or raising it to the surfoce. They therefore had no claim to either interest or wages. What they call their property was not brought into being by the effort cf any human being that ever lived, but, by the stupid law which does not discriminate between a gift of °saute and a proiuct of labour, they have the power to prevent it from being woo ked stall except on their terms. The Royal Commission, which sat over twenty years ago, brought many interesting facts to light which ought to be kept in the lisht. For instance, the Haswell Ctlliery in Durham, paid £9,700 is way- leaves for less than an acre and a hali of land.besidee many thousands of punds annually in royalties. One col iery had to jay £l,OOO a year for waylesves over land that was worth no more than £lO a yell.. Another had I, at over £lO,OOO in twelve years, and had paid exactly that sum in wayleaves alone,besides royalties. The 133trcw Hematite Steel Company in three years paid £374,000 to three noble land ores Devonshire. Puccleuch and Ilunraster, and during that time did not pay a farthing in dividend. Sir Arthur Markham, Ml'. told the 1.1. use of Commons, when the Budget tax en minerals was labia discussed (September 22nd 1909) that the Wntan Coal and Iron Company spent £500,000 en one m dliery and in fourteen yearn' working did not pay a ' dividend. Bat it had regularly to pay a minimum rent of £lO,OOO a year to the Duke if Newcastle. Instances like this are typical of what is going on all the time, and they justify completely the dem tnd that the mineral deposits, which are so vital to our very existence as a nation, should be made national property. • A very good stait might be made by a declaration by Pat Bement that all future di-coveries of mmerals shall be reserved to the State. Property of an enormous but incalculable value might thus be nationalised quite justly a ithont a flatting of com- pensation being paid.—Yours faithfully, JOSEPH HYDER.</p>
        <p>Land Nations Motion Society, 432, West Strand, London, W.C. October sth, 1911.</p>
        <p>A Warringtonian in New Zealand. How State Insurance Works. Sir.---The "Examiner" readers may be interested in M aring a little about two of the most advanced measures ever placed by any Parliament of the world on the statute book. I refer to the National Provident act and the Advances to Worker's Act of New Zealand, both of which became law lett year. These measures are reckoned so sound from an actuarial standpoint that not even a hide-bound Tory of the old school can attack them with impunity. The first of these acts makes provision for the thrifty workmen against incapacity to work, for a retiring allowance after attaining the age of GO, and for an allowance up to £6 on the birth of each child. Its benefits are generous and amp,e. The fund is subsidised by the state to the extent of 25 per cent. The payments made by contributors are small in comparMon with its benefits. This Act is claimed to be the first serious attempt I made by any country in the world to organise labour so that during illness or distress a worker and his wife shall not suffer want and the closing years of their life not be Jived in penurious gloom. It is entirely free from the taint of charity. The some a man withdraws he has paid for. A contribution of 3s. a week by a lad of 16 will ensure for him these benefits : —Two potipds a week for the rest of his life after he has reaciled the age of sixty; 7s. 6d. per week for each of his children under 14 years of age if he has been incapacitated from work for three months; £6 medical expenses at the birth of each child; eliculd he die his wife will be entitled to 7s. 6.1. per week for each child under LI and Sc. 6d. for herself. This means that if a man with four young children died, his wife would be entitled to £1 17s 61 per week until the children reached the age of 14. These benefits alone are exceptionally great. If, however, for any reason a man wished to with- draw from the fund he can do so at any time without loss. The State will simply have acted as his banker, the caretaker of his money. The passing of this Act has partially solved a difficult problem that for a long time has cried for solution, and is the embodiment of a high principle of justice. With regard to the Wcrkers' Dwellings Act it may be noted that during the past three years the Nerr Zealand Governmer.t has advanced for the creation of workers' homes the sum of £1,074,000. During last session a epecial Act was passed that provided that on clement of a deposit of ten poands I any landless worker will be advarced sufficient money to buy him a house. No other countiy makes such generous provision for the worker. It enables him to buy his own home outright on payment of less money than he generally pars in tent. how will the Act operate? Take a dwelling to cost £500; for that the worker would pay £lO down and £25 a year for twenty-six years and a half. He will then own bin own home. The Act will mean the d ath knell of high rents when the workingmen get to thoroughly understand that for less money than they are paying ordinarily each week to their landlord they can buy a house of their one. Just as tie advaoces to Settlers' Act pulled down the rate of interest and prevented the farmers froml being charged xorbitant inn rest by the capitalist, I so will the Workmen's Dwellings Act pull down rent ar.d prevent workers being at the mercy of unmerciful landlords. The effect of the Act may mean this. In fifty years every man in this land will own his own home. The Government has been empowered to borrow £500,000 every year for the purpose of Workers' Homes. This will make provision for a thousand workers to obtain a borne every year. All this will be done without the State being called up nto pay a penny. The State simply guarantees the money to the lender.—With kind regards to friends in Warrington, I reread°, yours sincerely,</p>
        <p>U. FORD.</p>
        <p>Ilikurangi, N. Auck'ancl, New Zealand. _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>HARVEST -FESTIVAL.</head>
        <p>HARVEST -FESTIVAL.</p>
        <p>sE_PAUL'S CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The harvect tiMOk-sgir;i4"-seivri;reti-Wre-re concluded on Sunday. There were large congregations, especially in the evening, when every seat was occupied. The preacher in the morning was the Rev. G. C. Briggs. vicar of Christ Church, Latchfe rd. At 215 a united children's service was held, followed at 3.15 by a united Bible Class service. At the latter service an address wee given by the Rev. T. Vickers, curate-in-charge of St. Clement's. The Vicar the Rev. W. Bracecerop) preached in the evening. Special music was rendered the Magnifirat and Sane Dimittis being beautifully sung to Bradley's settings. Mr. E. Wilkes presided at the celiac The offertories at all the services were in aid of the Additional Clergy Fund.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>" CALICO " BALL.</head>
        <p>" CALICO " BALL.</p>
        <p>PICTURESQUE SCENE AT THE</p>
        <p>PARR HALL,</p>
        <p>Or e of the prettiest fancy dress balls ever held in I Warrington took place at the Parr Hall on Wednesday evening, under the auspices of the Warriegton Women's Liberal Association. A distinctly novel touch can given to the aff,,ir by its designation as a " calico ball," the object of the promoters being, to popularise the event by offering prizes for the most striking and miginal costumes made nf that heady material. It was stipulated that each dress should not cost more than 'abut some of the dancers had 01-viotraly exceed, dth.rt amount. Their lictureuqas productions had therefore to be passed by when the judging took place. There was. however, a consensus hf opinion that the character " make-ups' which tenured the awards were well entitled to succeed on their merits, considerable ingenuity having been displayed to get the best results from a small outlay. The scene when the ball was in full awing was one of kaleidoscopic bemty, the many cams intermingling with charming effect. All the personalities if romance and every- day life which me invariably associates with fancy dress balls were to be seen in tl is pretty pageant. There we, e Romany lasses, flower girls, milk-maids, daughte, s of Erin, the Queen of Hearts, Dutch girls, and a Grecian lady. One competitor essayed the ' difficult task of repreevnting Free Trade and Protection, ' whiht an titer hi.bbled along as an old age pensioner.</p>
        <p>Photo Toll9o.</p>
        <p>The representation of " Superstition," which gained first prize in the original section, was particuiary good, the lady's dress being embellished sith such important is fineness on " luck " as number 13, a black eat, a spider, and a broken mirror. One lady wore a harem *Via and a striking contrast to this latest rt ntinental adornment w: a the homely attire of Sarah Gamp, wi h the inevitable big bonnet and formidable umbrella. Other effective characterise tions were the Egyptian girl and Slismrock,the latter hiving as headgear a im del yacht. " Perfection " was the role of another lady whose dress was c, nipletbly covered with soap wrappers. ¶1 lie work of judging Was undertaken by Mr and Mrs A 13 Crasfield, Mr and Mrs C E Parker, Mrs James D. dds, Mr T PeMbeitnn. and Councillor J T Leeker, the competitors merehilig in procession round the body of the ball. Handing was then resumed to the strains of Mr 'Blake's band and continue I until 2 a.m. The duties of MO's were discharged by Mr. J. Critchley, Mr. E. Osbetne, Mr. P. Delaney and Mr r Locker. The stewards were Messrs. H Baker, J Walsh, L McMullen, J Smith, J H Booth, G Molyneaux, J Mather, E Hughes, J L Jones, T Flanaglian, I Smith jun., J Emmett,'! Monks, W Unsworth, J Hassell, A MolyneaultE Miller, F. A Sanders, H o:.borne, C Locker, T W Wallington. Supper was served in the crush room, the tables being presided over by members of the committee and helpers as follows : —Mrs S Hill, Mrs Lo..ker, Mrs Palmer, Mrs Willett, Mrs Voile, Mrs H Osborne, Mrs Hewitt, Mrs Milner, Mrs Young, Mrs. Miller, Mrs Gregory, Miss Nurse., Mrs Swarbrick, and Miss Willett. The cloak rooms were in charge of Mr. and Mrs Ashworth.</p>
        <p>Daring an interval Councillor LOCKER announced the names of the prize winners. He added that no money had been taken from the funds of the associa- tion to provide the prizes, the donors being Mr and Mrs A H Crosfield, Mrs John Crosfield, Miss Croolield, Mr and Mrs Parker, Mrs Dodds. and several gentlemen of the Liberal Association. . . .</p>
        <p>Mrs. A. H. Crosfield then pre-ented the irizes - a very handsome collection -as follow:.</p>
        <p>Moat original costumes: 1, Miss Womeraley (Superstition); 2, Mrs Foster (Shamrcelc); 3, Miss</p>
        <p>, KT. • . , , • . .</p>
        <p>MISS WOMERSLEY as " SurEasTama."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>INFIRMARY CONCERT.</head>
        <p>THE INFIRMARY CONCERT.</p>
        <p>To the Workingmen's Committee of the Warrington Infirmary invariably belongs the distinctimi of opening the local concert season with its annual effort on behalf of the funds of our leading charitable institution. The audience at the Parr Hall on Saturday evening was one of the largest which this always popular event has attracted of recee.t years, and a particularly pleasing feature was the presence of many prominent townspeople who take an active interest in the affairs of the Infirmary. While the programme presented little of exceptional intrinsic merit from the musical point of view, it was sufficiently well varied to sustain the interest of all parts of the " house " for two hours and a half. Appreciation, too, was evident from the generous bestowal of re-calls. As usual Mr. T. J. Down's orchestra placed its services at the disposal of the committee, and all honour is due to this enthusiastic body of instrumentalists for its never- failing readiness to as,ist in the cause of charity. Under the conduc'orship of Mr. Arthur Down half-a-dozen choice selections—chiefly of a light character—were submitted with a gratifying measure of success. On the whole, the playing was characterised by refreshing unity of purpose and crispness of attack, but there was room for closer attention to detail, a• d a more vivid realization of contrasting moods in the effort to do I justice to the intentions of the composers. flat beautiful gem, Schumann's " Slumber song, "was not made to goip the listener as it should have done, if 1 greater feeling and expreasios had been infused into its performat.ce. The collection of Sullivan's melodies proved a most acceptable feature, these delight-I fully tuneful and esr-haunting airs receiving ' tasteful and effective treatment, although the connec• tive passages might well have been improved upon. I The epen'ng march "Sangre Torero" (E're;ti) and the overture " Le Philtre " (-labor) were given in spirited style, and the cornet eolo in the familiar "Post Hors" gallop was skilfully played by Mr. W. Rutter. The committee had been happy in their choice of soloists. Madame Ratbbone made artistic one of her powerful and flexible soprano voice in " The Moorish Maid " (Parker) and " Chinese love song " (Clayton). Madame E. A. Sanders, a great favourite at local concerts, delighted the audience with expressive yet unaffected renderings of " In the Chimney Corner" (Cowen) and " The songs my mother sang " (Grime/raw), the latter, with its introduction of the homely songs familiar in childhor Xs happy days, meeting with a special degree of commendation. Mr. Horace Rinks, who p-sseses a pure-toned tenor voice, sang " Farewell in the desert (Adams and" Come into the garden Maud " (PAW in soulful, earnest style, but he should guard egiisst a tendeacy to dramatic exaggeration. Mr. William Coleman (base) had not much scope for the display of his powers in the simple ditties • Stone-cracker ,John (cohtes) and "Corporal's ditty,' CS•plitel, but the'itiat and piquancy with which he sang tkein left nothing to betiesired. +</p>
        <p>Cod' (Perfection) •' 4 (equal).Mrs Lindley (Mrs Clamp) and Mrs Wiltiama(Old Age Pensionet). Prettiest Costumes : 1, Miss Short (Egyptian Girl); 2, Miss Fitton (Milk mid); 3 (equal), Miss Weave IDatch girl) lull MN ifalliwell get room girl); MiSi E Evans (tul flower) and Miss Cleave (trellis work). . .</p>
        <p>Mr. A. H. CROSFIELD, who was greeted with cheers, complimented all concerned upon the pretty and inspiring sight which the ball presented. That was the s.cond exceedingly iuteresting and attractive gathering they hail had in that building that day. They had been glad to welcome so many distingaished representatives of leading thought amougat the Liberal women of England who had *wended the conference of the Lsncashire and Cheshire Astociations, and it was a happy idea that that ball hod been arranged to follow the business meetings, as it showed that in these days Liberals did not forget the social pleasures of life and, further, that they were in very good fig and in good heart too. (Applause.) " We are quite as touch entitled " continued Mr. Crosfield "t) our enjoyment as anybody on the other aide. Moreover, I think we are entitled to a little amusement at their expense. (Laughter and applause.) I don't often go to music halls, but I have seen nothing more comic on the boirds than the comedy that is bring played by the Tory party before the whole country at the present time. (Hear, bear. We see our opponents in pursuit of a policy, and a sort of 'chivy chase' is going on after their own leader. (Laughter). It reminds one of what you sometimes see -a child trying to grab a fly on the window, and it always manages to escape. (Laughter). We are } quite eatit'ed to enj y ours elves at their expense</p>
        <p>Photo Tonge,</p>
        <p>when we see this performance going on." Turning to the serious side of politics, Mr. Crosfield said they must congratulate themselves that they had such splendid leaders, such a fine policy, and such an inspir- ing programme to work for. (Applause). He wished to thank the women cf Warrington for the active and enterprising psrt they had taken and the really valuable practical work they were doing in the pro- gressive cause He must frankly confess that he was not surprised that women in these days should take every year that passed, a more serious interest in the affairs of the country which more and more directly con- cerned them, as the great work of social retorm riroceeded. If they only looked at the plague spots of modern civilization—whether at home or abroad— it Was enough to make anybody a pessimist. On the other hand, they had a right to be optimistic because they had practical evidence of the fine work that had been done in the last half dozen years, and which had tended in the direction of • better distribution of wealth, and to better opportunities all-round, safe- guarding also the general welfare of the whole of our people. The very fact that people were now alive to evils which wera formerly ignored was another earnest and guarantee of the good progress we had made. (Applause.) Of course, everyone knew that there were formidable for ces on the side of reaction in Warrington, but he was sure that many who were connected with the old Liberal cause would continue to put their backs jab thy work, b lieving that the women of Warrington would help to carry thcir cause to victory. (Loud applause). .. • . .</p>
        <p>For non-dancers, ti;vhist 'drive was arranged, the M C.'s being : Councillors J. Wilkinson and J. 'f. Locker, Messrs. Joseph Wilkinson, F. Lindley and W. Bibby. The prize-winners were: Ladies, 1, Mrs I John Cowden ; gentlemen, 1, Mr. George Walker ; 2, Mr. MeLanglan. The hall had been tastefully decorated in the Liberal , colour by the following ladies : Mrs. Bill, Mrs. E. V. Walker, Miss Walker, Miss Dodds, Miss Short. For the excellence of the arrangements and the smoothne•to with vrbich the function passed off, grest credit is due to Mrs. E. C. o.borne, hon. secretary of the associaticn.</p>
        <p>Messrs. !links and Coleman further distinguished themselves in the duet '' Watchman ! what of the night ? " (Surges it) which well deserved the hearty arplause it called forth. We were indebted to the local Orpheus Quartet (Messrs. 11. W. Cook, E. Walker J. Critchley and L. Ankland) for some of the most enjoyable items of the evening, their voices blending admirably in "Nellie Gray " and " Robin Adair " which were given with admirable taste. The subdued vocal accompaniments were most excellently done and greatly heightened the effect. Mr. Howard limes, who was responsible for the humorous element, suns eminently successful in his efforts to make the audience laugh long and heartily. His songs and witticisms were really amus- ing and be did not resort to the buffoonery which too often serves for humour at popular concerts. Mr. W. H. Payton, Mus. Bac., accompanied in bia usual tasteful and unobtrusive style. Mr. W. Peter Rylands gave a short address on the excellent object which had brought the audience together, and etnphaaised the needs of the Infirmary. Amongst other prominent town,men present at the concert were Sir Gilbert Greenall Bart., C.V.O. (president of the Infirmary), Mr. A. 11. Crosfi-Id and Alderman Burton (chairman ef the Workingmen's Commi tee).</p>
        <p>. * ' atoll .., °;,` .., (V . ..- . i ' 441111,.., , ... -.' ~* V ~,) 14 • ..,„„, '*- , F- ' . ''- '1,4 it'* • -,.. - 4,,i 4!", .1, , . -..: , ~.,.... - , -....tira, ~..., ~. --,„.---Iviii . .</p>
        <p>mas. LINDLEY as " SARAH GAmr."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>SEQUEL TO MOTOR ACCIDENT.</head>
        <p>SEQUEL TO MOTOR ACCIDENT.</p>
        <p>At the County Court on Thursday before hia Honour Judgs 11. Y. Stanger, K.C., Aliston Morland Johnson, electrical Engineer, Manchester, claimed d.:42 133. 3d. for personal injuries and damage to a motor cycle, alleged t) have been sustained by the negligent driving of a motor car by J. Bayley Isaac, provision dealer, Warrington. Mr. Soifer, of Manchester, (instructed by Mr. W. 11. Todd, Manchester), appeared for the plaintiff, and Mr. Cuthbert Smith, of Liverpool (instructed by Mess. s. Wood and Lord, Manchester) for the defendant. It was stated by Mr. Slifer that on the Sunday after Whitsuntide the plaintiff wan proceedirg en a motor cycle from Kelsall to Manchester, and the motor car was g ing from Tarporley to Wadi; gton. It was nearly 11 o'clock at night, and whim the plaintiff got within GO yards of a road crossing he sounded his horn and waited for a respmise. He did not gd con and continued to slow down. The plaintiff would swear that there was no answer to his warning signal and that it was on'y when he arrived at the middle of the creasing that a motor car dashed out of the Tarporley War, ington-road and that he was ent•apped. 'He swerved to the left but the car knecked him down. The defendant also tried to avoid the collision but he was going at too great a speed to enable him to pull up. The tatdor car made an effort to turn to the right and in d, ing so collided with the porch of an old toll , house, broke one of the tillers and also demolished pad of the dructute itself. The case was adjourned until the next sitting of the Court. it Thursday week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>MR. A. H. CROSFIELD AND NONCONFORMITY. Striking Speech at Latchford Sale of Work.</head>
        <p>MR. A. H. CROSFIELD AND NONCONFORMITY. Striking Speech at Latchford Sale of Work.</p>
        <p>REBELLION AN3 BIGOTRY CONDEMNED.</p>
        <p>This is the jubilee year of Latchfordr Bap- tist Church, and the annual sale of work on Thursday and yesterday took the form of a special effort to raise funds for the restora- tion of the fabric of the church and school, the decoration of the main schoolroom, and the improvement of the classrooms. When the safe opened on Thursday afternoon the schoolroom presented a very bright and attractive appearance, the stalls having been very artistically draped in vane-coloured materials. There was a great array of useful and fancy wares. Mrs. A. H. Crosfield, who was accompanied by Mr. A. 11. Crosfield, opened the sale in the presence of a large gathering. Mr. James Kenworthy presided, and was accompanied on the platform by the Revs. J. Stewart Chedburn (pastor), H. Ogden and John Yonge, Miss Crosfield, Mrs. Dodds and Mrs Kenworthv . .</p>
        <p>The chairman said he had known that place all his life, and, although he had not been associated with the church there, lie had been connected with churches of the same order. It was a remarkable thing that nearly all charitible and religious objects had at times to take extraordinary steps for the purpose of raising money. That sale u-as such a step, and he hoped the result would be such as to set at rest any doubts and anxieties of Mr. Chedburn in regard to_financial_matters. They -</p>
        <p>were proud to-have Mr. and Mrs. Cros- field with them. (Applause.) Although they had not to talk politics there, he could not help expressing the hope that before long Mr. Crosfield would again occupy the position he occupied a short time ago. (Laughter and applause.) Mr. A. H. Crosfield, responding to the re- quest of the chairman to address the .gather- ing, congratulated everybody who had been responsible for the arrangements on the very beautiful and artistic effect they had succeeded in producing. • lie hoped their efforts would receive a good reward, to the great benefit of that religious institution. (Hear, hear.) He came of an old Noncon- formist stock that dwelt for many generations in the beautiful hill-and-dale country of the north-west, and one naturally felt a great deal of pardonable pride in that old God- fearing Nonconformist ancestry. (Applause.) He had always felt that the English nation and the whole English-speaking race through- out the world owed an undying debt to the Free Churches of this country, which bad stood for liberty of conscience and liberty of the individual. He must be careful what he said that afternoon, because that was not a political occasion—(laughter)—but he did not think he would be exceeding what was per- missible and legitimate if lie said that in the present day it seemed to him that the great Free Churches of England would have no less serious a duty than heretofore in maintaining the old gospel of religious toleration. (Hear, hear.) He must draw their attention to what, he had no doubt, had struck many of his hearers—to the extraordinary doctrines that had been preached within the four corners of the kingdom, doctrines which were sub- versive of all law and order—the preaching of rebellion to a minority. He did not think the Free Churches of England could fail, to a man, to withstand such pernicious and evil doctrines. Another thing that gave him good hope for their future influence in the world was the fact that, besides the spiritual re- generation of the people, the churches were every day more and more alive to their material needs. Near where he lived in London there were striking illustrations of the effect on mind, character, and life of the environment of our people. Every child that was born at Hampstead had a chance of living fourteen years longer than the child that was born in Southwark. They had not to look very far for the explanation when they con- sidered that at the present moment there were 70,000 people in South London living in one-roomed tenements. It was only too easy to imagine the moral and spiritual conditions under such circumstances. "Think of it," continued Mr. Ceasfield, "in cold weather or in the drought of a hot summer like we have Just had. And don't let us forget that so long as these evil conditions obtained not only in London, but in other big cities, so long will there be a great work for every good man and woman and for the religious bodies of our country to do. (Applause.) In con- clusion, let me again express the hope that the Free Churches will continue to stand, as they have always done, for that fine and great gospel of religious toleration. Don't let us tolerate this attempt to fan the old fires of religious animosity. (Hear, hear.) I was astounded to find that even in our own dis- trict the effort to do so had, only a few days, received something like approval. We must get our faces against these reactionary in- fluences, and, in thanking you for giving me the opportunity of saving these few words, I sincerely wish you 'God-speed' and all pros- perity as the result of this undertaking." (Applause.) Mrs. Crosfield recalled the fact that her promise to open the sale was given to Mrs. Wilkinson on a very memorable day—election day (laughter)—at a time when they were all wondering what the result would be. (Daughter.) Continuing, Mrs. Crosfield ten- dered her hearty congratulations to the mem- bers of the church on the attainment of their jubilee. She was glad to see they were cele- brating it not only in a pleasing but in a practical manner. "I am sure," added Mrs. Crosfield, "that the ladies have had a hand in that, because it is always the ladies who know how to do things in a practical way. (Laughter, and a Voice: " Sometimes.") I don't want impolitely to contradict that statement hut I think, as a rule, it is always the ladies who are practical in this world." (Laughter.) Having expressed the hope that the mile would realize the highest freectitions of the promoters, Mrs. Closfield said that, with such a charmingly arranged and beautifully decorated display, all that now remained was for the ladies to um a great deal of persuasion and to have a cheery r ptinnam,bec nine if they were optimistic and fe,t certain they were going to succeed they generally managed to do so (Applsose). The Rev. J. S. CHEIMI:11!:, in proposing a vote of thanks to Mr. and Mrs. Crosfield and the Chairman, add that one could not live in Warrington very long without hearing the praises loudly rung of the kind lady wh- se presence and active sympathy with them must lend a powerful impetus to their enter- prise-a lady who had corns of recent years to this ancient borough to adorn its social and public life and to mtke for herself a revered sanctuary in the hearts of all its peop'e, high and low, rich and poor —of whatever religious or politic 4 persuasion they might be. (Applause.) Mr. Crrsfield was worthily sustaining the high traditions of his family. He was not only byed by al his Mende, but honoured and revrected by his opponents. (Applause\ Mrs. PEAR3•ON seconded and the vote was heartily pas.ed. kliss Carmine was calle I upon by the Chairman to say a few words and expressed her best wishes for the success of the ss'e. Mrs. Cao,yiel.a, in acknowledging the vote of thanks, said she could not find words to express her deep appreciation of the kindness which had been extended to her ever since the first day she came to Warrington with her husband. (Applause.) The sahs was re. opened yesterday by Mr. Thomas Ford It was announced that tie premed' of it. previ-us day amounted to Ell Ils. fid. The tottl proceeds amounted to MU 4s. In</p>
        <p>Church Society. The Warrington Church Society opened it 4 winter session last evening when a largo congnenation assembled et the anniversary service in the Perish Church. The Rev. Caron Bilborough, Rector of Liverpool was the preacher. An interesting series of lectures has been arranged. The Rev. Prebendary Waketord. of Liverpool, is to spent on " Ilarti4ge and ' Divorce," the Nen. Archdeaeon Lloyd, of Rbyl, will deal with" Welsh Disestablishment,* end the Rey. L. J. T. Darwall, ;pf ~yostspill 'lnetnin on Liturgical Masse." •.</p>
        <p>Furnlshing Problem -Solved.-</p>
      </div>
